        <p>MADE-UP DISHES</p>
        <p>hich are a most important in the week's menu of Ty household, are made Lost piquant and tasty by the addition of</p>
        <p>OVRIL,</p>
        <p>hich is the guaranteed</p>
        <p>product , of ' IME ox</p>
        <p>BEEF</p>
        <p>and which</p>
        <p>imparts the full flavour of Fresh Meat to even the most tasteles dish.</p>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS. You are only as poor as you feel. But even this is bad enough. IN the lexicon of fortune we go up hill on our feet and down on a bicycle. THE new woman seems to like to hear the old, old story. A LETTER-CARLIER.—The absent-minded. hus- band. FOND PARENT: "I wish, Bobby, that I could be a little boy again." Bobby: "I wish you could— littler than me," THE medical profession divide humanity into two classes—the poor whom they cure, and the rich 'whom they doctor. HE: "I can stay but half an hour." She: "Too bad, George. You won't have time to bid me good-night." HE loved her dearly. He was too bashful to tell her, so he wrote her an anonymous letter and told his love. PASSENGER: "Don't the passengers make you tired with the questions they ask?" Captain: " Yes; very much. What else is it you want to know?" Miss SWEETY : "Yes, I can't get married be- cause poor George is suffering from heart failure." Her Friend: "How terrible !" "Yes. He tried twice to speak to papa, but his heart failed him." OLD MEMORIES.—LittIe Girl: " Gran'pa says he remembers veen the snow was so deep it was up to his waist." Little Boy: " When ? " "Oh, ever so long ago." " Mebby it was w'en he was a little baby.' OBEYING THE DOCTOR.—Family Physician : "See here! I ordered an immediate and complete cessation of all mental labour, and here I find you writing." Literary Man : " It's nothing—nothing at all; only a society novel." AN OCCASION FOR REVISION. Little Ned: "Don't take away the light." Mamma: "I want you to learn to sleep without a light." Little Ned: Must I sleep in the dark ? ' Mamma: "Yes." Little Ned : " Well, then, wait a minute. I guess I'll get up and say my prayers a little more carefully." DEPENDABLE.—" Our new clerk is the most, reliable one we have ever had." How's that?" "You can always tell what he is going to do next." "And what does he do?" " Nothing."</p>
        <p>HER NEW HAT. —Poet : "I hear you were a great success as a graduate. Your fr:end said 43omething about a poem. I would like you to show it to me." Gladys: "Why, I have it on !" IT'S So.—Bachelor: "I am told that a married man can live on half the income that a single man requires." Married Man: "Yes. He has to. "WAS it cool where you spent your vacation?" " Cool ! I should say it was. I went away for a couple of days, and returned unexpectedly. I found the old farmer wearing one of my shirts and my straw hat; his two sons away at a picnic in my best clothes, and his wife straining jelly 'through my while flannel coat, and all they said was, "We hadn't been expectin' ye hum so soon.' It was the coolest family I ever knew." CURBING HIS IMAGINATION.—WaIIgrove : " Snap- shot's last book is rather a poor thing; and he used to write such brilliant society novels a few years ago !" Plugwinch ; " Ah, but he has gone into society himself since then ! " HER RIGHT. —Mrs. Brisk (severely) : "Maud, when I looked into the parlour last night your head was resting on Mr. Huggin's bosom !" Miss Maud Brisk: "Yes, mamma; but that is my Nested_ right now dear Walter had just pro-</p>
        <p>posed." A REASONABLE THEORY. Physician: "Yes, sir; my opinion is that one-half the diseases that afflict humanity are due to over-eating." Friend r(reflectively) "It may be—may be. Now I think of it, it is months since any one was sick at my boarding-house. A HARD JOB.—Friend: "Wasn't it hard for you to give your daughter up?" Father: " les, indeed! I was just about ready to give up trying." Why do you and Bobby quarrel so much? I • hope my Willie is not a selfish little boy." "No, mamma, I'm not selfish, but Bobby is. He always wants to play the games I don' t want to." NAUTICAL.—There had been a wedding in high life the night before, and Brown and Robinson were discussing its glories at the club. "Great affair that !" said Robinson. " Magnificent ! " agreed Brown. "How the wine flowed !" "And how theboysdidbathe in it! No one took too much. though--except Jones. "YS. That's the worst of Jones. He never knows when he's had enough. I'm sure he was half seas over last night." "half seas over? Oh, no! When I saw him he was sail- ing_into port." FATHER ROUGH.—Ragged Robert (at Stony Point) : "It's poor Christians these folks is." "Jagged Jake: "When ye ask for bread they give ye a stone." Rayed Robert: "I wouldn't mind if they'd just give'llf stone to me, but they throw it." A WISE CHOICE.—" I am very glad to learn that the young lady you are engaged to is deeply religious. You have made a wise choice." Son: Yes, we go to all the prayer meetings, church sociables, and revivals that come along. It's a heap cheaper than theatres, balls, and operas." A THEORY.—Wing : "Have you found out who stole your wife's jewellery ?" King: "I think that -detective did. If he didn't he couldn't possibly know so much about how it was done." Mas. HusinioßE : "You'll have to settle' up or leave." Summer Boarder: "Thanks, awfully. 'The last place I was at they made me do both." AN ACTIVE WOMAN.—Husband (breathlessly) "I must rush off on very short notice, for an ex- tended trip, and I can take you along if you can get ready. Do you think you can do it in two hours and a quarter?" Wife: "Easily. I can pack the trunk in five minutes, and that will leave me two hours and ten minutes to dress." SHE TRIED THE7,l.—Mrs. Blinkers: "What ! Going away? Why?" Servant: "Please, mum, when I come yesterday, you gave me the keys to your trunks, and drawers, and chests, and jewel- boxes, to keep for you." Mrs. Blinkers: "Yes, I did that to show that I trusted you. What is the matter?" Servant: " Thur don't one of 'em fit." THE DANCING LESSON.—" One, two, three." Show me how you do' it. " Left foot—right foot." I can ne'er go through it. "Arm around your waist ? " I can do that better. Now, my little lady, I have got it to the letter, With a "one, two, three." I'm very much your debtor. "One, two, three." It doesn't seem so easy. Seems to me th,2 'loot- is getting -t‘easy. " Wax, not grease ?" Jove, I'm in a jumble I Now, my litth. radv, I am feeling very humble. Slip ! Cra,sh ! -Rio' sinzt.ii a turnWe !</p>
        <p>Critchley's " STAIiCIS GLOSS I The 'Royal Lannd -esFes irry a heautifid. finish to starched is uneo,..4lled for imparting men &amp;c., either cold cr hoiling water starch. Mr.—. Corn- wall, says : -•• :ids, wit- will riot tin-bh our linen without your " Starch Gloss.' " From Mrs.---, Bradford :—Dear am pleased to inform von that I have tried many sr-called Starch Glazed ~nd Volisbes, but find none equal to yours." It should be used in every house. Sold at id., 3d., and 6d., by most starch B.llers. If uot in stock wiil prccuro it. T. CEITCHLEY, Blackburn, I% oprietor.—Advt.</p>
        <p>eAPITAL" CliAl.</p>
        <p>Mr. Richard M‘Ghee, who has been selected by a County convention as Nationalist candidate for South Louth, will reinforce the band of Labour members in the House. He is a friend of Mr. Davitt's, and has been associated with the dock and riverside labourers' interests at Liverpool and Glasgow. He is the president of the National Union of Dock Labourers, which has its head- quarters at Liverpool. He is a native of Lurgan, where he was born something less than fifty years ago.</p>
        <p>Last week the postal authorities throughout the country made a raid on halfpenny letters. Every- body knows by this time that circulars and kindred communications may be sent through the post un- sealed, with a halfpenny stamp. Latterly this pri- vilege has been grossly abused. Numberless com- munications that should have been conveyed at the ordinary rate of letter postage have been ' risked " with halfpenny stamps. It is almost inconceivable, yet none the less a fact, that banknotes have been posted in this way for the purpose of saving a half- penny. To check this fraud on the revenue the ' postal authorities ordered the opening of all com- munications bearing only the halfpenny stamp that passed through the Post-office last week, and the usual penalties were exacted when the regulations were found to have been broken. I understand that the financial "haul" resulting from the week's operations was expected to reach as much as £lO,OOO, and it will be interesting to know what sum has actually been obtained,</p>
        <p>Dr. Jameson and his officers are observing their parole with strict honour. The doctor is at Deep- dene, near Dorking, on a visit to his old comrade, Lord William Beresford. They were in Zulu War and Dr. Jun was reputed the best doctor of the force. He comes to town this week.</p>
        <p>--- In consequence of Mr. Lahouchere's question in the House of Commons on Monday evening there was quite a rush of members and strangers to look at the statue in the Central Hall "purporting to be that of the late Mr. Bright." The general opinion was unfavourable to the work. "Upon my soul," said one hon. gentleman, who was well acquainted with the deceased statesman, "it's more like a respectable grocer in his Sunday clothes than John Bright."</p>
        <p>Mr. Armitstead has returned to London from en- tertaining Mr. and Mrs. Gladstone at Biarritz, and afterwards joining the ex-Premier as a guest on the visit of the party to Lord Bendel at Cannes. Mr. Armitstead will meet dr. and Mrs. Gladstone on Tuesday next at Folkestone on their return from their winter visit abroad, and conduct them to London. Mr. Gladstone has never had a day's sickness while abroad. His health has been mar- vellous, and the amount of work he has been able to get through is stupendous. He is never out of his library except when absolutely necessary for health's sake, and he reads and writes for hours without feeling fatigue. Is is astonishing to much younger men how he gets through his work with- out appearing tired, and his conversation at dinner every night is of the most brilliant character. He has been inundated with telegrams and letters on every conceivable subject that touches not only Great Britain, but. also European countries, the United States, and the Far and Near East, asking in nearly every case that he should give his opinion. To ail these he has been able only to say that his position is one of retirement.</p>
        <p>" Ah ! "exclaimed Bernhardt recently," in some previous state of life my dog has been a great stage- manager, Don't laugh. I am in earnest. That's why I bought him. The instant I set eyes on this dog I realised that he had the dramatic instinnt to a remarkable degree. To-morrow night,while I am playing, he will lie in the wings, watching me fur- tively. If he wags his tail when I come off, I shall know that I have given a really great performance. If he growls—ah well, if he growls,then I shall be in despair."</p>
        <p>General Booth is expected in London on Monday orluesday, and the news precedes him that he is thoroughly pleased with his tour. Commissioner Booth-Tucker, who is spoken of as the likely new head of the Salvation Army in America,accompanies the General Besides settling matters in reference to the organisation in America, General Both will on his arrival have to complete the other changes which are in progress in the most important Salva- tionist commands.</p>
        <p>--- The desire of the Japanese to obtain a share of the carrying trade of the world is bringing impor- tant orders to British shipbuilders. For the new Anglo-Japanese line of the Japan Mail Steamship Company, or Nippon Yusen Kaisha, six additional twin-screw steamers will be required. One of these is being built in Japan by the lklitsu Bishi Company, and orders for the other five hare just been placed with shipbuilding firms in this country. Each of the new vessels will be 450 ft. in length, and of 5 550 tons, the ctirrying capacity being 7,500 tons dead weight, and the ave,rago speed twelve knots. The "British Trade Journal" for March publishes these and other particulars, in an account of an in- terview with Mr. Shoda, the representative of the Nippon Yusen Kaishn, now in this country, who is concluding arrangements for the new line.</p>
        <p>The ParneHires propose to advance an amend- ment expressing their protest against the naval expenditure. Their contention is that if England wants a big Navy she is welcome to provide one, but Ireland has no interest in the subject. and she objects to contribute what. it is contended, is a largely excessive share of the cost.</p>
        <p>A movement among a section of the more advanced Rndical members. including English, Scotch and Welsh. in favour of pressing on their leaders Home Rule all round as an active part of the Liberal policy ha., been afoot for some time. It may be remembered that a discussion on this point, raised a week or two no, was postponed. It is now settled that the sojourned consideration of the subject is to come off next Tuesday week. There is an anticipation that the members advocating the adoption of this as a substantial plank in the official Liberal platform will prove to be practically unani- mous in the representations they make. It is by no means to be assumed that the lenders will dance enthusiastically to this pulling of the strings.</p>
        <p>CARTER'S LITTLE LIVER PILLS.</p>
        <p>lmall Pill. Imail Dose. mall Price.</p>
        <p>Cu_ , _ Sallow ,omplexion, ant, Sick Headaches promptly : and cure them so as to stay stared. Chemists, Is. lid.</p>
        <p>3ohn Ellis has elicited from Mr. Curzon that there is a district in Armenia where the Turkish authorities have seized the money distributed in re- lief of the people for the payment of taxes—a bit of business-like cutene.. on the part of the objects of Sir Ellis Ashtnearl-B.rtlett's admiration about which Sir Philip Currie will have something to say to the Porte.</p>
        <p>The executive of the National Union of Teachers I are in high spirits at Sir John Gorst's recent promise to set the much talked of superannuation scheme on a solid footing, and in a moment of -ffa- sive gratitude they have almost declared Sir John to be the greatest Minister of Education of modern times. They say at least that if he carries out this purpose be will have done more for education than any Minister during the decade. This rather dis- counts all Mr. Aslland's important work at the Edu- cation Board, which ran upon very different lines, and had very different objects. The executive have passed a resolution of thanks to Sir John which cannot fail to stimulate him to make a liberal recognition of the teachers claims.</p>
        <p>The Queen who travels as the Countess of Bal- moral, will leave Windsor Castle shortly after ten o'clock on Monday morning for the Riviera. Ports- mouth has been definitely selected for the Royal embarkation. Her Majesty will sleep on board the Victoria and Albert at Cherbourg, and will resume the journey to Nice on Tuesday morning.</p>
        <p>The Prince of Wales was on Wednesday elected for the twenty-second year in ccession, as Most Worshipful Grand Mater of English Freemasons. and Grand Lodge afterwards passed a resolution of deep and sincere sympathy with the Queen and Princess Beatrice on the death of the Prince Henry of Battenberg.</p>
