      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY MARCH 7. 1-496</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY MARCH 7. 1-496</p>
        <p>when his uncle, now Duke of York, sits on the throne.— This i 9 because, by a statute of Henry VIII., a Prince of the blood must be a son, a daughter, a nephew, a niece, or a grandchild of the Crown. The Riviera is being right royally patronised this year. —Among the distinguished visitors already there, or expected to arrive are the Emperor of Austria, the D)w- ager Duchess of Saxa-Coburg-Gotha, the Dowager Czarina, the King of Sweden, the King of Denmark, the King and Queen of Saxsny, the Prince of Wales, the Duke and Duchess of Connaught, the Duke and Duchess of York, and the Duke and Duchess of Coburg. ; Mrs. Meade, whose second series of " Stories from the Diary of a Doctor" are announced far immediate publica- tion, is by birth Irish, and a popular writer for girls.— She comes from Bandon, co. Cork, her father was a clergyman, and she is one of a family of sir. Mrs. L. T. Meade began her career as a story-teller by relating mar- vellous flights of fancy to keep her younger brothers and sisters quiet, and was seventeen when her first work of fiction was written. It was called " Ashton Morton " and she sent it through a friend to the late Mr. Newby, who accepted it, and great was the delight of the young people at the rectory when a modest little cheque arrived which was shared by each alike.—Mrs. Meade usually turns out four or five books a year, and her home is in a south-west suburb of London, Mrs. M. 0. W. Oliphant, who is new writing an his- torical romance, can boast of being the most prolific author of this century. Her first novel was published nearly fifty years ago when she was not yet twenty-one. She has long been associated with " Blackwood's Maga- zine," and during the last half century hardly a year has passed but she has given to the world several novels, histories, or biograpkies. Apropos, it is a fact that nearly all our women novelists write under pseudonyms. Many following the example of George Eliot assume masculine names. Thus we have John Strange Winter, who is Mrs. Stannard, George Egerton, otherwise Mrs. Clement, and John Oliver Hobbes, who is Mrs. Craigie. ' Others prefer to be feminine if pseudonymous. For in- stance, Edna Lyall,whose name is Miss Bayly.Mrs.Andrew Dean, author of "The Grasshoppers," who is really Mrs. Alfred Sidgwick, and Sarah Grand, shortly to give to the world, in the form of a novel, her recent experiences in Paris, where she lived as Mrs. McPhail. Eton coats will be much worn again during the bright spring days and early summer, and there is no doubt they are most becoming, when well and carefully cut. At the Skating Palace in Argyll-st., a few afternoons back, I noticed one of the very newest of these coats worn by a tall graceful skater. The skirt was in black cloth, and the Eton in purple velvet. The wide revers were lined with white silk, also the wrist ruffles, while the neek stock was in creanay lace and chiffon. The four buttons which adorned the coat were of very fine cut ON hand a lar,3e black velvet picture hat trimmed with featherrrlnd bunches of violets completed this altogether fass mating toilette.</p>
        <p>Already sleeeless jackets have made their appearance. Last week at a smart up-to-date estanlishmeut I much admired one composed of black cloth, applique on a ground of heliotropa velvet, which had a delightful lace- like effect. It was made with a high Valois collar,sloped to a point, and was cut up at the dart on each side. This dainty little jacket is destined to be' worn by a u ell known society woman, accompanied by a gown of helio- trope liberty velveteen, made with a vQst of white cloth. Coquettish ruches in black, white, or colored silk</p>
        <p>gauze,with tufts of soft ostrich feathers dotted here and there still enjoy considerable favor among up-to-date women. These ruches are mounted on satin ribbon, stiffened with muslin invisible stitches keeping them in position. These dainty ruffles are easily made at home by girls possessed of clever fingers and a capacity for imitation, and they import a charm to many a middle aged gown that alas has lost its pristine freshness. With regard to evening shoes, the very newest are in satin, exactly matching the gown, or in fine kid em- broidered with beads. They are cut very low to show a good deal of the foot. For outdoor wear patent leather and glace kid are worn by the most fashionable women. How thankful the majority of us, who live in,or near, large towns, will be to Mr. Jacoby for his "Street Noises Bill " which be purposes presently to ask leave to introduce in the House cf Commons. It provides for the registration of itinerant musicians, singers, and mountebanks. No certificate holder is to play, sing, or perform, within 300 yards of any asylum, hospital, public institution. The same restriction applies to private residences where the certificate holder has been required to withdraw. Personally,l wish this Bill every success, for barrel organs are my pet aversion. 'Thera is oce I have in my mind at the present moment, that plays Gounod's " Serenade," that exquisite thing with the gentle oscillation of a cradle or a hammock in it, and this terrible instrument instead of ending on the last note, which should be so soft awl dreamy, finishes off by playing the chord of the scale in thirds and clos- ing an ontave higher. After the " Serenade " cornea that beautiful old Eaglish air " Sally in our Alley," drilled out in the wooden way inseparable from mechani- cal music. It seems deplorable that all the beauty of such songs, can, with impunity, be stolen from them in this unblushing fashion. Whenever I hear this particu- lar organ I get out my supply of cotton wool which I have iound it necesssary, in case of these emergencies, to have ever ready to hand. Some years ago the Marquis of Salisbury issued in- structions to our ambassadors and consuls abroad to re- port to him the regulations in force in foreign cities, and it was then found that we alone, of all European nations allow the itinerant musician to make day and night hideous without restriction, But apart from street musicians (?) the noises in great towns are doubtlesa sources of real suffering to many, especially in the poorer quarters. Well or ill the unfortenate occupants of small houses have to bear the almost endless racket that begins with " milk oh," and goes on with the yells of the coal vendor, and costerinong,ers , the muffin man's bell, " Potatoes all hot " ana the itinerant oyster merchant all day long. I note with interet the announement in a New York paper that music is now being used as a curative agent in several American lunatic asylums. Simple tunes played by Italian organ grinders have it appears had an astonishingly quieting effect upon the lunatics, and ex- periments are now being carerully carried out to observe the exact effect of melody (?) on madness. I have never before heard of a barrel organ aa a soothing agent. Ac- cording to my experience it only maddens men and women ! The King of the Belgians detests instrumental music of any kind. It seems to cause him real physical suffering. At the sound of the piano or the Qaeen's harp, his Majesty swiftly vanishes. It seems we may expect the flying machine to become as accomplished as the auto-cars. " Let us fly " is an appeal which is, at the present moment only heard in melodrama, or read in the conventional three volumeq, but it may be a prosaic reality before long, and as com- mon as an invitation to cycle or skate. This suggests fearful terrors for the future. What with running the risk of an aerolite dropping down from the sky, horse- less carriages dashing about the streets and flying machines vvhizzing about overhead, which may at any moment crash in on one's hat, through the gear going wrong, the chances for aur poor nerves grow smaller and smaller. Our only hope is that the County Councils will cieclare flying-machines to be illegal sky signs. My readers shnuld remember that not one scrap of orange peel should be wasted. It should he dried in a cool oven and stored away in paper bags. It is ex.:ellent for lighting up fires that have become low, or in case of illness when the crackling of wood is likely to disturb the patient. Jamaica jelly is a nice way of usint tinned pinsiaPnle. First soak one ounce of gelatine in half a pint of cold water for a couple of hours, then add all the liquid from the tin of pineapple, the juice of two lemons.four ounces of loaf sugar and half a pint of boiling water. Strain through a piece of flannel. Have ready a quart mould filled with enld water, turn this out, pour in a little jelly, lay on it a few oilcan of fruit cut small, allow this to set, then add mere jelly and pineapple till the mould is filled. Stand for six hours and serve on a glass dish. " MADGE."</p>
        <p>The numhrr of dark-haired girls who get married greatly exceeds that of the fair ones, a statistician tells us, and in order to prove this is owing to man's choice, not the redundancy of brunettes, he proceeds to show that an overwhelming majority of these women who "live and die unmarried "—he is too gallant to call them old maids—have fair hair and blue eyes. Tortnisehell cats are said to have come originally from the Danish island Langelan,which is also the home of the finest dogs called " Great Danes." The famous dogs, as in the case of horses, display a great fondness for torteiseshell cats, with wham they form inseparable companionship. One of the most slosTgish creatures known is the tau- tana, a nine-ineh lizard, whose home is in New Zealand. Ha is usually found clinging to the rocks or logs, and has been known to remain in one position perfectly motionless for many months. Professor Lepine of Lyons had recently under kis care a young girl who had for four days been affiicted with hi-ccough, the respiration being 30 per second. The girl was not hysterical, hot complained of certain symptoms of dyspepsin. Asked to show her tongue, she held "the unruly member" outside the den'al arches for some time Struck by the cessation of the hiccough while the tongue was in this extended position M. Lepine main- tained the traction for several minutes, with the result that the symptom disappeared definitely. This is not the first successful application of the method for the core of hiccough, M. Viaud having reported two cases in , 1894.</p>
        <p>TO PRESERVE YOUR HAIR use common sense: don't ruin it by sousing it with cold water or starve it by neglect: all living, things require n utrishment. and pure grease is uu- doubtedi the beet nourisher of the hair : for mord than a century. ROWLAND'S MACASSAR OIL has been considered the best pre.erver of the hair ever used it prevents it falling off or turning grey, r,rnoves scurf and promotes a luxutiant, growth : for children it is unsurpassed : also in a golden colour for fair hair. Bottles 3s 6d., 75., 10s 6d. (equal to 4 small), Row:and's -Macassar Oil.—Nourishes the hair. Rowland's Macassar Oil.—Prevents baldness. Ask anywhere for Rowland's Macassar Oil, of F.O, Hatton Garden, London, and avoid cheap, worthless imitations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>LADIES' .LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' .LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAB.EST NELLIE,</p>
        <p>I am revelling in the advent of the spring flowers, but I am not in a great hurry for the spring this year, my bank balance not being in the condition which I could wish, while my wardrobe keeps up to its perennially shabby traditions; and under these circumstances I can hardly welcome the sun which shall reveal to the world at large the sins of omission in my costume so kindly concealed by the murky atmosphere peculiar to this delight,. ful England of ours. ,</p>
        <p>A hat that looks very stylish and effective is one made of drawn black silk, wi'ch a scarf of black net with a white lace appliquo upon it, fastened on one side with a bunch of black feathers. I still continue to talk about sleeves, those which fit the arm holding a high place in our esteem, while we are ungrateful enough to denounce the large puff in no moderate terms, and to contem- plate the bodices which are our immediate joy merely to deliberate whether it be possible to reduce the fullness of their sleeves. This is a serious question, but for my part, I am afraid it can be answered only in the negative ; for, where- as the sleeves of yester-year excelled in width, tile sleeve of to-day will excel in length, falling for the most part over the hand and setting in wrinkieg from above the elbow. Under these circumstances we may as well at once abandon all thoughts of altering our old bodices with any degree of success, and turn our attention seriously to the contents of our purse to see whether these will justify us in buying new frocks, which, of course, we all of us desire. However, I am occupying my spare time in trying to induce my last year's sleeves to look as if they were of this year's date. It is rather a hopeless task, and only to be managed by gather- ing the material tightly round the arm. about six inches above the elbow, and letting it disappear in pleats into the seam, making it perfectly tight- fitting, but yet, of course, full.</p>
        <p>A very becoming dinner or ball bodice has the sleeves made of brocade, while the bodice itself, with gored basque, is of a dark-hued velvet lightened by bead trimming and paste buttons, and with a cascade of lace down the front. Over the shoulder are four rows of beads keeping the bodice in place. A stylish cloth bodice, of which the chief feature is the quaintly-shaped revers and epaulettes, which require very accurately cutting, and carefully lining with tailor's canvas or buckram; in fact, it would be wise to insert the small steel or whalebone stiffeners in the points, for, if these become limp, the style of the garment is lost at once. The sleeve is cut in three pieces, including the rever or epaulette. The basques are slashed at back and front, as shown in the sketch, revealing tabs of velvet, and the three tiny buttons make a smart finish to these. Amateur dressmakers, however, would do well to omit these slashings, and to have plain basques, just finished with stitching.</p>
        <p>A very pretty spring costume is made of electric blue velveteen, with the shoulders and the w aist outlined with v, Ivimining or jet, the small square at the neck being tided up with white lisse tied into a bow at the back. Walking gowns will be of two distinct types ; the severely simple, and the extremely elaborate. In the former category must be placed the coat and skirt, which will retain popularity in an up- to-date form. The coats will be very plain and quite close-titting ; in fact they are nothing more or less than well-shaped jacket bodices, with very little ornamentation, and with comparatively small coat sleeves, becomingly full at the top, and set in the armhole in fine small regular pleats—not box-pleats—on each side, from the top of the shoulder to about five inches down, and thence eased in quite plain. These sleeves are a great improvement on the voluminous ones that were worn in the autumn, and that were so awkward to manipulate. It is impossible to wear a blouse under these coats, which must follow the lines of the figure accurately, and smart women wear a spencer either of silk chamois leather, or finely- knitted yarn. The latter are the warmest for present wear.</p>
