        <p>ROUND ASIA ON HORSEBACK.</p>
        <p>AN ADVENTUROUS PRINCE. tea Prince Constantine Wiasemsky has just comple soF journey lasting two years and a half which has Wilier less interesting from a scientific point of view than 4, tile in incidents. He has been round Asia on horsebati'ro covering a distance of 26,875 miles, visiting in Siberia, Mongolia, the whole of China from noT;bit. south and from east to west, Tonquin, Ana, ent.,osi China, Cambodia, the Wis country, Siam, the niy• But mah, Manipur, Assam, India, kashmir, Thibet'kedl Caucasus. In the course of his travels he was atteo'oll four times by wild people and twice wounded ill shoulder and leg, while, but for a lucky escape, captivity, he would probaby have ben liilled by a tr.! of fanatics in Thibet. Alter having been delayeti floods in Siam, where he contracted malarial fever!, nearly died from thirst in the desert of Gobi. whey. 01 ,ty his horses perished, and he did part of his joa :6110 other animals riding oxen in Cambodia an doing' 600 miles on elephants in Siam, while in Thibet be strode the yaks, or mountain cattle, which travel W ii horses cannot go. When he could not get a h91:;11 travelled on foot. Be took copious notes upon the ti:4,74A' and dress of the natives, and visited several Budgf monasteries in India, where he claims to have discolin the secret tat the fakirs. Prince Wiasemsky had OAP ridden round the Mediterranean from France to Fejt), and thence to Africa, and he dined with Gordone,rloct a few weeks before the capture of Khartoum. Wiasemsky, who says that he lost all the twelve Olt which he took out, is residing at Rome to recaperii""oei strength, as he is suffering very much from the fatig ot his last journey.</p>
