        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 7, 1896</p>
        <p>• was of opininn that considering the exceptional cir- out of the moneys coming to them large grants to insti- cumstances, they would not be wasting this money at totes in county boroughs, for instance the Harris Insti- all by expending it in the way they intended. tute at Preston .had received £1,700 ie.three years ; the The MArog observed that this building (the Town ;Owens College, Manche.ter,£Boo in two year. ; the Uni - Hall) was certainly very inadequate for the require, I versity of Liverpool,. £BOO in twi:: years; £5O had been ments of the Corporation. He wished the inspector granted to the School of Cookery at Manatiester, and would come round one of these days and see six of their £lOO to the School of Cookery in Liverpool. The Wigan clerks working in a space of but 360 cubic feet instead Corporation,in connection with the School of Mines,had Of 2,000. Yet, notwithsthnding this the Corporation had a very handsome contribution. Whilst they in was going on year after year without doing anything Warringeon had prepared their Eirrangements.for 1895-6 practical. upon the basis of previous year, they had almost with- The amendment was then put and voted for by nine, out notice,and after they had entered upon their scheme. whilst 22 members opposed it. The fallowing were the .had their grant reduced from £6lO to £51.8, and that. members who voted :—For'the amendment—Councillors notwithstanding the fact that the County Council itself Roberts, Longton, Bennett, Farness, Duncan, Tinnion, had not received a wryly less during the year than it Rogers, Woods, and monks. Against The Mayor, received during the preceding year. The effect was Aldermen Greenall, Button, Wright, Bolton. Hutchinson, absolutely to cripple the intentions of the Technical In- and Francornb ; Councillors Davies, ,Pierpoint. Shaw- struction Committee of Warrington, and render more Green, Plimpton, Smethurst, • Shaw, Evans, Ellison, unSatiSfactory than otherwise Would have been the Baird, Shillcock, Lee, Wilkinson, flepherd, Garner, and case, what wou:d ha,ve been a magnificent institution in Heaton. The minutes were then carried. • ' three years.. . (Hear, hear.) It was becOming a.hiolutely MARKETS COMMITEEE. intolerable to have the heavy iron 'hand of the Couety , The minutes of the committee were of a routine char- ('. )uncil upon them in regard,to the distributipn of these acter, and were adopted, on the proposition of the moneys. (Hear, hear, and applause.) DEPUTY-MAYOR, seconded by, Alderman HUTCHINSON. Councillor MONKS said that theenattee'was even worse GAS COMSIITTEE.—A CAUSE FOR THE SCARCITY OF COPPERS. than it had been made out by Councillor Roberts,because Councillor UEPHERD, in proposine the adoprieri of' the the Museum Committee had to submit to the County minutes, remarked that owine'to the penny-in-the-slot Celincil an estimate of expenditure for the coming meter system,,liavine, been nclopted, there had been an winter,and the county did not approve or disapprove; in increased consumption of gas. During the last qoarter fact they left them in ignorance, and hearing nothing they had collected about 101,000 pennies from these from them the Museum Committee thought the matter meters, which represented £421. (Hear, hear.) It had had bten approved. NoW,when it was too late,they cut been brought to histknowledge that a scarcity of coppers their income down £lOO a year. had been brought about by this accumulation iii the The MAYOR : And how are you going to make up the meter., and-the committee had wisely made arrange- deficiency? You will be in debt. ments by whibh tradespeople, or anyone else requiring Alderman Surrox said it was hoped their representa- coppers, could be supplied on-application at .the ofEces. tives on the County Council would endeavbur to improve (Laughter.) Matters. (Hear. hear.) Alderman SUTTON : For nothieg. (Renewed laughter:) The discussion then dropped,the 'minutes Were passed, Alderman BOLTON seconded the minutes. • and the Council rose Councillor ROBERTS, after haying congratulated the GAS Committer upon the most satisfaetory result that had been obtained by the use of the slot meters, went on to express pleasure at the knowledge that the committee had accePted a local tender for the supply of the iron• work needed for the 'erection of a roof at Longford Depot. (Hear, hear.) He was of theopinion that where the difference between the amount of the local and out.- side tenders was comparatively small, the preference should ne given to the local firm. (Hear, hear.). He was not arguing from the -merchant's point of view, but thought that where the article was produced in town, and Warrington labour was ernployed,it would be better to pay a little more than to ge outside, for when the tender was given to a local firm the money went in wages to local labourers, and subsequently came back in the shape of rates. (Hear, hear.) , RIVERS COMMITTEE. The minutes were of a formal character, and were adopted, on the motion of Councillor Roneters,eeconded by. Councillor Ileaercee. FIRE ENGINE COMMITTEE—THE FIRE AT COCKHEDGE MILLS</p>
        <p>Alderman HUTCHINSON proposed the confirmation ol the minutes which appeared in our last issue, and in re- ferring to the serioui fire which had recently occurred at Cockhedge Mills,remarked that the particulars of this event would he given at the next meeting of the Coun- cil. As was to be seen in the minutes,however, the cost only amounted to £24 75., which was ;my small, he thought. Councillor Straw GREEN: What: only £21.. Oh ! you mean that's the .cost of putting the fire out, and not the amount of the damage. (Laughter.) Alderman Fltercanisox : Of course it is for the cost of extinguishsng the fire. Continuing, he said the fire commenced at about a quarter to six in the morning,and it was the CA Dtai ,tht, large engine,which was first taken out,and not the Major, which did good service, as the manager of the mill and others had admitted. The Major worked up to about eight o'clock.or 830, and when the fire had been almost extinguished several tubes in the engine gave way, and it could play no longer. Its ser- vices were not further required then as a matter of , fact. The MAYOR : Is this the first time the tubes have even way at a fire P Alderman HUTCHINSON : It is not the first time, but an engine having worked for sixteen years is, like an old man of 70, bound to get loose BOMB tiMP. (Laughter.) In two or three days after the fire the Major was fit to do its duty again in case of a fire either inside or outside the town. Let the mnmbers of the Council give credit to the Fire Briade for once. (Hear, hear.) Councillor SMETHURST seconded the motion. Councillor BENNETT said he was glad that he did not rise to cemplhin of the fire brieade but that he could congratulate tne committee on what seemed to have been a piece of geod work on the occasion 'of a fire at CockhedgdMills; but the Council ought to know what the exact time was from the 'moment the alarm was received until the engine was in the mill yard. (gear, hear.) Alderman HUTCHINSON : From the time the bell was rung until the engine was in full working order and play- ing on the building 35 minutes had elapsed. Councillor SMETHURST explained that it was a longer time than ought to have been occupied, hut it should be explained that the position in which the .engine was placed was a very awkward one indeed. They had to knack part of the wall down to get the suction pipe through. Councillor SHAW rose to speak, but was ruled out of order, as was also Alderman Hutchinson, who said he I wished to correct a remark he had just made, and the minutes were pa,.aed. WATER COMMITTEE. —NEW WATER ,PITTINOS INSPECTOR. Alderman FRANCOMB moved the confirmation of the minutes of the committee which we published last</p>
        <p>week. Councillor HEPHERD seconded the motion. Councillor SHAW wished for some information with reference to the following minute-: " That Frederick Lloyd be appointed (subject to one month's approved serlire) water fittings inspector at 325. per week, with uniform." Had proper credentials been given, and was he a qualified man? , • Councillor Latvians suggested that Lloyd should also be instructed to inspect the water fittings at the Cor- poration public buildings. (Hear, hear.) Alderman FRANCOMB said that the appointment was unanimous on the part of the committee. but Lloyd wonld have to give a month's satisfaction to the com- mittee. He was a properly qualifirKl man, and he read a note which had been received from Messrs. Thomas Allen and Sons (where the inspector was once employed) in support of that statement. He (Alderman Francomb) saw no reason why Lloyd should not do as suggested by Councillor Longton. (Hear, hear.) The minutes were agreed to. GENERAL PURPOSES 'CONDJITTEE.—OOI7NTY COUNCIL AND THE TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION GRANTS.—GREAT INJUSTICE TO WARRINGTON. The MAYOR, moved and Alderman Sullen; seconded the adoption of the minutes which we published last week. Councillor ROBERTS explained, as had already been done in the newspapers, that the position taken up by the Corporation with regard to the petition against the Ship Canal- Bill was simply to enable them to obtain a locus standi, and to make mutual arrangements with re- gard to the Bill. The Councillor then proceeded to refer to the resolution with regard to the necessity of a more favourable distribution of the grants for technical in- etruction, about which he splice very strongly. Be did not know whether the *hole of the members of the Council were aware of the fact that the resolution was the outcome of a petitian presented by the Non- Ccunty Boroughs Association, and if they succeeded as he heped they would, in the object of their petition, the result to Warrington would be that they would obtain an additional grant that would compensate them for many years' contribution to that important Association. (Hear, hear.) The memorial- ists stated "that by the operation of the Local Taxation Act of 1890, as administered hitherto in Lancashire, the non-county boroughs of Lancashire were placed at con- siderable disadvantage with the rest of the county and with the county boroughs in the matter of technical in- struction, by not having placed at their disposal their fair share of the grant as intended by Parliament; and they suggested as a remedy for the abuse the passing of an Amending Act, providing alternatively either that the non-county boroughs should have their share of the grant paid to them direct, as in the case of county boroughs ; or that,in cases where the Council of a muni- cipal borough which is not a county borough desire themselves to contribute for the purpose of technical instruefinn under the provisions of the Local Taxation Act, 1890. they may send notice to the County Council that for the purpose of such contributions for technical instruction they require payment in each year of their share of the grant as may be ascertained by the Amend- ing Act (either on a base of ratable value or population, or both) of the moneys received by the County Council under the Local Taxation Act, 1890, and thereupon their share shall accordingly be paid in each year by the County Treasurer to the Borough Treasurer out of the moneys received under the last-men- tioned Act in priority to any other payment.' The distribution of the Exchequer grant, continued Councillor Roberta, at present To most inequitable,and it was towns like Warrington—which was entitled to be made into a county borough in view of its population— that suffered very unduly. County boroughs would receive their full quota as a matter of course, and had absolute control over the distribution ; but Warrington eves entirely in the hands of the County Council,and the distribution of the grant in Lancashire was more inequit- able than in any other county in England. Mr. Roberts then proceeded to give statistics to prove that that was so. The total amount received by the Council of Lanca- shire since the passing of the Act was £198,553.03. 4c1., or an average of £41,197 for the four years,after deduct- ing the grants made to non-county boroughs. The received a £911.9. .0 1001: average amount received per year durine the four years by the non-countv boroughs from the County Council had been .175, perT n e 17070- non-county nttbye population, boroughs against £32 15s. 3d. per 1,000 received by the rest oe as county and as against £23 14s. 6d. received by per 1000 r the county boroughs. e Taking the percentage' of money received, and comparing it with the percentage of pop- ulation, the county received 9.19 too much of the grant the county boroughs 3.71 too little, and the non-county boroughs 5'48 too little. The county had made</p>
        <p>STRENGTHENING THE NAVY.</p>
        <p>THE PROGRAMME AT A GLANCE. Mr. Gosehen's proposed addition to our Navy and its cost may be summed up briefly thus THE MEN.</p>
        <p>Added seamen „ engineers artisans 342 „ mariners</p>
        <p>Total 4,900 Active list of 1895 88,950</p>
        <p>Total active list Additions to reserve Previolits reserve</p>
        <p>Total reserve 25,000</p>
        <p>Total active and reserve 118,850 There have also beeen 23,000 men added to the dock- yard establishments.</p>
        <p>THE SHIPS. to. Proposed new battleships 5 „ Ist class cruisers ... 4 2nd class „ „ 3rd class „</p>
        <p>Total lB Already under construction</p>
        <p>Total ships and cruisers building 47 Prop,sed new torpedo boat destroyers 2B Now under construction 4O</p>
        <p>Totol torpedo boat destroyers Add to the above new guns, increased ammunition, new hospital and barrack accommodation, a training- ship for Queenstown' new docks at Gibraltar, &amp;c. Tall MONEY. Cost of accelerating Lord Spencer's shipbuilding : Wages £150,00 Armour Stores Guns 2)O,00 i'''''</p>
        <p>£975,000 In round numbers, but stated by Mr Gosehen to be really Cost of new shipbuilding 4. 10,030.000 Cost of new ordnance 850,000 Naval works</p>
        <p>Total £17,450,000</p>
        <p>THEY WONDERED TO SEE HIM. " I could not move a yard without help. I can now walk for miles." There is certainly a very sharp contrast between these two statements. When we see a person who, because of illness,is unable to move a yard without help, we do not expect to meet him on the road and on foot miles from home, soon thereafter ; if indeed, we meet him at all. At least we should regard these extremes, considered as within the experience of the same man, and enclosed within a comparatively brief period of time. as some- thing to wonder and ask questions about. And people did wonder at and inquire about it. Many said the cir- cumstances recalled the age of miracles, supposed to have passed forever away. The facts (briefly set forth in a letter from the man himself) are as follows. We may add that Mr. Henry Jackson is a farmer, well known and respected in his district, and his case is familiar to neighbours and friends of his throughout the vicinity. If," In the early part of 1892," says Mr. Jackson," I be- gan to feel weak and ailing. I was low in spirits, and my bodily strength seemed to be leaving me. There was a bad and nauseous taste in my mouth ; my appe- tite, which had always been good, failed until I had no real desire for food whatever, and after eating I had much pain at the chest and a fullness around the sides. My stomach always felt burning hot, and I had a gnaw- ing pain at the pit of it. " I remained in this general condition until August of the same year, when I was taken worse. My legs be- gan to swell, and rheumatism set in all over me, more particularly in the hips and back. No local treatment bad any effect upon it. It grew worse and worse, until I was no longer able to rise from my chair without as- sistance. In truth. I had no power over myself, and could not move a yard without help. " I suffered so with mere pain that I could not lie in bed, and for over twelve months I never had my clothes off. "During this time I was attended day and night, being literally unable to do anything of importance for myself. All the sleep I got was taken in naps and snatches while I was bolstered up in my usual place in an easy chair. Under the terrible strain of the pain and loss of proper rest my nerves broke down so that any uncommon event in the house, or noise, was more than I could bear. My heart was very bad, and thumped until I could scarcely stay in the chair and endure it. ' " The doctor who had charge of my case said my condi- tion was critical. He said that my lungs and liver were badly affected, and that I had Bright's disease of the kidneys. Still his medicines did me no good and after attending me ten months he said he could do no more</p>
        <p>for me. "I then got a doctor from Bolton to see me, and he held out but slender hopes of my ever getting any better. I thought the same, and so did all who saw me. "In October, 1893, my daughter, Mrs. Dickinson, of Bolton, told me how she had been benefited by taking Mother Seigel' s Curative Syrup, and thought it might possibly help me. I had small faith, but there could be no harm in trying. So we sent at once to Mr. Pare, the chemist, in Fold-road, Bolton, for enough to decide whether it would do me good or not. After taking it a short time I was better. I could sleep better, and had some appetite for food, and what I ate agreed with me. I This was hopeful and cheering indeed. "I kept on with the Syrup and it acted :wonderfully with me. The worst symptoms abated, and I gained strength. Soon all the water in my legs passed off ,and the rheumatism troubled me but little. Still using the Syrup my condition continued to improve in ever y respect. until I once more stood on my feet, and felt like a man of this world. I can now wale for miles and have no pain. All my friends think as I do—that under the circumstances my recovery was nothing short of mar- vellous. You are at liberty to publish this statement, and refer any interested persons to me. (Signed) Henry Jackson, Pewett Hill Farm, Culcho th, near Warrington, October 9th, 1895." No words of ours can add to the convincing force of Mr. Jackson's plain statement. His disease was origin- ally and radically of the digestion. The attack was sharp and profound, and developed into the resulting conditions he so well describes.. He may not have had Bright's disease, but that he was directly progressing to- wards the fatal malady there is no doubt. The effect of Mother Seigel's Sprup in his case only serves to show afresh its rare and remarkable power. Scarcely is so great a victory to be looked for from any medicine. Yet the facts are undeniable. We congratulate Mr. Jackson on his escape from a danger which was much more seri- ous than even he probably imagined.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.-THE NAVAL ESTIMATES.</p>
        <p>Mr. LABOUCHERE asked the First Commissioner of Works in the House of Commons on Moneay whether his attention has been called to the statue" purporting to be of the late Mt. John Bright" in the central lobby, and whether it was to remain there. Mr. AKERS DOUG- LAS, said he did not think, under all the circumstances, the question was one for him to consider. He had heard very various views with regard to the artiatic value of the statue, but nothing that he had so far beard would lead him to take anysteps in the matter. Sir M HICKS-BEACH, in answer to Mr. Oswald, said there would be no space to impress the words" a farth- ing" on the obverse side of farthings coined in the future without a complete alteration of the design of the coin, which he was not prepared to make. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN statei that he had no information as to whether Sir H. Rebinson or Sir J. de Wet knew on the 7th of January of the terms of Dr. Jameson's sur- render, but he had telegraphed to South Africa for in- formation. On going into Committee of Supply on the Navy Estimates Mr. GOSCHEN made a statement as to the naval programme of the Government. They proposed, he said,to increase the number of men by 4,9oo,making a total strength of about 94,000. The supply of young officers was no important matter, and after consultation with the heads of some of the great educational estab- lishments the conclusion had been arrived at that if the age was advanced a year it would be possible to have naval classes at the great schools. They had also decided to establish a training college to take the place of the training ship Britannia. He should ask the House to assent to increases of £850.000 for ordnance and £500,000 for storage and magazines. They had concluded to build three docks at Gibraltar instead of one, and the cost of the new works was estimated at two and a quar- ter millions. The works included in the new Naval Bill, including docks, barracks, hospitals, Sze., would bring up the amount from £8,500,000 last year to £14,000,000. As to where the money was to come from, the Chancellor of the Exchequer had authorised him to state that he would put aside the surplus of this year, atter satisfying the Supplementary Estimates, to form a fund which in the first instance should supply the expense of the naval works. They also proposed to add five battleships, four first-class and three second- class cruisers, and 28 torpedo-boat destroyers to the navy, making 105 ships and 62 torpedo-boat destroyers added since 1889. The cost of the new ships,in addition to what was in hand at the beginning of the year,would be £lo,ooo,ooo,which would be spread over three years. It was proposed to finish the whole of Lord Spencer's ships and the whole of the present Board's ships by July 1899. The total of the Naval Estimates for the year would be £21,823,000, against £18,701,006 last year. The Estimates were not Estimates of provocation, but of self-defence. The debate was at once adjourned. The Local Government (Electons) Bill was passed through its.committee stage, read a third time, and passed. Mr. RITCHIE then formally moved the second reading of the Light Railways Bill. Mr. LOUGH moved an amend- ment asking the House •,c) decline to sanction any schema wkich woull increase the burden of local rates or add to the weight of Imperial taxation. Mr. PERKS seconded the amendment. The debate was continued until mid- night, when Mr. Batsorra moved the closure. The Imotion was adopted, and the second reading was then agreed to.</p>
        <p>In the House of Lord; on Monday the Bill to enable women to be elected as members of Boards of Guardians in Ireland was read a second time.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY. Ta the House of Commons on Tuesday Mr. Hanbnry informed Mr. 11. Heaton that there was little likelihood of the Postmaster General adopting underground tubes for the transmission of letters. He also told the same hon, member that there was no evidence to lead the postmaster General to believe that dishonesty was increasinF,v in the postal service. Mr. Lona, in reply to Colonel Mellor, agreed that it was frequently very desirable that dog-muzzling orders should be applied uniformly over a sufficiently wide area irrespective of the fact that the area was comprised within the jurisdiction of several local authorities. He I was aware of the conflicting and contradictory orders for the muzzling of dogs issued by the County Council sad certain local authorities in Lancashire, but he was afraid the inconvenience caused thereby must to some extent occur, whatever might be the boundary adopted. Mr. S. SMITH asked the House to accept a resolution expressing " its deep sympathy with the sufferings of the Christian population in Asiatic Turkey," and trusting that further endeavours would be made to • ameliorate their lot. He said he had no intention to censure the Government, for he cheerfully allowed that they had made most earnest and strenuous attempts to protect the Armenian Christians. His desire was to obtain a verdict of the House in favour of further efforts on behalf of the Chris- tians in Turkey, perhaps on different lines. Sir J. Ken- naway seconded the motion. Sir E. GREY defended the action of the late Government in regard to the Armenian massacres. There was, he said, a tendency to hold that the Liberal Government was responsible for the policy of their successors, and that the fact that they did not act alone before the end of June was proof positive that Lord Salisbury could not take action alone. Neither of those statements would bear examination, and he held most strongly that Lord Salisbury's policy had been dis- tinctly his own policy. Mr. CURZON said that in its Present form the Government were prepared to accept the resolution, but it must not be understood that they regarded it as possible that the endeavours referred to in the resolution could be made by force of arms. He defended the position taken up by the Government, and stated that they had not been prepared to risk a general European war for the sake of the Armenians. The reso- lution was subsequently agreed to, amid cheers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>WEDNESDAY.-WORKING MEN'S DWELLING</head>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.-WORKING MEN'S DWELLING</p>
        <p>BILLS.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Wednesday Sir A. HICK- mAN moved the second reading of the Working Men's Dwellings Bill, the object of which, he explained, was to permit a local authority to lend money to workingmen under certain restrictions, for the purpose of enabling them to become the owners of the houses they lived in. Sir H. VINCENT supported the bill. Sir C. DIME objected to some of its provisions, and moved as an amendment that in any measure for facilitating the acquisition of dwellings for the working class by the Use of public moneys the freehold should be vested in public bodies, and not in the individual. After some remarks from Mr. M'KENNA, Mr. GALLOWAY, Mr. HALDANE, Mr. FLAN- NERy, Mr. A. HcauciNSON, and other members, Mr. CHAPLIN said the Government approached the considera- tiou of the bill with every feeling ef sympathy, but what was proposed was an entirely new experiment in legisla- tion, and they must proceed with caution. He suggested that after the second reading the bill should be referred to a Select Committee. Sir li. H. FOWLER also said he would like to see a Select Committee take the bill in hand ,and the House would see whether in its final shape the measure was one of which they could approve. The amendment was defeated by 276 votes to 91, and the bill was then read a second time. Sir A. HICKMAN pro- prsed that it should be referred to a Select Committee, but objection being taken the motion stood adjourned.</p>
        <p>RUPTURES.—REYNOLDS' Patent Imperceptible Indiarubber Stem Pad TRUSS. Suitable in all cases. perfect Fit Ensured. Write or call for particulars.— REYNOLDS, 10, Slater-treet (a few doors Bold-street), Liverpool. Open on Saturday until 7 v.m.— advt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CH URCHES-WARRINGTON,</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CH URCHES-WARRINGTON,</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>- - -- Wycliffe Church was crowded in every part on Sun- day afternoon, when the annual meeting of the P.S.A. Society was held under the presidency of Mr. T.Birchall, J.P. After devotional exercises in which Mr. W. H. Waite read the lessons. the Chairman said he felt somewhat honoured in being called upon to preside at that meet- ing as it was the first Sunday in the commencement of the 4th year of the Society. He thought it a movement, one of many movements in fact, of very great import- ance as showing that there was a new development of religious life in the country. (Applause.) The present was a time, as they all knew, of great unrest and great disquiet. Some people thought it was a bad time on that account, but he did not. Men were beginning to feel what great possibilitiee life had for them ; that this world was not made for a favoured few but that all the children of the Great Father ought to share in its bless- ings, to see and appreciate its beauties, and feel that they too were inheritors ofthis world as well as the world to come. (Applause.) A few weeks ago :se read in the "Christian World" a'passa.ge which struck him as beau- tiful and forcible, in which the writer said " Of late there seems to be what amounts to a re discovery of Christ." He (the speaker) believed there was. Not the Christ in the old theology but the Christ wholcame very near to them to-day, into the daily life of men and 'women, which he made brighter and better. Men were finding out that mere outward systems of reform were not enough and so they were looking anxiously for something else. The thing which appealed to them act supplying that want was:the gospel of Jesus Christ. With all that disquiet did He not say "One is your father, even God and all ye are brethren "—not a favoured few but all. Then He went on to tell them how deeply they ought to enter into His spirit until they loved one another as he loved them. Christ taught them the true secret of life, that a man was not great for his possessions but for his help to his fellows. When they made that principle truly part of their beings he (the speaker) thought that the salvation of the world would go on by leaps and bounds. The Chairman concluded by mentioning how interested be was in the movement and how deeply he sympathised with it, and with all the earnestness he was capable of he wished them God speed.' (Applause.) Dr. Waite, of Liverpool, said he was especially pleased to be with them at their anniversary as one of the ob- jects of an anniversary was to remind them of the things of the past and quicken their hope for the future. He rejoiced with them most heartily in all the success of the past, and in looking forward to the future he trusted that their brightest anticipations of prosperity and use- fulness might be realised. (Applause.) Dr. Waite then spoke at length on "Christ, the gift of God," and many other "gifts" bestowed upon us by our Creator. They were he said for the benefit of the race and not for the advantage of any favoured few. (Applause.) To the remainder of his speech the audience listened with great attention and frequently applauded his utterances. The Rev. John Yonge mentioned that 730 members had already re-enrolled themselves, and that spoke well for the future membership of the year. Dr. Waite gave a powerful rendition of the song "Redemption," and his son, Mr. W. H. Waite, sang with vigour and taste "The Mighty River."</p>
        <p>CONSUMPTION. 13RONCHITIS AND A_RTIIMA. I will demonstrate to the whole world and to suf- ferers from these unfortunate maiadies. how they can be permanently cured by an entirely New 'Method of treatment, without the chance of failure. Particu- lars ,"Gratts" from Mr. 'WILLIAMS, 11, Burwood place, Norfolk Crescent, London, W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>TO OUR WIVES AND MoTHERS.</head>
        <p>TO OUR WIVES AND MoTHERS.</p>
        <p>The health of woman is the foundation of beauty. I Good health ensures beautiful symmetrical liqes and loping, the healthy and blooming appearance, which even brings charms to irregular featoies. Beauty with- out health is impossible ; consequently it is obvious that women sherild pay greatest attention to retain good health. Unfortunately, women are greatly ex- posed to disorders which cause untold suffering and prevent them from fu!filling their home as well as social duties, marling life a burden. It is, therefore, not to be wondered at that War- ner's Safe Cure is such a great friend to the ladies. This remedy cures all derangements of the kidneys and liver, as well as the pelvic organs, removing irritating Acids from the bloqd—the cause of all inflammation, headaches,lassitude and the numerous other distressing symptoms so prevalent in female troubles. Thousands of ladies have been restored to health andlblooming ap- pearance by the use of Narner's Safe Cure, and their appeal to other lady sufferers should not be disregarded. Ms. J. A. Mitchinson, 5, Double Row, Harton Tyne Dock, co. Durham, says: "I can conscientiously attribute! my recovery to the use of Warner':: Safe Cure. In January 1895 I had a severe attack of inflammation of the lungs and a congested liver, which continued for over two months. Being under Medical treatment without any signs of permanent improve- ment, I then resolved to try Warner's Safe Core, and by taking one 4s. 6d. bbttle of same, improvement was per. : ceentinuitig the use until I had finished three more bottles, and my health was fully restored."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>MURDER OF-A WAttRINGTON</head>
        <p>THE MURDER OF-A WAttRINGTON</p>
        <p>LADY.</p>
        <p>FURTHER PARTICULARS OF. THE ARREST.</p>
        <p>We understand that. the only clue the Manchester police had to work upon in the Arnold murder case was the description given of a man who was seen in the Ram Hotel at Arnold, and who asked questions about Mr. Powley, who happened to be in the room. This proved to be but, a slender clue, but.fortunately.the Nottingham police found more important information. Photographs of known criminals were shown to those who had seen the supposed murderer and one of them was immediately identified by several people. It was that of a man named Samuel Wilkinson, a native of Firbeck, near Rotherham, who has been enployed as a stoker or gar- dener, and who is known of at Sheffield, Worksop, CleethorPes and Derby. A descriplon of Wilkinson was sent all over the country, and the police were every- where most active in looking for him. Late on Wednes- day night information was re3eived at Nottingham that a man answering the description of Wilkinson was in custody at Manchester, and Superintendent Harrop, of the Notts county police, and a Nottingham borough detective wont over to Manchester and identi- fied the man in custody as the prisouer they wanted. It appears that Wilkinson was in custody at Strangeways on a charge of breaking and entering into a golf pavilion in Birch-lane, Rusholme, and stealing a number of articles therefrom. In due course he was brought up before the Manchester magis- trates and remanded pending inquiries being made about him. Detectiva-Inspector Goodwin, of the Manchester city pAice, upon seeing Wilkinson, who, by the way, had given the name of Thomas Wilkinson, was struck by his resemblance to the suspected Arnold murderer, and he commnnicated his suspicions to Chief-Detective Cutinada. with the result that Nottingham was corn- munic.ated with, and Mrs. Powley. proceeding to Man. cheater, identified Wilkinson, on Thursday afternoon.in Strangeways Gaol. The news of the arrest spread in Nottingham with great rapidity, and there was tremen- dous excitement at Arnold,which• village has been in an unsettled state ever since the tragedy was perpetrated. IMPORTANT DISrOVERIES TN MANCriFSTER.</p>
        <p>REMOVAL 01' WILKINSON FROM STRANGEWAYS: The Horne Secretary has issued a writ under the Habeas Corpus Act tor the removal of WilkinsOn from Strangeways Prison to the County Police Station at Not- tingham, where the Capital charge wit be preferred alainst him'. It may be mentioned that the police have nothing to do with the removal of the prisoner, which , will be effected by the gaol authorities. Two very im- portant pieces of evidence have been discovered by the Manchester detectives working in conjunction with Mr. Superintendent Tlarrop, of the Notts police. Detective. Sergeant Bicknell succeeded in tracing a watch and chain which were stolen from the house where Mrs. Kaye was murdered, to the prmtession of.a man nartd Ashton, residing in the neighbourhood of Ilyde-road. Ashton stated that:he bought the jewellery fram Wilkinson, whem he knew well, having 'both lived and worked with him. In addition to this a box containing the clothing which</p>
        <p>the prisoner was suppnsed t 1)9 wearing at the time ef' the enmmission nf the tragedy, has been found at the</p>
        <p>Left Luggage Office at the Central Station, Manchester. It is stated that between the dates of the murder and Wilkinson's; errest on the charge of breaking int) the Anson Golf Club Pavilion at Rusholme he was a frequent visitor to a licensed house in theivighbourhood of Hyde- road.</p>
        <p>Her Majesty's ship Doris, the second of the two twin screw second,class cruisers now being built by the Nal Construction and Armaments Company, was laun('•hed at Barrow yesterday. Her total complement of officers and men will be 450.</p>
