      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>TO CURE BRONCHITIS.</head>
        <p>TO CURE BRONCHITIS.</p>
        <p>Think of the th busar HS of children who suffer every year with bronchitis, without receiving permanent re- lief. Notwithstanding many remedies given them, their systems are st.ll wt..ak, and every spell of bad weather brings anxifly to the parents. In all damp climates people are sulject t') colds, coughs, and affections of throat and lungs. The climate taxes even those who are strong, while the weak suffer. A little g,irl, Winified Hoihreot, who had bironch.; ; every winter, did n,)t get any relief that was permanent for some time. Her mother writes :</p>
        <p>.6.1‘1y little daughter Winifred, age five years, having derived such ben, fits from Scott's Emulsion. I thought I would write to inform you of this fact. From a baby she has suffered from bronchitis every winter, and I could not get an3,thinc,, to do her any r,-al good until a friend advised me to try Scott's Emulsion. I bought a bottle and gave it to her in teaspoonful does. From the first I could see the good it was doing her. She soon began to like it, lnd wanted to be taking it all the time After she had finished taking one large bottle, her couzh had entirely disappeared and her breathing was quite free. She has put on fleah also by taking the Emulsion, and is better now timn ever before. I have recom- mended this preparation to several of my friends who have had sickly babies, and they hz.ve used it with marked results.—(Signed), MRS. L FIOLBROOK, 13, Grovc-stmet, Bath, November 2nd, 1895." There are many reasons why Scott's Emulsion is such an effective nourisbment e rid curativeagent for children. It is cod-liver oil so perfectly emulsionised and skilfully manipulated that it is eagy of assimilation and perfectly palatable. Clmbineki with this important agent are the HypophoThites of Lime and soda, alike beneficial to the nerves and framework of the body. In no other form is to be found this perfect combination of these valuable nutritive and curative remedies so that Scott',, Emulsion has stood alcne nearly a quarter of a century, and is endorsed by the medical profession all over the world. Imitations do not accomplish satis. factory results, which prove their inferiority. Scott's Emulsion soothes the throat,acts directly upon the lungs, healing sore or inflamed places, and it feeds the blood with nourishing properties that give flesh, strength, and a healthy colour. No other preparation gives such quick and lasting benefits. In oil the condi- tions of decreased vitality and depleted blood, Scott's Emulsion will be found :lust what is needed to give flesh, strength, and vitality. The genuine Scott's Emulsion has a trade-mark of a man carrying a big fish on his back, on the wrapper.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON PERAA.A ENT BENEFIT BUILDING SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Last night week the annual meeting of this society was held in Cairc-street S3hoolroorn, Mr. William Tinsley occupying the chair. The CHAIRMAN, said he was a manager of the Warrington Saving Bank,and could say with confidence that thrift in Warrington was increas+ jug, which they might take as a most gratifying sign. (Applause.) The twenty-ninth yearly report showed that the re- ceipts were £4,296 17-,. 91; amount advanced £1,217 155. 111. against £983 83. 9,3. repaid ; the balance of the mortgage account .being £41,251 Is. 7d. The am- ount satoicribel by itiv,,,Aors was £1,563 is. 81. against £2,391 withdrawn, leaving the balance ot that account £3.328 113. Bd. 'flie profits, atter deducting working expenses, amounted to £1,534 183. 91., tont of which a dividend at the rate of £1- per c,nt. was declared, leav- ing £22 43, 111, to tie carried to the contingent fund. There were 406 members on the books, of whom 350 were investors and 55 borrowers. The auditors, Messrs. J. Dolton and W. Dick. had certified to the correctness of the balance skeet and report.—Mr. JAMES KENWORTHY proposed the adoption orthe report and balance sheet as printed. Ha mentioned that notwithstanding the recent bad trade, and the conse-quentidiffitaaies that had beset them, the work on the whole nad been slightly better than in the Previous year.—Mr. SHACKLETON seconded, and Mr. ROBERT Dams supported, the latter remarking that he had looked through the accounts and had the greatest • confidence in supporting them. The report was adopted., Messrs. James Kenworthy, Sargent Brayshaw,Thornas Critchley, John Dewhurst, Elwin %V. Gaskell, George Legge, Jehn Critchley, John Mather,Teter Charles Price, W. H. Robinson, and Charles Tiss were re-elected managers, Mr. Richard Sha,ckleton being selected to MI the vacancy caused by the retirement of Mr. Frederick Massey,who had gone to live oat of Warrington. Mr.A. Cook and Mr. Thomas Ince were elected stewards, the latter taking the place of Mr. Richard Shackleton. Messrs. James Bolton and William Dick were again chosen as auditors, and Mr. William Twiss as surveyor. On the -motion of Mr. WILLIAM [Vass, seconded by Mr. KENWORTHY, it was decided that the secretary, Mr. Robert Proud, should apply for a certificate of incor- poration for the Society.—Mr. KIRKCONNEL remarked that unless the Society took this course it would die a natural death 6n) the 25th March. A vote of thanks to the Chairman concluded the pro— ceedings.</p>
        <p>It is stated that the Admiralty has instituted an ins- quiry with reference to the delay in landing the troops trom the transport. Victoria, the vessel by which Dr„. Jameson and • his fellow-officers wer3 brought to this country. A Leamington guard named James Walton was killed at Noneaton on Wednesrray owing to tht brake-van of a train leaving the metals.</p>
