      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>*'*-***-* *IV</head>
        <p>*'*-***-* *IV</p>
        <p>"WHEN AND WHERE?" The Tea-tree was a wild and natural 24K . • *growth in the primeval forests fringing the 2or Himalayan foothills Its seeds was prob- ably carried thence into the upland of China and Japan long ere history began. Though milar the quaint Chinese 'mused and used the ,'-gr• *kindly leaf for ages before the Christian era, *. it was never known in England until some viethree centuries ago. And even then,so rare in London was the precious leaf, that courtiers lir allik paid ten guinees for a lb. of it. Think of *that and compare it with the mere twenty- 3or pence a lb. which you pay BROOKE, BOND'S for tea that's even better. *The Chinese call it ` tsha and. in the ipir days of good Queen Anne, Our fore-mothers 4v and the poet Pope sounded its English * name as ' tay ' as, indeed, do the French to-da:. *Up till three-and-thirty years ago, all the tea consumed in the United Kingdom came from China, vet, now, very nearly all the *tea we British drink is produced on the * British sell of lode and Ceylon. This vast Airchange in our national taste is chiefly due to the fact that the leaf grown and cured in * our Indian Epmire is purer, strong‘r, *better altogetherthan that of China. And whinev.r you want to prove how good is AIL the most carefully bleeded product of India and Ceylon. just g, or send to one of •All, Olin AGENTS, WHO ARE EVERYWHERE, Aga- * for one of the well-known packets sold at , 1/2, 1/4, 1/8, 2/-, 2/4, &amp; 2/8 a lb. AC BROOKE, BOND BC, Co. .1 Ltd - THE LANCASHIRE TEA WAREHOUSES 17 PsCOADILLY * q 9. MARKET STREET M&amp;NCIIRSTER * " *-* *</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>SCIENCE OF DESTRUCTION.</head>
        <p>THE SCIENCE OF DESTRUCTION.</p>
        <p>For the past quarter of a century, or so, human in- genuity has, says " Science Siftings," devoted itself in an extraordinary degree to the invention and perfection of instruments for the destruction of life. A study of the tables recording the losses in battle show that at the beginning of the century, when the musket in use was an extremely primitive specimen of the mechanician's art, two Englishmen could generally kill one Frenchman in a fair day's work, and two Frenchmen, at their best, could kill one German, the German output being frac- tionally less satisfactory. But with each succeeding im- provement in weapons, the time and labour involved in disabling an enemy has become greater and greater, till with the very latest improvement, bee-Metfords and Maxims, it really becomes a quPstinn whether the game its worth the candle, and whether it would not be better to revert, by international consent, to the use of stones and slings only, and thus relieve the suff-ring proleta- riat of Europe of some part of the extravagant expendi- ture on armaments under which they are at present groaning. As ttie subject is really one of great import- ance, a few more figurees may be excused. At Sal- feria°, in 1859, when rifled artillery and small arms were first employed, it took about ten Frenchmen a hard day's work to kill one Austrian, and fifteen Aus- trians about performed the same service for one French- man, and the average throughout the war was still more disappointing. In 1870, when breech-loaders were em- ployed on both sides for the first time,though in isolated instances some approx.im a tion to the old standard was at- . _ _</p>
        <p>. . tained(at Vionville, four Prussians killed one Frenchman and eight Frenchmen one Prussian in twelve hours' hard fighting), the general average for the whole war was almost ridiculous, only three per cent. of the invading army being killed and twelve par cent, wounded by the combined exertions of about a million men, for,in round numbers, one hundred days ; and, we have it on the authority of a " Globe " correspondent,that the cost for each Prussian killed works out to nearly £20,000. The influenza epidemic in 1692 in Germany caused an equal number of deaths without any expenditure at all.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>SILK.</head>
        <p>SILK.</p>
        <p>AU moths produce some form of silk. The silk worm is liable to over one hundred diseases.</p>
        <p>The professional name of the silk worm is the Bombyx Mori. Henry VIII. was the first English King to wear a pair of silk stockings. The silk-worm cocoon is from one inch to one and a half inches in length, and a bright yellow colour. In the time of Aristotle cocoons were unwound and silk was made in the Island of Gee. In the eighteenth century silken cocoons sold in the London market for one shilling per pound. In the South of China silk worms have been reared and silk manufactured for over three thousand years. The silk moth emerges from its cocoon in from fifteen to sixty days, according to the temperature. The size of each thread, as spun by the silk-worm, is one two-hundredth part of an inch in diameter. Some naturalists say that no insects except tho silk worm feeds upon the leaves of the mulberry. In the book of Ezekiel a fabric is mentioned by the name of silk as well known in Assyria and Babylon. An edict of the Emperor Tiberius forbade Roman citizens to wear any garments made in whole or in part of silk. _</p>
        <p>„. The official executioner of the Sultan of Turkey uses a silken cord in strangling persons ordered to be put to death. The silk worm is three inches long and is well provided with legs,;having no less than sixteen of these valuable members. Silk is the strongest of all vegetable or animal threads. It is three times as strong as a fitxen thread of the same size. Superstitious persons say that a wart cut off by tying a silk thread round it and drawing the ends tightly will never return. The silk from cocoons containing male insects is said to be stronger and better than that from cocoons made by females. The silken thread is spun from two orifices in the nose of the silk worm, the two threads being united by a gelatinous substance. Silk worm authorities estimate that the larvre from one ounce of eggs will eat one thousand two hundred pounds of mulberry leaves and produce one hundred and twenty pounds of cocoons. There is a shellfish in the Mediterranean which pro- duces a good quality of silk. Fabrics have been manu- factured from it, but only as curiosities. Among the coronation presents of Queen Elizabeth, a list of which is still preserved,two pairs of silk stockings were found. They were sent to her from Spain. The silk product of all countries in 1887, the last year for which complete statistics are available, was fifty- two millions of pounds, and its value was estimated at ninety-eight millions sterling. The spider produces silk of a fair quality, but the difficulty of rearing spiders and the small quantity of produce from each insect has caused the abandonment of all efforts to produce spider silk. In Greece in the third century B.C. the wearing of silk was forbidden to women,the husbands of those who violated this law being heavily fined, on the theory that a husband ought to be able to control his wife's taste for finery. The thread of the cocoon of the silk worm is not wound around and around, as might be supposed, but irregularly here and there, as the worm moved his head to and fro within his self-made house. Many yards of thread may sometimes be unwound from the cocoon without turning it ever at all. In the reign of Francis I. silk geWLIS were forbidden to all ladies below the rank of countess. The wives of wealthy merchants managed to evade the law by having woollen gowns lined with bright-coloured silks. The skirts were made open in front., turned back and but- toned like those of an old style army coat, to show the expensive lining.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>WELL KNOWN TRADE MIRK</head>
        <p>A WELL KNOWN TRADE MIRK</p>
        <p>UPHELD.</p>
        <p>The illegality of certain colourable imitations of the well-known trade mark "Carter's Little Liver Pills " has just been finally declared by a perpetual injunction of the Court of Chancery, granted by Mr. Justice Stirling, January 31st. The defendant had used the titles " Little Liver Pills," and "Coated Little Liver Pills," and the injunction restrained him from the use of these, and of any other title colourably resembling the words Little Liver Pills," the sole right in which was claimed by the Carter Medicine Commny, proprietors of Carter's Little Liver Pills, at whose suit the injunction was ssued.</p>
        <p>It is understood that the Carter Medicine Company is initiating a vigorous campaign against all infringers of their rights in this title, at Common Law and otherwise, and notices warning medicine-vendors and others have already been issued. Private information, to be treated confidentially, is being Invited in regard to the matter by Mr. C. Urquhart Fisher, of 45, Holborn Viaduct,Lon- don, E.C., solicitor to the plai nears, the Carter Medicine Company, with a view to proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>GRUESOME BEDCHAMBER.</head>
        <p>A GRUESOME BEDCHAMBER.</p>
        <p>TWO MYSTERIOUS DEATHS.</p>
        <p>- •• - -- - From a small town on the Marne comes a curious story of a bedroom at an inn, which, owing to the mys- terious incidents that have occurred therein, is known to the inhabitants by the ominous name of" The Accursed Chamber." According to report, a month or two ago a young man took this room, which is situated on the first storey of a small auberge, and retired to rest seemingly in the best of health. In the morning, as he did not make his appearance, the landlord went upstairs and knocked at the door. No answer was returned,and when the chamber was entered the young man was found life- less in the bed. Considerable surprise was expressed at this catastrophe, and the death, neverthelehs, was attri- buted to natural causes. Much excitement, however, was created among the population on Sunday (says the "Telegraph " correspondent),when it was declared that another terribly sudden death had occurred in the same room. The landlord and his wife had engaged a servant gill, 20 years of age, and to all appearance very strong and healthy, who had entered upon her work on Satur- day afternoon. The young woman was sent to the " Chambre Maudite "to pass the night, and as she did not appear next morning her master and mistress tapped at her door without eliciting any reply. Much alarmed, they sent a messenger at once to the gendarmerie, and when the agents of the law broke into the chamber they found that the girl was dead. The affair was promptly reported to the mayor, who has instituted an inquiry into this peculiar case. The body has been examined by a doctor, who has so far declined to grant a certificate for interment. Nothing,it is added, was discovered in the room to account for the death of the girl.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>LAST WEEK'S TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>Although the number of seagoing vessels which navi- gated the canal last week was less than in the previous seven days, there was a slight increase in the aggregate nPt registered tonnage. Coastwige traffic suffered a diminution of nine ships and 1,521 net tons, but this was more than counternalanced by an increase of 2,443 tons in the foreign trade.</p>
        <p>A terrible mining disaster has occurred at Kattowitz, in Upper Silesia, where the Cleophas Coalmine took fire. Up to Wednesday twenty-one bodies had boen recovered and the fate of a hundred additional workmen was uncertain.</p>
      </div>
