      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>EXAMINER SATUROAY, MARCH 7. 1896. \ RUNCORN AND WIDNES</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATUROAY, MARCH 7. 1896. \ RUNCORN AND WIDNES</p>
        <p>FREE GOSPEL YISSION, RUNCORN. Ist this mission church on Sunday evening,a success ml service was held,entitled "Joseph and his Brethren." Scenes illustrating the subject were shown on the scree by Mr. Hoxworth. There was a large congregation The Band of Hope meeting was held on Monday, node the presidency of Mr. Cooke. An interesting programme was submitted, and a pleasont evening was spent. MARINERS' CHURCH, RUNCORN. At. the weekly entertainment in this church on Mon- day evening, a lantern exhibition was given, and songs, Ix., were agreeably interspersed. Mr. W. Hox- worth occupied the chair. A reading entitled " Cash three " was contributed by Mrs. Forster, songs by Miss A. Hill, Miss M. Hil, Mr. W. Cooper and Mr. B. Hill ; ' ducts by Mr. T. Charleston and Miss Haves. The accom- panist during the evening was Mr. W. Gardiner. RUNCORNIANS AT THE WIDNES EISTEDDFOD. At the Eisteddfod at Widnes pn Thursday, a number of Runcorn people took part in the competitions, and were successful in securing prizes. In the soprano solo competition, Miss Heatherington, of Runcorn, carried off the first prize. The judge who awarded the prize, said she had given a most intelligent rendering of the piece (" Ora pro nobis "), and with beautiful finish. A num- ber of choirs bad entered for the glee competition but only one party from Bethesda Congregational Church, Runcorn (conductor Mr. C. Le Contour) put in an ap- pearance,and they gave an excellent rendering of "Sleep gentle lady," and the conductor was invested with the badge. In the Congregational choir competition, the prize was divided between St. Paul's Wesleyan kßuc- corn), and Trinity (Widnes). Mr.B. Crosby wielded the baton for the Runcorn choir.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>IS INDIGE3TION.</head>
        <p>IS INDIGE3TION.</p>
        <p>IT is to be hoped that the tax- The Relief of Lanalords&amp; payers and ratepayers of the country will pay very close attention to the rapidly developing policy in domestic affairs of Her Majesty's present advisers. Everyone knows that prior to the General Bloc- tion,sorne very attrxctive baits were,held out to the electors by the Ministers who now hold office. A good deal was said, for instance, by them and their supporters about old-age pensions; it was hinted that something would he done to enable workingmen to buy houses of their own, and even Lord SALISBURY vaguely suggested that something might be done for the unemployed. The questions are now all tabooed by the party in power. They have answered their purpose in securing votes and they are now either ignored or discredited. Old- age pensions schemes are rarely mentioned except with contempt and ridicule, and Lord SALISBURY seems to have now forgotten that there is any want of employment amongst the working-classes. It is true that on Wednesday a private Unionist member of the House of Commons moved the second reading of a Bill to provide facilities for the acquisition by working- men of their own dwellings, but what is the fate of the Bill? Are the Government prepared to secure its passage through Parliament as they might easily do with the large majority at their command? Not at all. Mr. CHAPLIN gave it a very modified approval, but in the end it was "blocked" and it is generally understood that it will not survive this process. The loss of the Bill is, on the whole, not to be lamented, for it was condemned by the most direct representatives of working-class opinion in the House of Commons, but its fate is a very useful icdication of the want of enthusiasm in the Unionist ranks for measures of social amelioration. In another direction a very different spirit is being manifested by these same politicians. The Tories are evidently bent upon again proving that they are above all things else a landlords' party. They have said very little publicly, and especially before the General Elec- tion, of their intention to give relief, directly or indirectly, to the landlords of the country, but it is now evident that a determined attempt is to be made to give that relief. A report by a majority of the members of the Royal Commission on Agri- culture has been laid upon the table of the House of Commons, and that report contains some ery important recommendations. As it is certain that the Government will, to a large extent at all events, act upon these recommendations it be- hoves, as we have said, the taxpayers and rate- payers, and especially those who are neither land- lords nor .griculturists, to consider the sugges- tions very seriously. The proposal made by these Royal Commissioners is, broadly speaking, that three-fourths of the rates now paid in respect of agricultural land should be transferred at one sweep to the shoulders of the State—in other words, that three out of the four millions of local rates now derived from the land should be replaced 11; u 4ditiCogai three millions to be taken froze the taxpayers who are for the most Part town residents. This is to be done under the plea of relieving land of some of its burdens. Its direct effect will be to relieve landlords from the necessity of r:ducing rents in times of depression, and to raise rents at the expense of the taxpayers in times of agricultural prosperity. In other words, it will be a direct gift to those who appro- priate the" un.earned increment" of this country. This would, surely, be class legislation with a vengeance. The partial way in which the Tories propose to relieve land of its burdens will be seen when it is considered what these " burdens" are. Ask any farmer what his burdens are and he will undoubtedly reply—" Rents, rates and tithes." The rents are received by the landlords, the tithes by the clergy, and the rates are used for the pub- lic benefit. The Tory royal commissioners do not propose to touch the rents or the tithes, yet what a margin for the relief of agricultural distress might be found here. By evidence given before the Royal Commission itself, was shown that in the cases of sixty-three tenant farmers who had kept accounts the total of their combined profits only amounted to £2,315. From these same sixty-three holdings, the landlords drew no less a sum than £37,044 in the shape of rents. Yet the commissioners do not suggest that relief might be given to these sixty-three farmers by even a slight remission of the extortionate rents they are called upon to pay. The relief is, in fact, not to come at the expense of the grasping land- owner but from the pockets of the town tax- payer. In other cases it has been shown that the tithe reaches an oppressive amount, and even exceeds the rent, yet it is not suggested that re- lief may be found in a remission of tithes. Nor is there any hint in this partisan report that the rates should be divided between landlord and tenant, so that the latter may in this way be re- lieved of part of the burdens which depress him and cripple his energies. The report is, it is now apparent,only a preliminary move in the direction of class legislation of a character so flagitious that even Tories should hesitate to romote it.</p>
        <p>The reply which the Chancellor of the Exchequer gave to a question put to him in the House of Com- mons lastiweek will not be altogether satisfactory to the people who are afraid of having one of the new farthings palmed off upon them for a half-sovereign. It was suggested to the Chan- cellor that the Queen's head might be taken off the obverse side of the farthing so that the copper and the gold coins might be more easily distinguished from each other. But the suggestion cannot be adopted. The subject of the Queen's bead on the farthing has, it appears, been very carefully con- sidered" by the official mind and no alteration can be made." It only 'remains, therefore, for people who are entitled to a half-sovereign to adopt the advice of certain enterprising tradesmen—they must ask for that coin and "see that they get it." The insidious farthing, however, new and bright it may be, can with a little care be easily identified. For instance it has the word " farthing " stamped upon it in clear letters and its edges are plain and uot milled like those of the half-sovereign.</p>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S MEN'S BIBLE CLASS, RUNCCRN. A. musical service in connection with this class was held on Sunday afternoon,and was largely attended. The President, Mr. Kniveton,occupied the chair, and he gave a Bible reading. S)los were sung by Mr. A. Shingler, Miss E. Alice Cooke, Mr. J. Leatliwood, and Mr. Wesley Cooke. A cornet solo was performed by Mr. Greenwood and a duet was contributed by the Misses Heatherington. Mr. William Cooke presided at the organ.</p>
        <p>THE PARISH CHURCH CHOIR. This choir gave an excellent rendering of the cantata "The Man of Sorrows" in the Parish Church on Wed- nesday evening, under the conductorship of Mr. James Littler. There was a large congregation, and the Rev. A. Spafford preached. The cantata is a most beautiful work and abounds with many telling passages. Mr. James Littler sang the tenor solos and Master Lane. the treble. Both acquitted themselves in a creditable manner. CHARGE OF STEALING BRASS AT RUNCORN. At Runcorn Folice Court,on Thursday morning,before Mr. Edwin Imison, three youths about seventeen years of age named Samuel Dut.on,Mason-street • John Gard- ner, Heath-street : and Peter Fernival,Rock'Mount,were charged with stealing a large quantity of brass belong- ing to machinery at the Old Quay Works of the United Alkali Companv.—On the application of Superintendent Okell the prisoners were remanded until Saturday: EXPEDITIOUS WORK AT RUNCORN DOCKS. There was an expeditious discharge of cargo in con- nection with the Bridgewater and Ship Canal Under- taking at Runcorn docka on Monday. The steamer Bishop Rock arrived during Sunday with a cargo of flints weighing 650 tons. Half an hour after midnight operations to unload were commenced, and at 10.30 the same morning they were completed. An hour later the vessel proceeded up the Ship Canal to be laden with coal, so that she arrived and departed within twelve hours. THE PRIZE CHOIR CONCERT. As previously intimated, the last concert of the season to be given by the memt -re of the Runcorn Tonic Solfa Prize Choir, will take place in the Public Hall. Runcorn, on Wednes evening, when Handers " Joshua " will to perforn:Fdl on a most adequate sc le. Tne reputation I of the choir is so well known in the town that it re- , qaires no recommendation here. In addition to a power- ful chorus, four special artistes have been engaged, namely. Madame Goodall, formerly a member of this choir, and winner of the Swinton Dolby Scholarship.and medallist Royal Academy of Muaic ; Mr. Tom Child (tenor), and Mr. A. S. Kinnel (bass). Further particu- lars will be found in our advertising columns. DEATH AND FUNERAL OF MISS HEWITT. On Wednesday afternoon, the grave closed over the remains of Miss Mary Jane Hewitt, daughter of the late Mr. John Hewitt, ship chandler, Percival-lane. Her death took place on Saturday morning at the age of 22 years, after a severe illness. Since her father's death she took an active part in the business, and her untim ly death has come as a great blow to the members of the family. By her kindly disposition she had endeared her- self to many frands. She was a regular attendant at St. Michael's ciaurch, and had engaged in Christian work. BETIIRSDA CHOIR.--SOCILL GATHERING. A delightful time was spent in Bethesda schoolroom on Wednesday evening by the members and friends of Bethesda Congregational choir, of which Mr. C. he Cou- teur is the conductor. The social gathering was for the benefit of those who took part in the rendering of the cantata "The Healing of Naaman." About forty get- sons sat down to tea and at the subsement proceedings, songs were interpreted by Miss E. Clare,Messrs. Gowan- I lock. Crossley, Chorlton, and the Rev. J. S. Simpson. Mr. J. Mack contributed a piccolo solo. The accompan- ist was Mr. C. Le Coutenr. The renuirlder of the even- ing was spent in games. SATinula EVENING CONCERTS. These concerts which are held under the auspices of the Women's Temperance Association, in the Trades' Hall, St. Rl' cet, have been, up to the present time, very successful, and on Saturday evening there was again a capital attendance. The programme provided was of a miscellaneous description, and the artistes ac- quitted themselves most creditably. The chair was oc- cupied by Sister Annie, of Weston. The first item was contributed by the Nels. `reet Subscription Band, and Mr. W. Cooper followed . t a song. The other vocal- ists were Misses Moore, Messrs. J. Holt, J. Moores and son, T. Shallcross, J. Broady. P. Green, and W. Price. Mr. J. Foulkes performed a pianoforte sole, and Mr. W. Hulse recited, During the evening 12tIntl and ceffer were supplied. For NAME 81100011113, soli silverd name brooches lot&amp; (any name) Government stamped. Presents or 1 Articles for yourself, you may visit DEBBETE, 10, ' CHURCH-STREET, LIVBRPOOL, where you can get hundreds of articles such as Watches, Clocks, Jewellery, Blectro-Plate, Cutlery. Purses, Pocket Knivei, etc., at wholesale prices. See advertisement (Illustrated Chron- graph Watch, 6s. lid.. etc.), on pne 7 f this Paper. •Adut VOLUNTEER SHOOTING CLUB, RUNCORN. This club's annual dinner was held at the George and Dragon Hotel on Thursday evening last, and full justice was done to the excellent repast provided by Mr. Shone. Captain G. Ashton presided at the subsequent proceed- ings, and amongst those present were Captain Thomp- son, Lieutenant Clarke, Lieutenant Prundret, Mr. A. Timmins. and Mr. W. Firth. The loyal and patriotic toasts having been honoured, the toast of the health of Colonel Ashton was most heartily drunk. The "Shoot- ing Club Committee" was proposed by Captain Thomp- son, and responded to by Sergeant J. W. Woodland I (secretary). In doing so,he remarked that the club had I not lost a single match for several years .The proceedings I were enlivened by contributions by a glee party under the leadership of Mr. Lancaster. DEATH OF Mae. Massa:. RUNCORN. Mrs. Mary Massey, who had attained the age of 751 years, died at her residence, Halton-road. Runcorn on Friday. She was the relict of the late Mr. Samuel Massey who was well-known in the neighbourhood. He was al landlord at Appleton Thornithence he went to the Crownl Hotel, North wich, afterwards to a hostelry at Nautwich, ' and ultimately came to the Lion Hotel, Greenway-road Runcorn, where he died. His widow carried on the busi- ness for some years, and subsequently took up her resid- ence with her daughter,Mrs. Robert Rigby, who held the licence of the London and North-Western Hotel, Low- lands-road, and remained there until her daughter's marriage with Mr. Jones, when she removed to Victoria- road, and thence to the house in which she expired. 'I he deceased was well know in the town and district. Her remains were interred in the Cemetery on Wednesday afternoon. CAMDEN WESLEYAN BAND OF HOPE. The members of this society gave an interesting en- tertainment on Monday evening, The proceedings were presided over by Mr. A. Harrison, and there was a good attendance of the public. The programme was of a varied character. Recitations were mtributed by Lucy Hinde, J. Santley, Walter Littler. r atrice Harrison, J. Britland, and songs by Nellie Temperlev, Martha A. Hoult, and J. liszley. Marion Holt performed a piano- forte solo. and Mr. J. Clarke gave a reading. An interest- ing feature of the evening's entertainment was the nurse maid's song, taken part in by six little girls dressed in nurse's attire. An encouraging address was delivered by Mr. S. Littler. During the evening the choir sang a number of glees, and five members took part in a dia- logue entitled" Patrick versus Michael." Altogether a most enjoyable time was spent. SEAMEN'S INSTITUTE, RUNCORN. The entertainment which was provided in this insti- tute on Tuesday night was arranged by Mr. George Orme. The first part of the programme consisted of a number of songs and recitations, and a short dialogue. The vocalists were Miss Nellie Douglas, Miss Ada Allen, Mr. George Orme and Mr. Joshua Nall. Miss Florence Brockley recited, and she also took part in a dialogue entitled "Our Dollies" in company with Miss Cassie Allen. An original dramatic sketch in three acts with the title "Condemned on Suspicion," was performed and comprised the second part of the evening's pro- gramme. The performers acquitted themselv.ei exceed- ingly well, and were as follows :—Misses N. Douglas, Ada Allen, Messrs. G. Orme. J. Nall and T. Wakefield. The dresses and rcenery were appropriate, and the per- formance was much enjoyed. Mr. Shaw (miesionarv) presided, and at the close he pronosed a vote of thanks to those who had taken part. Mr. Price seconded, and it was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTEICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>This Council met in the Boardroom at the Workhouse, Duttun, on Tuesday afternoon. Mr. J. Mclntyre, J.P., occupied the chair, and there were also present : Admiral Parry, Messrs. Joseph Knowles, J. Weir, J. B. Chadwick, W. Bradford, E. Aaron, T. Wright,J.L.Jones, NV. Holt, J. Whitlow, T. Baxter, C.C., J. Darbyshire, J. Brandreth.H. E Fleywood, J. M. Williams, S. Woodward, T. Darlington, T. Lewis, J. Stokes, J. Parr,C.Royla.nee, E. Bretherton, M. Mounfield, also Mr. Dansey (L. G. B. Inspector),Mr. J. Ashton (clerk),Mr. G. Ashton (deputy clerk), Dr. Adams (medical officer), Messrs. Diggle and Swinton (surveyors), and Mr. Farrington (inspector of nuisances).</p>
        <p>ROAD IMPROVEMENT AT WEST N. The tenders for the construction of a sewer ip South- road, Weston, were opened, and it was decided on the motion of Mr. Aaron, seconded by Mr. Baxter, that the contract of Messrs. T. Kirkham and Company, of Run- corn, for £B7 b. 3 accepted.—lt was further decided that a cheque be passed for the sum of £2O, the price aqrs-ed to be paid to Captain Clarke for gardens and palisad- inga in connection with the work.—Mr.Aaron in moving this, remarked that there was no road in the United Kingdom in such a bad condition. THE PROPOSED PORT SANITARY AUTHORITY. The Local Government Board intimated that they bad instructed their Inspector, Dr. F. W. Barry, to fir;ld an juquiry as to the constitution of a port Sanitary Authc- rity for the port of Manchester, at the Town Hall, Man- chester, on the 10th inst.—Mr. Chadwick moved, and Mr. Williams seconded, and it was agreed to. that the members of the Council's Committee should meet tbe members of the Runcorn Urban Council to consider what action to take in the matter. A CLERK'S COMPENSATION.</p>
        <p>The Lords' Commissioners now determined that the compensation payable to the Council to Mr. H. Roscoe, for loss of office as clerk to the East Bucklow Highway Board, was an annual sum of 13s. 63. from the date of the terminatioa of his appointment, as agreed to by the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOROUGH EXTENSION BILL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH EXTENSION BILL.</p>
        <p>Sr. PAUL'S WIPILEY GUILD, RUNCORN. There was a good attendance in Brunswick schoolroom on Wednesday evening on the occasion of a concert given by the Wesley Guild Choral Society with the object of raising funds to meet the expenses incurred in furnish- ing the Guild Parlour. The choir under the conductor- ship of Mr. J. Leathwood, sang several glees in a most praiseworthy manner, and solos were nicely interpreted be Miss may Clare, Miss Oliva Clare, Mr. Atherton, Mr. J. Birkettoild Mr. Frank Clare. The two first mentioned vocalists were deservedly encored. A duet" I've wan- dered in dreams " was also given by Miss Clare, and Mr. F. Clare. Instrumental selections were contributed by Mr. Frank Clare who performed a violin solo, and Mr. Greenwood who played a couple of cornet solos. Miss Ida Baker recited. The accompanists were Miss Jessie Crosby and Miss A. M.Taylor. A pleasant evening's en- tertainment concluded with the singing of" God ;save the Qu-en." TEE ELECTRIC SPARK MINSTRELS AT RUNCORN. With the object of rendering financi.l aid to the Run- corn Cycle Club. MI excellent entertainment was pro- vided in the Public Hall. Runcorn, on Monday evening by the Liverpool Post Office Electric Sr irk Minstrels. Thera was but a small attendance. The minstrels with their drollery and comic songs and sketches provoked the audience to laughter, and provided a most enjoyable evening's entertainment. The soloists were—Messrs. R. Laver, J. Wadsworth, McDonald. J. N. Lancaster, E. Fairey, G. W. Balfour, F. Mack, H. Nottingham, J. C. Evans and R. Hatton. The choruses were inwically sung, and the exhibition of " Hypnotism up to Date" was highly amusing. The stump oration by Professor Blackberry, and " An alleged musical sketch" by R. Flatton,wpre also interesting features of the second part of the programme. Mr. J. Rowlands was the conductor, Mr. .1. J. Gmilburn interlocutor, Mr. T. W. Newbould pianist, and Mr. J. J. Evans organist. The arrangements had been carried out by a committee of the Cycle Club, of which Mr. W. H. Mack was sectetary.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Lyon Vihittle, town clerk of Warrington,wrote respecting the petition of the London and 'North- Western Railway Company in opposition to the Bill for confirming the provisional order of the Loral Govern- ment Board to extend the borough. The object of the company, although not disclosed, is to obtain a la's ex- emption from the borough rate, a proposal wh eh the Lona Government Board woulel not entertain. They asked the Council to petition in favoiir of the Bill.and to render all the assistance in their power.—Mr. Darbyshire proposed that the memorial he on the table.—Mr. Williams thought by doing so they would be doing them • salves a back-hand; d slap. He proposed that they me- morialise Parliament in the manner requested. If the Bill were passed,it would be a great economical gain to the Runcorn Council.—Mr. Wright seconded, and Mr. Aaron supported. These was only one dissentient, Mr. Darbshire,</p>
        <p>SUTTON WATER suPer.V. A plan, report, and estimate for the Sutton water supply scheme was submitted by Messrs. E. Timmins and Sons, Limited, Runcorn. It showed that the tows- ship up to the present had been entirely without a reli- able water supply, the geological formation of the district having been proved and found unfavourable. The Council therefore proposed to trke a supply from the Vyrnwy water main of the Liverpool Corporation, which had agreed to supply the township at the rate of 9d. per 1,000 gallons. The total number of inhabitants requiring water was upwards of 500. The proposed pipe lines were named. Reference was also made to the sur- ply to Frodsbam Bridge mill and about twelve cottazes for the Frodshem water works. The estimated coat for the latter would be £9l 165.; and the former £1,173 9s. 6d.—Mr. Williams gave notice that at the next meet- ing he would move that the scheme be adopted, and in the meantime the Sutton Parish Council should consider the matter. BELSBY WATER SUPPLY AGAIN, AND OTHER MATTERS, Mr. Jeffs, chairman of the lielshy Parish Coeneil, wrote saying that the letter of Mr. Ashton NO been laid before this Council. after which the following. resolu- tion was adopted. " That the Parish Counsil regret that the District Council cannot see their way to add the Chairman or other councqlers of the Parish Council on any committees they may elect to deal with Holsby matters, which must colirern the ratepayers whom the Parish Council represent." Mr. Jeffs, of Fielsby, wrote. saying that the Parish Council after:considerine the letter of the District Council referring to ifelsby Water Supply decided to call their attentien to a resolution passed on October 2nd, 1895, namely "that this Conned is of opin- ion that the Helsby and District Water C enpany should have powers to lay water pipes on all the roads under the control of the District Council, saihjsct to such terms as are usual with water companies." As such resolution expressed the unanimous opinion of the repreeentativest of the ratepayers, the Parish Council did net think it necessary to put the parish to the expense of a rate- paeers' meeting with the almost certainty of a poll being demanded.—Messra. Potts, Potts and Gardner, drew i attention to newspaper reports of the proceedings of the I last meeting, and explained with regard to the quality lof the water the analysis taken had been pronounced first class. The Water Company were under the impres- sion that they had complied with all the conditions laid down by the District Council. They agreed to the prices to be charged for supplying cntt lee prop-rtv being settled by a Local Gevernment arbitrator, and furnished a scale of the charges they th-mselves proposed making. \ They reeretted if they had inadvertently over-looked any conditions laid down oy the District Council, and would endeavour to rectify it on hearing in what respect they were considered to be in default. There was no justification for describing the scheme as a half scheme. The company was formed after the Parish Council had decided not to take steps towards obtaining a public supply at the cost of the ratepayers. and they quite hoped to receive the support of the District Council.— Mr. Brandreth moved, Mr. Roylanee secohded, and it was agreed to,that the communication be referred to the committee considering the matter. MOORE PUMP. 0 Mr. Arthur de Prez, clerk to the Parochial Council of Moore,wrote drawing the attention of the District Cnun cil to the nubile pump of Moore, the water of which was quite impure :and useless, and a source nf danger to anyone using it. If it could be done away with altogether it would meet the views of the Council.—Mr. Farringtoe stated that the pump was useless and should he removed.—Mr Rovlance objected to that course being taken. stating that it was the gift of the Rev. George Heron, and had been very useful, and was so to-day, Moore was not well supplied with water, and within one hundred yards from the pump mentioned, there was six farm houses. They would be badly prepared with water in case of fire.— Mr. Jones said the pump was a great nuisance, and of no use at all. The farmhouses were supplied with water from the Warrington Corporation mains, and he did not think anyone but Mr. Roylance would oaject to the re- moval of the pump. The rev, gentleman who gave it told him that he would like it removed.—Mr. Wood- ward proposed that an analysis of the water be taken.— Mr. James seconded, and this was agreed to. THE ALLEGED NUISANCE AT FRODSHAM STATION. Mr. Iladfisld, of Isiyerpsol, in a letter. asked if an reply had been received from the London and North- Western and Great Western Joint Railways, with refer- ence to his complaint of the nuisance caused at Frodsham station by the discharge of bones, putrid meat-, rec., and the censequent damage and depreciation resulting to his property in Frodsham. He politely suggested that this was not a matter which brooked much more delay, and the great in) ry caused to mane interests through the nuisance complained of, needed a speedy remedy. The authority having the power to compel the railway authorities to remove the traffic to a siding elsewhere, should, he submitted, exercise that power. If not en- forced, he, with regret, should have to exercise legal pressure for the ()Tined to protect his property and stop a sanitary evil which was causing great loss .to health and property.—'1e Clerk reported that the matter was before the Council a month ago, and he was instructed to write the rail way company on the matter, lie did so, and in reply Mr. Tdasen stated that they were making ineeliries, hut these were net yet completed. fie, how- ever, hoped to be able to reply shortly. me matter was again censidered at the last tneetine of the Frodshain Parochial Committee, and they lead instructed him to write the company again on the inatter.-It, was decided that the Clerk should inform Mr. Ha.dileld what was taking place.</p>
        <p>THE HEALTH OF THE DISTRICT. Dr. Adams reported timt cases of scarh,t fever had occurred at Overton, three lt Frod.hlm, two at Keck- wick, one at Moore, and two st D.Aresbury. This was all the business of public interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>WESTON POINT.</head>
        <p>WESTON POINT.</p>
        <p>---- SINKING OF A FLAT.—On Tuo,sda.y afternoon. during the gale wh;ch prevailed, the fiat Energy, belonging to Mr. William Burton, of Sankey Hall, Warrington, and laden with manure, was sunk in the river just outside the Ship Gina' wall. She was endeavouring to enter the Weston Mersey Lock when she struck violently against thewall, and was so seriously damaged as to sink almost immediately. She was in charge of Captain Horton who was assisted by a mate. Fortunately both escaped. The vessel has since been raised.</p>
        <p>nip PROPOSED PORT SANITARY AUTHORITY. At Husc)rn Town Hall on l'hursday, a special meeting of the Health Committee was held with reference to the proppsecl inquiry at Manchester on Tuesday next.regard- ing the Order for the formation of a Port Sanitary Authority in connection with the Solo Canal. It was decided that a depptatiou cousiiting of Mescrs. Lightburn and WiswaU, should attend on behalf of the Runcorn Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>EXAMINER• SA.TURDAY, MARCH 7th, 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER• SA.TURDAY, MARCH 7th, 1896.</p>
        <p>R. CROSBY &amp; SONS Have now on sbow all Immense assortment of CABINET AND UPLIOLSTERY FURNITURE, All of the newest Styles, and best make. In their 22 Shovi rooms, contkiining 21,000 feet of spa.</p>
        <p>ist ALL OUlt GOODS ARE OF THE BEST MAKE. JACIALITY—C Art VED DARK- FURNITURE THE OLDEST PR A.CTICA.BLE I°IAKERS IL LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>63 AND 65, GREAT GEORGE-STREET.</p>
        <p>ESTABLISHED 19,27.</p>
        <p>R• 1/4._)110.8Y</p>
        <p>LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>FOR some little time past the The Watch Corn- relations between the Warring- mittee and the Magistrates. ton Watch Committee and the local magistrates have been somewhat strained, and this is largely owing to the fact that the members of the Watch Com- mittee have attempted to arrogate to themselves functions which are clearly outside their duties. , For many years it was the custom when the holder of any drink license was reported for a breach of the law, for the Chief Cctistable to lay the facts as alleged before the Magistrates' Clerk, ,and if it was considered that there was sufficient evidence to justify sending the case to the magi s- ' trates, that step WP.4 taken. Th's arrangernen has been altered by the Watch Committee, pro- bably to the advantage of the licensees and to the disadvantage of the order and good government of the town. The Watch Committcs now requires all the reports in question to be first bubmitted to them, and if, in their opinion, the cases are not bad enough to serld further, why, of course, they are not sent further. Considering the constitution of the Watch Committee, it may be reasonably assumed that when a case does get through it is a tolerably strong one. On the Bench itself a few years back the spirit of tehderness in dealing with this class of offence be- came so marked that, however glaring the case, anything more than a nominal fine was an utter impossibility, and such a penalty as endorsement of the license was unknown. The police were thereby discouraged, and the general conduct of the "Trade " was decidedly loose and more or less discreditable. Drunkenness was markedly preva- lent, and time after time regrets were publicly ex- pressed at the terrible amount of intoxication per- ceptible in the streets,more especially amongst the young or female portion of the population. In 1892, at a very full meeting of justices, who had been specially notified, a resolution was adopted, provoked by the action of the Watch Committee requesting the Chief Constable to notify to the whole body of magistrates in future any offence against the Licensing Acts. It must be borne in mind, however, that this notice only applies to such cases as have been passed on to the Bench after preliminary enquiry by the Watch Committee. The result of this action on the part of the Watch Committee has been that when a case has been betore them, and has been satisfactorily preyed, a conviction has followed and a penalty proportionate with the offence inflicted. But this is not all. The effect generally upon the conduct of the " Trade " has been clear and distinct. Licensed houses are to- day undoubtedly much better managed and the business transacted on more lawful lines than formerly, and the appearance of our streets amply confirms this fact. In spite of this improvement brought about without a trace of injustice, certain members of the Bench have never ceased to clam- our for the rescinding of the resolution just re- ferred to. Although they cannot point to a single case of unfairness even to those licensees who have been summoned and convicted they urge on every occasion with much more zeal than discretion that " people " are much annoyed that—what ?—why that people who have broken the law with their eyes open are getting treated somewhat similarly to other law breakers. The statement that this class of offetices is singled out for special treat- ment is not correct, as the notice is sent to all the justices alike. It could be urged with infinitely more fairness that the Watch Commit- tee do single out these cases fJr special treat- ment, and any body who has watched the pro- ceedings cf the Town Council for years past must have noticed how very careful the prevalent majority has been to prevent the committee from becoming unduly " leavened " with anything in the shape of a" teetotaler." Because the magis- trates inflicted a fine and costs upon a licensee a few weeks ago for selling drink to two people already intoxicated a majority of the Watch Committee requested the Mayor to call a special meeting of the magistrates for Monday last to " reconsider " the obnoxious resolution. The meeting was held, and, it is said, a warm discus- sion took place, which was remarkable perhaps for the voting which is always a test of prin- ciple and for the unreserved advocacy of the interests of the Trade which was indulged in. A majority of the magistrates present su ilapdpporrotevdedthoie grreesl aotusteiornvi, oceninthpe groundsrocuringa tmhaartkeidt improvement in the management of licensed property without inflicting a shadow of injustice upon anybody. And so for the present the matter remains.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>This Council met at the Town Hall, Rumorn, on Thursday morning. Mr. E. lmison, J.P., occupied the chair, and there were also present Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Messrs. S. Taylor, J.P. C.C., J. Pritchard,F. Wiswall.H. J. Grice, J. Jones, G. 'H. Kirkham, J. Wilson, J. W. Lightburn, W. Collier. T. Dean, M. Sweetman, D. Bis- brown, It. H. Posnett, and Mr. F. A. Lake (law clerk), Mr. E. Marshall (secretary). Dr. McDoneall (medical officer), and J. Wilding (surveyor). HEALTH REPORT.—" EMINENTLY SATISFACTORY." Dr. McDougall in his monthly report stated that the number of deaths registered amounted to twenty-two. Two were inquest cases and of the remaining twenty, two were due to mesenterie disease, three to heart di:- ease, two to senile decay, tour to convulsions three to bronchitis, two to inflammation of the lining membrane of the brain, and one each to jiundice, spinal , disease, scarlatina and phthisis. The twenty-two deaths showed an average death rate for the twelve months of 13.08, and exclusive of the inquest cases of 12 06 of thc estimated population. Fifty-one births were r gistered during the month, and fifty for the month of January. For the corresponding month of last year. twenty deaths and sixty births were regist Gastaldi : The report is very satisfactory.—Mr. Light- burn: Eminently satisfactory. HIGHWAYS AND BUILDING'' COMMITTEE. This committee recommended that a bracket lamp be fixed outside the Post Office at Savage's Bridge, the gas to be provided by the Port Office department ; that the plan of Mr. T. J. Kirkham for the erection of six houses in Dalc-street be approved ; that the plan of Messrs. J. Sefton and T. Parry for the erection of two houses in Greenway-road be approved.—These were adepted on the motion of Mr. Taylor, seconded by Mr. H. J.Grice. HEALTH COMMITTEE. It was recommended by this committee that plans be passed for sanitary improvements at the" Boat Rouse" Inn, for Mes.,rs. Gieenall, Whitley and Co., and at 101, 103, 105, and 107, Halton-road, for Mr. John Littler; also that the offer of Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co. to let the stables and yard at the " Boat house" lon at a rental of ten guineas per annum, the tenancy to be a yearly one.with the right cf the ROTICOTH Urban Dittrict Council, to erect sheds (at an estimated cost of £4O) be accepted, subject to the company agreeing to the Coun- cil taking down the sheds at any time and to the sheds being the property of the Council.—Adopted on the motion of Mr..Wiswall, seconded by Mr. R. H. Posnett. RUNCORN PUMPING STATION. The Water Committee recommended that an insurance of £2,000 be placed on the two boilers_at the Runcorn Pumping Station to be available to cover any damage which tnay be caused from explosion of either or both for the premium of £8 49. 3d. instead of the present in- surance of £l,OOO on each boiler for the premium of £b. —Mr. Pritchard moved the confirmation of this minute. Mr. Wilson seconded, and is WAS agreed to. THE CEMETERY. It was recommended by the Cemetery Committee that a precept be served on the overseers of the poor for £lBO for the six months ending Michaelmns Day next; and that 80 tons of limestone chippings be obtained for the cemetery walks (tenders to be submitted).—Mr. Grice, in moving that the minutes be approved, stated that he thought it was time the footpaths at the cemetery were put in decent order. They were getting in very bad condition.—ln answer to Mr. Bisbrown, the Secretary explained with regard to the precept that £6O was towards the reduc- tion of a debt, £6O for expenses, and the remainder for the chippings, repairs, &amp;O.—The minutes were approved.</p>
        <p>THE TECRNICAL INSTITUTE. The only minute of the Technical Instruetion Commit- tee was the payment of an amount of £1 134. 61. for geometrical models for the art class, to Mr. G. Cussons, Manchester.—Mswall in moving its adoption,stated that the expenditure was not much, but it was required to complete a set of models and meet the requirements lof the teachers. The models they had in their posses- sion were not of the right size.—Mr. Wilson seconded, and the recommendation was agreed to. APPOINTMENT OF OVERSEERS. The Law Clerk read an order from the Local Govern- ment Board conferring on the Urban District Council the power to appoint overseers,remarking that that was one of the Parish Council powers for which they had applied It did not mean that ti,e others asked for would not fol- low.—Mr. Taylor asked if the appointment of overseers would not he made this month. Would it require a special meeting of the Council ?—The Law Clerk said it should be made during March, and he expected there would be a special meeting shortly. TEE FORTHCOMING ELECTION OF COUNCILLORS. A further communication was read from the Local Government Board by the Law Clerk prescribing rules as to nomination and election of Urban District Councillors in 1896. It stated that notices of election should be published on the 10th inst. The election should take place between the 28.... h March, and the Ist of April, but the actual date depended upon the County Council. They wonld be in possession af their decision in the course of a few days, and it would be stated on the posters. All the other formalities were the same as last year.-- THE SUGGESTED GRAVING DOM AT WESTON POINT. The Chairman read a communication from Mr. Ernest Inman, secretary of the Weaver Navigation, which he staled would be received with regret by all the members. It stated that the suggested graving dock at Weston Point had received their attention, but the Trustees did not think the project could be entertained, as the pro- posals were impracticable. The Council then rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>BY OUR RUNCORN CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>The decision of the farliamentary Committee of the Runcorn Urban District Council not to oppose the Bill of the House of Commons will come as a surprise to many people in the town. Considering the paramount importance of the Bill and the pro- bable affects upon Runcorn, if passed, it cannot he said that our local authority is to be . congratulated upon its course of action. Many will fail to see how the Ship Canal Company have deserved this leniency. Tbe company have received freely at the hands of RIIIICOrn, but it cannot be said that the company have freely given, and the question is asked how long is this lop-sided arrangement to continue? One of the latest examples of the Ship Canal Company's thoughtfulness for the interests of Runcorn is the reduction of the staff at the Sprinch works. -</p>
        <p>Instead.of prompt opposition to the scheme o the company we are told that the Parliamentary Committee are zealously continuing their in- vestigations, and if it is found that there are sufficient grounds for oppposing the Bill, it. will be petitioned against in the House , of Lords. These thorough and searching in- ' vestigations may be considered necessary, but there is just a question whether they will not be too late. One result of the laborious investigation, I am told and of the careful bottling and sending away to the analyst samples ef canal water is that the members of the Council are now assured that the water is "unfit for domestic purposes!" Well really, if this so, one would have thought that the mental faculties of the worthy councillors would not have been very much exercised in arriving at that decision, without calling in professional assist- ance, and it is to be hoped that the continued inves- . , tigations may result in something more substantial P 1 upon which to base their decision. --</p>
        <p>It is questionable whether the dilatoriness and supineness of the members of the Council in tack- ling the Ship Canal Company will be to the benefit of Runcorn,either from a financial or sanitary point of view. The Runcorn Rural Council, the Warring- ton and Widnes Corporations, private landowiliers and others are at least preserving their locus stancli on the question, but poor Runcorn is to be left out in the cold as without an opinion on the matter— at least, so far as the House of Commons is con- cerned. The advocates for the Company may quote us as not 13,,ing adverse to the bill; that Runcorn, the moat affected of all others, does not care much whether the tidal openings which admit fresh water into the canal, are opened or closed. And what, may be asked, may Runcorn expect from the Com- pany in return ?? ?-000. ---</p>
        <p>queAion.</p>
        <p>Runcorn may be considered to have many draw- backs, but it must be admitted that it at least has tbe arlyantave of a very low death rate. At the eeting of the Council yeqerday. Dr. McDougali'A e port was eminently satisfactory, the death ra e</p>
