        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>CHILD NEGLECT BY A DRUNKEN FATHER. Yesterday, before Alderman Harrison (chairman), Councillor Roberts, and Mr. S. Wright, at the Warring- ' ton Borough Court, Henry Howard, 335, Winwick-road, a widower, was charged with having on the 21st February neglected his four children, Joseph, Thomas, Harry, and Richard, in a manner likely to cause them suffering.—lnspector McAteer, N.S.P.C.C., prosecuted, and said the ages of the children named were respec- tively 12, 10, 8, 3. On the Bth of January he visited defendant's house and found the children in the back kitchen all alone. They were insufficiently clothed and in a filthy condition and almost swarming with vermin. Defendant was absent and the house was very badly furnished, while the bed he and the 6hildren slept in was also filthy. The house was dirty from top to bottom and the nell abominable. On the 21st inst. he again called and found the children alone and in the same condition, as was also the home. There was no improvement, notwithstanding that it was almost two months since his first visit. The defend- ant worked at Whitecross, and was in receipt of about 30s. a week and he did nothing from ,Saturday night until Monday morning but drink his wages along with other men leaving his children almost starving. The Inspector asked their worships to inflict a substantial penalty upon defendant. It would be a God-send for the children to go to the workhouse.—P.S. Cheney said he visited the defendant on the 7th Janu- ary and found the house as described. Defendant said be could not alter the case.—Mrs. Lightfoot, Mrs. Kinsley Mrs. Hall (neighbours), and Henry Howard, son 01 de- fendant,all spoke to the latter's drunkenness and neglect of the children.—Defendant affinitted that the children bad been neglected but added that he had been a month out of work from naffs at Christmas.—fle was sent to gaol for two months with hard labour.</p>
        <p>MINOR CASES. - WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COTIRT. FRlDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (chairman). Councillor R.berts. and Mr. S. Weight, NEGLECT or Wonk.—Mary Lee Portland-street, was charged with leaving her work under James Welsby. fut,lan cutter, on the 20th February and putting him to ithe loss of Sc. —Defend'lnt, who is 18 years of age, said she had left to get married and was now a wife.—She had two years appre. ticeship to serve and was ordered to return to her work and pay the money and costs at 6d. a week. gTRIXING A WOMAN. -Jahn Cheedie, 16 Dallam-lane, was charged with assaulting and beating Matilda Brickley Mellor On the 20th of February. Mrs. Mellor said that on the day in question she had some words with defendant and he struck her with his fist on the ear and "made it hum " Jane Clatke, 14 yeare of age. corroborated, and defendant who expressed his contrition, was fined 10s. and costs. StONDAY.—Bgere the Mayor (chairman). Mr. F. Monks. „ Alderman Francomb, Mr. J. W. Hayward, and Mr. T Birchall. Am:arum 'MARKET TRI:TT.—Vary Jana Owen, a married woman, living in Dallam-lane, was charged with stealing from. the Market Place on Saturday night a pair of hoots the property of Joseph Frisby,—She pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable stated the circumstances of the case which showed that the woman was suspected and watched. Ony,anel Saturday night she was seen to approach the stall of Joseph prib after looking furtively round, to take a pair of boots and put them under her shawl. Isaac Bowers. assistant at the stall, followed her and took the boots from her, and she then escaped. Information was given to the rx)lice,and detectives Soinks and Monaghan went to defendant's hence at mid- night and found her under the Influence of drink on the sofa. She became very excited and attempted to drink the contents of a bottle containing a poisonous drug but the officers threw it in the fire. She said she would make away with herself and seized hold of a paraffin lamp. sad swung I t round. She was again overpowered without damage being done. At the police station she had to be medically attended and a woman had to sit with her all night. This was her 6th appearance for various offences and there was something more than a suspicion thtt she had systematically committed thefts from the market. Defendant was not in want as her husband was working and she bad no children. -The 'magis- trates committed her to prison for one month's hard labour. BREACH OF THE PEACE—Bridget Costello, 20, Hill-street and Mary Alice Healey, 18. Hill-street, were bound over in El each to keen the peach for 6 months and orcbred to pay Sc'. and costs, for fighting on the 24th February.—P.C. F. Cooper proved the case. ANOTHER DIRTY BAKKHOUSE.--.7oini Henry Brown, 57.1 Stsott-street was charged with having on the 25th February allowed a bakehouse in Itils occupation to be in an unfit state for its use.—lnspe^tor Norman'said he /rid visited the defend- ant's bakehouse and found it dirty and filthy, both floors and walls. There was half an inch of dirt on thefloer and defend- ant had been warned a month previously.—A. fine of 21s. and costs was imposed. INTYP'ECTIVE LABELLING OF MARGARINE.—VRTV Miles, 33, Priestley-street, was charged with having on the 24th Febru- ary unlawfully exposed for sale in her shop n crantity margarine without its being labelled with letters the requisite size.—lnspector Norman sale in this case the letters were on the paper the margarine was wrapped in t square but there should have been also a board labelled with letters It Inches square. Defendant pleaded ignorance to him; and brought the board from another part of the chop. There was no fraudulent intent.—The bench ordered defendant to pay 3s. 6d. costs. TURSDAY.—Before Mr. IL _thrnett (in the chair) and Dr.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Smith</head>
        <p>Smith</p>
        <p>A BONFIRE IN A POLICE Cva.r..—A rough-looking elderly man named Peter Smith. of no fixed abode, was charged with 'having been drunk and disorderly in Sankey-street on Monday night. He was also charged with assaulting p.o. Plumb at the same bin-la.—P.C. Plumb stated that at about seven o'clock he saw prisoner acting in a very disorderly manner, and when be was requested to go away he refused and kicked witness on the legs several times. He then took him into custody, when prisoner became very violent. Assist- ance had to he procured, and Smith was carried to the Police Station.—lnsp ctor Taylor stated that he assisted to take priscaser to the Police Station. and he corroberatea the evid- ,ence as to the assault on the constable.—The Chief Constable said that he saw rrisoner on the previous night about six o'clock, enter a kublic house, where he was refuses drink- He then entered as 4 omnibus and rode into the centre of the town. when he asked for Rose and Crown-street. He was directed there, and the next that he (the Chief Constable) saw of him was when he was brought to the bridewell ma ri drunk. By some means prisoner managed to smuggle a match into the cell, and taking off all his clothing he placed it in a heap and set fire to it. He was, however, taken by surprise by the man who looked after the cells, or the whole brideweil might have been on fire and the prisoner might have lost his life. As it was only his shirt was burned.—Prisoner (who is deaf) on being toldby Inspector &amp;finks what the Chief Constable bad state 2.ranlied "Yes, I did it because I wai cold, and almost stary "—The Chief Constable remarked that the cells were warmer ban the Court was at that moment. -Prisoner was sent to Var't for one month with hard labour. DKENN RB9. w illiam Bayliss. a plasterer, was dis- charged with a caution for being drunk outside the police station. Irlam-street. on Monday afternoon. CRUELTY TO A HOEBE.—Abram Jones. of Bryn. near Wigan was charged with having cruelly ill-treated a horse by travel- ling it whilst in an unfit state on the night previous.—ln- spector Bilson. of the R.S.P.C.A.. stated that at about eleven o'clock he was in Frogball-lano when he noticed defendant in charge of a horse which was vety old and emaciated. It was lame in every limb, and on its back was a wound some eight inches long and seven incises wide. It could scarcely walk, and on being taken to the Eagle and Child yard it fell to the ground. Defendant was drunk. and scarcely knew what he was saying when questioned by Witness.—Asked by the Bench as to whether he would have the animal destroyed de- fendant replied, "No, I yrouid rather not. It belongs to my</p>
        <p>_ A MINUTE WITH YOU PLEASE Sufferers from Gr,vel. Lumbago Piles, Pains in the Bao Dropsy, Wind and Water Complaint. Diseasss of Kidneyll Bladder Stone. Sciatica. Rheumatism and Gout will find g positive Cure in Holdroyd's Gravel Pills. Try a small box ana if not satisfied your money will be returned. Pries Is. 1.0 Of all Chemists. or post free for 12 stamps. from HOLDRO7D' Medical Nall, Checkhe.aten, Yorks. Don't be put oft If you cannot get them writopi,the Proprietor: and', a box will be sestt mxt post.--Aclvt,;</p>
        <p>wife, and she thinks the w )rld of it, because it was given to her by a squire from the south of England." (Laughter.)-- A fine of 10s. and costs,or in default fourteen days' imprison- tnent was imposed. - WE.DNEiDAY.—Before Mr. John Fairclough and Mr. J. Geddes.</p>
        <p>NEGLECT OF FAMILY.—SEVERE PENALTY.—Patrick Dooley Was charged with having on the 2nd December, 1893, un- lawfully ran a•way trona the borough of Warrinton, thereby leaving his wife and child chargeable to the Warrington Union.—Mr. Shearer prosecuted, and said that it WAS tic- fendant's fifth appear Anne since 1880 for similar offences. He was a labourer, and had been brought from Hanley. Almost since 1880 off and on hls family had been chargeable. He had twice had three months' imprisonment and several smaller terms.—The magistrates committel defendant for another three months.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY PE 1Y SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Berore Captain Withington and Mr. S. B.</p>
        <p>Armitage. INTEMPERINCE.—Joseph Birchall, George one and Peter Hayes were fined the first 10s. and costs and the latter two 58. and costs each for being guilty of dpnikennes sand dis- orderly conduct in Poulton and Croft respectively. For the same offence James Scarisbnck was sent to gaol for 14 dava in default of paying 108 and costs ; Charles Miller, Sankey, was fined ss.</p>
        <p>DOG OWNERS FINED.--john Andrews was charged with allowing a dog to be at large oe the 18th February in Rixton. The casa was proved and the defendant fined Ts 6d. inclu iing costs. For keeping dogs without licenses the following per- sons were fined 7s. 6d. and costs: John Boscovr, Penketh ; George Davies, Burtonwood ; Maud Johnson. Penketh ; Joseph Natreas. Sankey ; and Jame 3 Collier, Sankey.</p>
        <p>- , LiW•BREAKING CYCLISTS.—For riding their bieyele3 on the footpath in various townships the following persons were fined ls and costs Arthur Oxley, William l'eover and James Whitehead.</p>
        <p>WORKING 1 LAME HORSE.—George Cordingley. a boy, was charged within-treating a horse by working the same while in an unfit state. Sergeant Barnes stated the ewe, and said the horse defendant was driving had a wound in the off hind fetlock • Defendant admitted the lameness of the horse, but said he did not know he was doing any harm by working it. -A fine of 28. 6d. and costs was imposed. ASSAULTS.—Chirlotte Hatfield. an elderly woman was chargedwith assaulting Mary Green on the 28th Feb.—Mrs. Green,whose evidence was supported by that of hcr husband, asserted that defendant had struck her in the eye making it black, as a result of some disagreernent.—Defendant was bound over in her own reeognisances to keep the peace flr 3 months.—Niary Darby and Elizabeth) Bailey were charged with assaulting Harriet Bailmy on the 23rd February.—Mrs. Palley said that onelof the defendants hit her in the eye and the other struck her on the temple several times.—They were bound over as in the first case.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED THEFT.—John- liiegheock, a respectable looking man, was charged with having on the 22nd November, in Burtonwood, stolen one machine drill the property of Henry Junes. and valued at 2s. 6d.—Mr Forshaw appeared for the complainant and Mr. Garner, St. Helens, for defendant. Mr. Forshaw said the defendant and complainant were contrac- tors. carrying on business at Collins Green Colliery. On the 20th November last conlplainant missed a file and maliing search for it found it was in the possession of defendant. He brought the matter before the manager, who said there was no doubt that the drill belong,d to complainant as it had his Private mark upon it.—Mr. Garner at this point handed Mr. Forshaw a letter which, on reading, he said showed him that he had beers wrongly instructed and he begged to withdraw from the prosecutimi.—Mr. Garner applied for the costs of his witnesses and these were granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>NEWTON PErIT SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>NEWTON PErIT SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>S URDAT.—Before Mr. T. J. Gillespie (chairman). and Mr</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>C. B. F. Borron</head>
        <p>C. B. F. Borron</p>
        <p>AN ALLEGED IMPATIENT RAILWAY PASSENGER.—Sophia Jakeman, a married woman who resides at St. Aldeate, Ox- ford, was summoned on the charge that on Janinary 6th, being a passenger travelling in a train of the London and North-Western Railway, she le:t the carriage while the train was in motion. Mr. Fennaprosecut ed ou behalf of the Com- pany, and Mr. J. W.Knowles defended. The tette- explained that Mrs.Jakeman did not appe r as the distance from Oxford to Earlestown was such a great one. Mr. Fenna sail that the penalty for the offence with whieh the defendant was charged was a fine not exceeding 40s. The circumstances were that she was travelling from Warrington to Earlestown by the train which left the former place at 5.25 p.m. on January 6th to see sorne friends in Earlestown. She was seen at Warring- ton in the third vehicle from the engine and seated with her back to the engine. She had a band ige over one eye, and she was accompanied by a little girl, her daugh'er. On arriving at Earlestown she got out of the train while it was in motion. She was rreviously standing at the door, and then appeared to have jumped out, and she fell on the side of her face. Be- yond a few bruise 3, she was none the worse for the tell.- The Company wished to put a stop to the practice of get log out of trains while they were in motion, but they were very often unable to get independent witnesses. On the present occasion however they had a boy who had no connection whatever with the company.—Thomas Robert Openshaw the boy in question was then called and said that he resided at Golborne. On January 6th he was waiting for the train from Warrington to Earlestown. and as the train came into the station a compartment was opened and a woman stood at the doorway. The next thing he saw was some men picking up the oefendant who had fallen to the ground.—Cross- examined by Mr. Knowles : He did not see the defendant tall out. The station was badly lighted, and there were only two lamps on that side of the piatforra. The defendant fell near to one of the lamps. There was only one porter on the station and he was collecting tiekete.--William Lornm, the guard of the trainin which defendant had travelled said that the train came into the station at a good walking pac about six miles an hour. The signal was against the train just before entering the station but it fell, and the driver then took off the brakes and went a little further, but the train was never stopped. The defendant was picked up a carriage length behind the compartment from which she fell, end the little girl who was with her, said that she had got oat before the train stopped and she fell. The train was delayed five minutes in consequence of the accident. William Nunnerly, driver, and William Street.the fireman, gave similar evidence —Harry Singleton, the porter at Earlestowe station, said that the bye-laws were posted at the station. The train came in at a good walking pace.—Mr. Knowles: What is a good walking pace. Six miles an hour P—Witness : lc ould walk ten miles an hour if I tried. (Lod laughter.)—Mr. Knowles: I will ask you no more after th st.e-James Whittaker gave evidence to the effect that the bye-taws of the Company were posted on Wsrringtor. station.—Mr. Knowles, on beha!f of the defendant, said that he could not 15,95 why so much was made about a trifle. His case was that, the train s opped and then started again to go a little Wither along the platform, and would call the daughter of the defendant to prove that. He contended that the case was brought to that court, to prevent ah action being brought against the Company. and alleged that this was proved by the fact that th 3 man 'ger had written to Mrs. Jakeinan asking for an apology, and as he did not get that, the present case had resulted.—Jane Jake- man, 10 years of age, said that she had come with her mother from Oxford. and when they got to Earlestown, the traits stopped and start d again. Her mother tried to open the door, and pressed her knee against it. This cauPed the door to fly open and she fell out. She WAS picked up only a few feet from the door of the conspartment.—After a rather lengthy consideration of the case, Mr. Gillespie said that the beech were of the opinion that the defendant had got out before the train stopped. bat the station was exceedingly badly lighted, and that might have led to the accident. They would therefore only fine the defendant in the costs (161.) DRUNKENNESS.—For this offence, Patrick Tavlin. Newton, was fined 2s. 6d. ; Hannah Milling, Earlestown 55. ; Wilijitin Ronayne, Newton. 20s. and costs. BREACK OF TIER PEACE.—James Eng'ish, and Charles Massey, aarlestovrn, were each bound over to keep the peace for three months for fighting. UNLICENSED Doos. —For having dogs without licenses, the following persons were fined: George Themes, P ter Wilson, Ana pickles, and Alfred W. Mitchell, all of Earlestown, were fined 103. A CROFT CASE SETTLED—Thomas Fairhurst, Was charged that between January 21st, and January 2 th, being the ISecretary of the Croft Bailed Society. he failed to produce for Inspection to certain members of the Society, the books and accounts of the scciety. Mr. Bryan of atindley prosecuted and Mr. J. W. Knowles defended.—Mr, Bryan said that he could make an off e• which if agreed to by the defendant could put an end to the case If the defendant wauld con- sent ta produce the books, to the complainants who were en- titled to inspect thems on any date, be would withdrew the case. The defendant hereupon agreed to produce them, and the case was adjourned for a fortnight to give him ac oppor- tunity of doing so.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY BF-98TONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY BF-98TONS.</p>
        <p>Nnia.Y.—Before Sir Richard Brooke (chairman), and Mr. James Handley. _</p>
        <p>LICENSE TRANSFERS.—The following transfers of licenses were granted. no objections being raised :—Castle Hotel. Halton. from Ellen Simpson to Joseph.' Walker; Railway Hotel, Lowland's-road, from Henry Darlington to James Chambers Wylie; George Inn, Poole-lane, from Joseph Baker to James Hughes Percival; Duke of Wellington, Percl- val-lane, from Joseph Smith to William' Johnson; Nelson Inn, High-street, from Henry Arnold to Samuel Houghton; Anchor Inn, Brunswick-street. from James Mach to Henry Arnold; He Lion. Halton, from George Leach to John Leach; Ferry Hotel, Church-street, from James Hughes Percival to David Millington; Travellers' Rest. Runcorn Hill, from William Johnson to Joseph Nield ; Spring Vaults, Ellesmere-street, from Samuel Garrett to Henry Davies. DOG OWNERS It TROUBLE AGAIN I—For allowing their dogs to be at large without muzzle or the collar sufficiently stamped John Willinge, 28 Porter-street, Runcorn ; and William Twigg, Brick Kiln Houses, a boatman, were each fined ss. including costs. IRREGULAR SCHOOL ATTENDANCE.—Fines of 6d. and 4c6d, costs were imposed on the following defeniants for neglecting to eause their children to attend school regularly :-11.31811 Rose, Duke-stre ; John Bazley, Mill-brow ; William Clarke. Surrey-street ; James Jackson, Robert street ; and Henry Kennedy, Granville-street. Mr. T. Bowyer, school visitor, prosecuted.</p>
        <p>The Sweetmeat Automatic Supply Co.,having d scontinued to use CADBURY'S CHOCOLATES in their automatics machines, those who still wish to procure CADBURY'S CHOCOLATES will be able to da so from most of the refreshment rooms at the railway stations, and from confectioners.—Advt. Terrible weather prevailed in the Bristol Channel on Wednesday niv ht. The revenue cutter Amelia, with the cJastguard families for Appledore aboard, put into Swansea on Thursday with machinery disabled. TOBACCONISTS commencing ; hid. Guide and Catalogue (259 pages) 3d. TOBACCONLiTS' OUTFITYLNG Co.-188, Ruston road, London. (Mgr ) HY. MEYERS.</p>
        <p>Patrick M'Gratb, a farmer, of Abrahan Causeway, near Tralee, was shot in the shoulder with a bullet near Tralee, on Wednesday night. It is alleged that he had taken a farm from which his sister had been evicted. The Ramsgate smack Ethel collided on Tuesday off Southwold with a Flemish trawler, name unknown. The trawler sank with, it is believed, all on board.</p>
        <p>BIRDS, ANIMALS and FISH STUFFED, and mounted in Glass Cases of any design. HEADS and HORNS mounted, al.o for Sale. Birds made into Hand Screens or Fire Screens. Dealer in British and Foreign Birds and Animals Skins, Glass Shades, Stags' Heads. Cabinets store Boxes. Nets, &amp;c.' for Butterfly and ligg Collectors.—B. COoKE, Naturalist and Furrier, 21, Renshaw-street, Liverpool. Bstaislished over 21 years. Telegrams from Amsterdam say that a fire broke oul on Wednesday at Asperan, a small town in the province of South Holland. The flames,fanned by a strong wind, spread over a wide area, and the fire is still raging. Up to the time of the report a church and forty houses had been destroyed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 7, 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 7, 1896.</p>
        <p>THE WONDER OF THE PLACE.</p>
        <p>A REMARKABLE piece of news Was brought in last week by the reporter of a local paper from Blackheath, a suburb in London. He had been deputed to interview Mrs. Bonnick, 5, Couthurst-road, Charlton-road, Black- heath, London, S.E. Mrs. Bonnick showed him her baby,</p>
        <p>I A STOTIT,HEALTHY LITTLE GIRL, under three years of age with a sunny smile brightening the whole, of her features. " Ah, but you should have seen her this time last year," said the mother, "you wouldoot have thought that she would have lived a day," and then she told me the following story :</p>
        <p>"Her name is Grace Ethel Bonnick. She was born on the 24th May, 1893. Till last winter she was a bright, healthy child. She was then taken ill with influenza. followed by plurisy and pneumonii. The doctor ordered beef tea, chemical food, and cod liver oil, but she was too weak to take them. She wasted to a mere shadow, and about the end of May dropsy jiet in. The doctor gave up all hope,and only called occasionally to see her. I had, myself, lost hope. when one day last June I read in a small book about Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People,how a child on the verge of death had been com- pletely cured by the use of the ,Pills, and as a last re- source Irdetermined to try them. I gave her the third of a pill dissolved in water, three times _a day, and the very first night</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>SHE SLEPT BETTER</head>
        <p>SHE SLEPT BETTER</p>
        <p>than she had done for weeks. She used scarcely to sleep at all. On the second and third nights she still slept better and much more peacefully. On the third day her appetite commenced to improve, and she asked for food. At the end of a fortnight she was able to sit up in her chair. She took two boxes altogether in two months, and at the end of that time was as well as she had ever been. She had been running about long befure that."</p>
        <p>"Has she had any relapse since then ?" "Na, nor any ailment of any kind. The people of the neighbourhood thought she could not last many days, and now they think she is a perfect wonder. She is simply the idol of the street, and all the neighbours spoil her." FlRsing pressed by me as to whether there might not be some ether cause for the child's remarkable recovery, the mother continued "No, nothing else would persuade me that it was anything but Dr. Williams' Pills," and then. she pro- ceeded to give the following logical reasoning for her belief : "I had given her nothing for a fortnight be- , fore starting with them, and seeing that the doctor bad given up all 'hope, and knowing myself bow ill she was, I do not see how great a change could bave come about of itself."</p>
        <p>"You must feel rather thankful that that book was left at your door," I said on leaving. " Oh, yes," replied Mrs. Bonnick, "I can never be too grateful. We</p>
        <p>ALL THOUGHT SHE WAS DYING,</p>
        <p>but she is quite cured now." Dr. Williams' Pink Pills are praised amongst the highest and lowest in the land, as a strengthening and tonic .medicine, whether for men women, or children. They are not like other medicine, nor can they be imitated, as is sometimes dishonestly pretended ; take care that the package bears the full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and in case of doubt send direct to Dr.Williams' Medicine Com- pany, 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, E.C., as the Pills can be had post free at 2s. 61., or six boxes for 13s, 9d. They have cured numerous cases of influenza, anemia, palpitation, and the disorders which arise from im- poverished blood, such as muscular weakness, loss of appetite, shortness of breath, pains in the back, nervous headache, early decay, all forms of female weakness, hysteria, paralysis, locomotor ataxy, rheumazism, and sciatica.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>CHIEF CONSTABLE'S ANNUAL REPORT.</head>
        <p>CHIEF CONSTABLE'S ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>The annual report of the Chief Constable of Warring- tan for 1895 (which is,of course, the first report prepared by Mr. Talbot), has been presented to the Watch Com- mittee and has been printed fOr..pirculation amongst the members of the Town Council. The report contains the , followirr, particulars :—The number of licensed houses 1 in the borough is as follows :—Licensed victuallers, 87 ; beerhouse keepers, 45 ; shopkeepers, 74 ; Music licences. 38. Transfers have been made in the cases of 6 licensed victuallers, 3 beerhouse keepers.and 7 shopkeepers (off). The number of children detained in Reformatory and Industrial Schools during the rear is 44, for whose nart maintenance the sum of £64 3s. 93. has been received by the Police and forwarded to the Government Inspec- tor of Reforma`nories. Eighty-seven pedlars' and six sweeps' certificates have been granted,thefees for which amounted to £22 10s., which is payable to the Police Superannuation fund. There are 11 cab and omnibus owners in the Borough and 70 drivers plying for hire, Eight marine store dealers, and 40 gunpowder and fire work dealers, are licensed for those businesses. Forty- four persons have been summoned for keeping dogs without licence; 20 being convicted, 23 being discharged, ' and 1 case was withdrawn. 422 dogs .were found at large unmuzzled ; 255 were given up to owners, 156 destroyed, 5 escaped, and 6 were sold. The sum of £27 18s. was received it fees. One hundred. and three sudden, unnatural, and violent deaths were reported to the Coroner through the police ; inquests were held in 45 cases. 55 were referred to the Registrar, and 3 were cer- tified by doctors, The verdicts, where i nquests were held, were 24 "accidental," 9 "natural causes," 3 "open," 1 "misadventure," and 8 "suicide during temporary insanity." Seventy-five lost children were found and restored to their parents ' • 70 horses, cattle and sheep found straying were penfolded,and afterwards owned. Three hundred and sixty-six shops.warehouies. and private houses were found insecure by the police on night day, by reason of doors and windows being left unfastened. Six fires took plane in the borough, the probable danaage amounting to £15,890, mostly covered by insurance. The latter figures include the damage done by the serious fire in May at Messrs. Skelton's premises in Anrsemarket-street. The gross total cost of the police establishment is £5,279 2s. ld., half of which was paid by the Lancashire County Council. Total cost in 1894, £5.076 3s. ed. The Police Superannuation Fund amounts to £10,338 75., the annual charges thereon amounting to £326 109. 81 Total amount in 1894, £9,290 174. 61,the annual charge thereon being £5O ss. 4d. Number holding certificates of St. John Ambulance Class :—One chief constable, 4 inspectors, 6 sergeants, and 42 constables. Total 53. First aid has been rendered in 40 cases. The strength of 1 the Force on the 312 t of December, 1895; was 54 all told.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>LIFE INSURANCE ON A. NEW</head>
        <p>LIFE INSURANCE ON A. NEW</p>
        <p>PLAN.</p>
        <p>THE LAW OF AVERAGES ALTERED.</p>
        <p>A Lava Insurance Agent recently made the following statement :—" The cost of all Life Insurance has, in the past, been governed by the law of averages. It has often been too expensive for the poor,and many schemes have been devised, such as 'old age pensions,' &amp;c., to make up for this difficulty under which the poor laboured. It has also been remarked by many that 'Life' Insurance ought te be ' Death 'lnsurance, as pay- ments are only made after the death of the Insured. The new plan alters both the old system and this assumption, because this Life Insurance has to do with the living, and not with the dead. Besides, we knew that Insur- ance Companies when they insure 1,000 people of any are, figure by the law of averages that a certain definite number will die dints.; each succeeding year,and on the average they base their prices. "Now listen," he con- tinued, "my new system is very different from that. Suppose 1.000 children are born to-day. Ido not deal with them, as a rule.till they are three months old,though if called upon I would deal with them at any age. At the end of about three months, however, they are all compelled to be vaccinated, and a certain number will come through the ordeal well and strong, others will have diseases of the skin, of the eyes, of the internal organs, or perhaps serofula or consumption, or what is more likely, that awful disease, syphilis. Others, again, will go into simple decline. Now. I can insure each one of the whole thousand, not to die but to live. for 2s. 91. each. One bottle of V. V. V. or Vaccination Virus Van- qi)isher, the great antiseptic blond remedy,for each child will, if given beginning either a little before or at the time the child is vaccinated, absolutely prevent the con- traction of any of these diseases. By its antiseptic action it will kill the eerms which are carried in with the vac- cine matter, or those that get in later through the sore, and by its other actions it will remove all from the system. Mothers, insure your children for 29. 9d. each, and brine them up healthy. If any of you suffer from chronic diseases caught through vaccination, take V.V.V. regularly and it will cure you. It prevents and cures. It is Life. not Death Insurance. It does not cure part of those treated, but every one, so there are no averages, all are cured." It is sold at 2s. 9. by all Chemists, by Barclay &amp; Sons, Limited, 95, Farrinedon Street, and by the Vaccination Virus Vanquisher Com- pany, Limited, 31 Snow Hill, London, E.C., from whom you may secure-a V.V.V. pamphlet free.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>FOOTTiA.LL.</head>
        <p>FOOTTiA.LL.</p>
        <p>CRICKETETtS shmild Isamu c the "Pocket Diary and Companion," issued by the well-known Sports Mann- fa turers. GEORGE G. BUSSEY SG Co„ Prckham, London. All the information Cricketers require is found therein, as well as the usual Diary, yet it will go in the waistcoat pocket. Leather bound, gilt edges. 6d.; postage, 4. If you are not satisfied, G. G. B. &amp; Co. will return the price. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>WARRINGTON v. ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>The weather on Saturday was utterly unsuited for football, and consoquently the meeting between War- rington and St. Helens at Wilderspool was robbed of a great deal of interest, Although there was a very good gate, still, its dimensions would have been much greater had the sky been clear,and the rain been absent. Instead of about 1,000, only about 200 or 300 came from St. Helens, and these with the Warringtonians present,made a gate of about 4,000. The Warrington tgam was as advertised, but St. Helens came without Sudlow and Briers who were helping lAncashire to beat Yorkshire at Huddersfield, the latter by scoring the first try, and the former by leading up to the last one. Mr. Slevin, of Wigan,who was rendered so famous by the treatment he received at Rochdale a few weeks ago, when he had to fly to Heywood to get away from the mob, was the the referee, and the following was the components of the two teams :</p>
        <p>Warrington : BPCSOM, full back; Carey, Cross, Bar- ber (captain), and Lawless, threeqm.rters ; " Smack" Bate and Foden, half-backs ; Nevins, Taylor, Swift, Saunders, Burrows, Hill, Andrews, and Fairhurst, forwards.</p>
        <p>St. Helens : Foulkvs, full back ; Doherty, Sutton, Jacques and Barnes, threequarters ; Cross (cattimin) and Little, half-hacks ; T. Winstanley, W. WinstailTvy,Glad- win, Dale, Rimmer, Caveney, W hiteley, and Fildes, forwards.</p>
        <p>Cross having lost the toss, kicked off against a slight breeze, and Barber's return found touch beyond the cen- tre. The opening stages were decidedly in favour of Warrington,for from a pass by Bate to Lawless.the latter was almost over soon after the commencement. Foulkes relieved the danger by a long kick down the field, and Jacques and Doherty were just becoming dangerous, when Heesom secured and sent the ball back to half way. The visitors proved to be very formidable drib- blers, and in some determined dribbles, they put great pressure on the defence, but the defence proved equal to it, and Barber and Cross were especially prominent in repelling the attack. A passing run by Foden and Bate got to the St. Helens end again, and great enthusiasm was aroused by a splendid outburst of passing between Bate. Foden, Barber, Cross, and Carey, )hut the latter was too closely attended by Doherty to get clear. War- rington still attacked, and Carey getting possession,' might possibly have scored had he dropped at goal. He preferred however to kick obliquely across the field, and the hall went-. ;. Lawless and Barber just failing to reach the ball I_.l m?*-.' Cross dropped out,and Bate re- turned and put Warrington on the offensive once more. From the line out, and subsequent scrimmage, the St. Helens forWards,led by Rimmer, Caveney,and Whiteley, started a dribble which was not stayed until the home quarter fltg was reached,where Heesorn pluclily stayed their progress. A scrirnmage now ensued, and Bate get- ting possession made a ponderous kick which Foulkes failed to hold. Lawless,who was on side,rushed up,and gave the ball a flying kick which _sent it within a few yards of the visitors' goal line. A fine race, more than half the length of the field ensued between Lawless on the one side, and Foulkes and Doherty on the other. Lawless was the victor,and not having time to pick the ball up to carry it over, he gave it another kick behind the goal posts,and followed up, one scared one of the best tries seen on the Warrington ground during the prescut season. The easy place kick was successfully taken by Heesom.and thus WarrtnE.,,ton were five points Aftc.;. 63r0;s's kick off, St. Helens put extra energy into V t_• Jay, and a strong dribble by the for- wards, got past Heesorn, and within two minutes from the Warrington score, the St. Helens team got the ball over the line, and scored "all in a heap," just outside the goal post Jacques being credited with the point. The same player added the major point, and the teams were thus level ..qice more. The rest of the play during the first half was very level, neither side doing much aggressive work, and the interval arrived with a score of a goal each.</p>
        <p>Hill restarted, and Warrington for a short time by means of good forward play by Fairhurst, Taylor,Swift, and Saunders, and good back play by Bate, Foden, and Barber, were the Letter team. Foulkes was not playing a great game any., means, and several times had almost let War.i, •.,on in. An irresistible dribble took play to the opposite end, and but for the splendid defen- sive play of Warrington would have got ahead. At this point Warrington practically were left with fourteen men, for Foden was hurt, and although he remained on the field it could t seen that it was only with great difficulty he couli, :uove about. Nevins, who during a temporary absence of CroAs in the first half had played three-quarter, now went half-back, and played in that position to the end of the game. WarringtOn were thus severely handicapped with being a man short, and also having to face the rain, and St. Helens attacked vigor- ously. Time after time they enileavoured to get over, but as often they were repulsed: Two penalty kicks were taken by Jacques from near the line, and in each case they almost resulted in a score, on one occasion the ball falling just below the cross bar. The last two minutes were especially an anxious time for Warrington. Foden managed to make his mark near his own line. but could not make good use of it, and Caveney made his mark in reply rightin front of the goal posts. Cross took the place, but Bate charged the ball, and St. Helens nad to be content with a minor': 'rhe whistle blew soon after this lucky escape,and the game thus ended in a draw of a goal each. RUNCOAN v. MANNINGFIAM.</p>
        <p>The long anticipated visit of the leaders in the Nor- thern Union to Runcorn was made on Saturday. and visions of a bumper" gate " disappeared before a heavy and persistent downpour. The ground was soaked through and through, and in the centre and bottom side presented more the ap‘pearance of a clay pit than a field. Runcorn had a strong side, but the county match rob- bed the visiting team of the services of Lorrimer and Brown, and there were also two changes from the selected team. W. Faulkner having the choice, wisely put the Yorkshire men to face the wind. In the first few minutes Tolson dropped at goal when he had a good chance to" run in," an opportunity to score, as it ul- timately proved, and never afterwards asserted itself. The honae forwat cis, scrummaging in determined style, made grand headway, and J. Robinson getting hold, handed to J. Faulkner, and the ball passing between Butterworth and W. Faulkner to Myers, the latter com- pleting a brilliant effort by planting beneath the posts, the same player failing at goal. After give-and-take play amongst the forwards, the home men went off on a loose dribble, Needham saving grandly on the line. Houghton replied to a flying kick by dropping into touch in Mannineham quarters, and another bout of passing between Robinson, J. Faulkner, Warder, and W. Faulkner, enabled the latter to score in a good position, and Farmer did the needful. Grand passing betwecn Procter, Needham.and Tol3on put Manningham on the attack, and a series of good old-fashioned hard scrummages occurred in dangerous proximity to Run- corn line, but Houghton relieved with a huge punt, and the advantage was further increased by a penalty kick. , The centre was the scene of operations for some time, but the visitors by a series of short rushes beat Runcorn back, and had hard lines in not scoring. Half time ar- rived with Runcorn eight points to nothing ahead. The second half saw no addition to the score. Considering' the state of the ground, the game was both fast and ex- citing, and each side attacked in turn, without tangible result, the home team having slightly the best of it.— Runcorn well deserved their victory. On a day not . . _ _</p>
        <p>suited to their style of play, they exhibited a dash and determination that trequently nonplussed their oppo- nents, and while the forwards held their own fairly well in the scrums, they were much the smarter lot in the loose, and the home halves and three-quarters handled the greasy.soaked ball in a surprising insoner.and quite outshone the opposing blckg. Tne latter kicked too frequently, which, with Houghton to return, was not a paying policy. Both the tries were the result of splendid combination,and Robinson deserves credit for the initia- tion of the movements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>WIDNES v. HALIFAX.</head>
        <p>WIDNES v. HALIFAX.</p>
        <p>This return game on the forreer's ground was com- pletely spoiled by the atmospheric conditions, which were wretched in the extreme. There was only a poor "gate," not more than 2,000 people lining the enclosure. Both teams suffered by absentees, but Halifax were the worst off in this respect, as Firth, Rigg, and Riley were all doing duty for their county at Huddersfield. Bren- nan, the home full back, was too unwell to play, and Grarson, of the "A" team, made his first appesrance in the League team. Widnes had in the first half to play with the wind and rain against them, but the opening exchanges went slightly in their favour. Thanks to some fine defensive play by Chorley and Garside, the united efforts of the home team were frustrated, and after some desultory play in midfield, Garside put in a useful sprint, causing the home back to handl% After a flying visit to the Halifax end, Widnes were again compelled to fall back on the defensive. There was little brilliancy in the play of either team, the wet state of the ball rendering passing movements almost useless. The game at this period was confined to the forwards, both sets dribbling well. Grayson accomplished a clever save by taking the hall from the toes of three of his opponents, but a moment later Barnes had reached the corner flag before Drummond rushed hiru into touch—a very narrow shave for Widnes. At the other end Halifax were penalised, but Barber's attempt at goal from a good place was a failure. Determined rushes by the Halifax front rank</p>
        <p>r-itnts —SEALSKIN JACKETS and FURS of every des- -1:4 cription Cleaned or Re-Dyed and Altered. Fur Skins Uressei and Made-up into Rugs, Muffs, Collars, &amp;c.—B. COOKE, Manufacturing Furrier, 11, Upper Newington, and 21, Reneliaw-Areet,Liverpl 01, E stablished:over 21 years,</p>
        <p>forced a couple of touchdowns, but nothing more tangi- ble accrued to the Yorkshiremen, who from this point attacked incessantly until the arrival of the interval, there being no score when ha.f-time was called. Dickin- son restarted operations, and early on Grayson was troubled with a dangerous rush by the Halifax forwards. Alford, however, eased the pressure, and Widnes then I found ,a footing in the v;sitors' 25. There was little cohesion;however,among the team when it came to cross- ing the line, and several good openings were lost. The pressure was continued, and in the space of a couple of minutes both Barber and Drummond crossed the line, but lost possession. Widnes continued on the aggressive but the backs dallied too long with the ball, and when the whistle sohnded there was still a clean score-sheet. Widnes had numerous opportunities of scoring a winn- ing point in the second half, but their attacks lacked dash and finish. In the first half the home defence was everything which could be desired, for if at times Grayson was weak and nervous,one of the thee-quarters was always there to cover him. In the second half they did nearly all the attacking, and they have only their own ill-judged tactics on the line to thank for the loss i of a point n the competition. Forward the teams were I fairly well matched. Halifax held an advantage in the pack, but in the op3n the home lot were the more speedy.Rispan, Nevitt,Alford back. and Donelly,Whelan, and Forster forward,were the pick of the Widnes team. For Halifax, Garside played a superb game, and was well supported by Chorley, Arnold, Barnes, Dickinson and Knowles.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON" A" V. ST. lIRTENS " A.."—Warrirgton " A " team journeyed to St. Helens on Saturday to play off their return fixture in the second team there. On the visit of the Saints to Warrington they were thrashed by 40 points to nil, therefore it was not with feelings of anxiety as to the result that the Warrington team departed for St. Helens. The weather was the worst feature of the day, the rain pouring down incessantly. At St. Helens the homesters kept the visitors waiting and it was not till 3.20 that the game was commenced. The field was about a mile and a half from the station and was in a very sloppy condition. The following players made up the teams :—St. Helens : Mercer, full back ; Hull, Barnes, Sirncock and Hopley, three-quarters ; Melling and McLean, half backs ; Grice, Gasket:, Deers, McLoughlin, Dolan,Davies, Dougherty and A. N. Other. Warrington: Randlestfull back; Foster,lsherwood, Scott, and O'Brien threequarters ; Clarke and Higginbotham, half-backs ; Hoyle, Wilcock, Berry, Bailey, Hughes, Sudlow, Dakin, Freeth, forwards. St." Helens kicked off and Higginbotham returned effectively. The forwards dribbled up well and Higginbotham securing passed to Clarke and the latter sccred an easy try. Randlea failed at the goal-kick. Shortly after the homesters were penalised and the free .kick given to Warrington was taken by Pligginbotham, but charged down. This gave Holt a chance and he atter a tricky run passed to Sim- cock who was however pulled down by Higginbotham. Warrington then pressed, but going offside the homes. tars were granted a free kick which Grice made good use of only to have the ball returned by Foster. Higginbot- ham and Scott exchanged, but the latter mulled and a good opportunity was lost. Handles made a good mark and had hard lines in his shot at goal, the ball just pass- ing the posts. The visitors again penned their oppon- ents in their own quarters and a smart passing along the threequarters reached O'Brien who immediately grounded the ball over the line, but the position made a goal almost impossible. Half-time shortly afterwards was called with Warrington leading by 2 tries (6 points) to St. Helens nil. On the restart the hemesters had the wind in their favour. Clarke's kick off was not returned and he following up the ball Tr ade a very smart run but being tackled by Mercer he essayed to pass and the ball went forward. A scrimmage took place under the posts from which the Saints got away and punted down the field. Handles returned the ball into touch and from the</p>
        <p>line Out Isherwood seized the ball and made a good attempt at a try. " The Saints now pressed for a time and scrimmaged repeatedly on the Warrington line but could not get over. Warrington at last relieved and the forwards dribbled to mid-field. Here Grice made his mark but in taking the place-kick he was so slow that Rovle over ran him and took the ball from his feet and kicked up field. Higginbotham made a mark and sent the ball still further up and a grand dribble looked like another score for Warrington when the ball went dead. Desultory play followed this attempt and nothing . _</p>
        <p>further was scored, the game ending in a win for War: rington by 2 tries (6 points) to St Helens nit.</p>
        <p>YORKSHIRE v. LANCASHIRE.</p>
        <p>Played at Huddersfield in wretched weather,rain hav- ing fallen continuously all day. The spectators came up slowly for such a match, and the attendance could not have exceeded 5,000 when the teams entered the field. On the Yorkshire side Cooper and Sutcliffe were re- placed by Goldthorpe, Hunslet, and Shackleton, Batley, whilst on the Lancashire side the absence of Barber let in Sudlow, of St. Helens. Lancashire started to- wards the pavilion goal, and having the bat of the opening exchanges . breed a minor within five minutes. The failure of Boothroyd to hold the ball again let in Lancashire, and after a smart dribble by Worthington Lorimer just managed to kick dead. Lancashire adapted themselves better to the heavy ground, and a timely save by Rigg just prevented them getting over: Then the Yorkshire forwards went off with a strong nush,and the Tykes' back division became very dangerous, and Boothroyd scored a smart try. Goldthorpe took the kick, but the ball struck the upright. Roused by this reverse Lancashire played up finely, and the brothers Berry put in some smart work behind the "serum." This was mainly instrumental in the invasion of the Yorkshire 25. Brown relieved with a good kick. Sam Lees was next seen under weigh aftbr some smart passing, but he was quickly stopped. The visiting for- wards, however, carried the ball on, and Briers equalised, Coop's kick at• goal failing. Mainly as a result of a dribble by Rigg,Yorkshire again attacked,and after nice passing B)othroyd dribbled over the line, and had hard lines in not completing the try. A magnificent dribble by the Yorkshire forwards again resulted in the inva- sion of Lancashire territory, and Wood, gathering splen-1 didly,passed to Boothroyd,who was just pulled up on the line. Lancashire relieved splendidly, and the game was contested in mid-field up to half time, when the score was one try each. On ground which was now a perfect quagmire Yorkshire resumed, and, by strong play, im- mediately forced Lancashire back into their own 25. G. Berry got off, but was well tackled by Boothroyd, who shortly afterwards got off with a smart dribble, which I was just dropped on the line by Lees. Though Lan- cashire relieved somewhat, Coop was shortly afterwards called upon to stop a strong rush by the Yorkshire for- wards. A smart burst by Sudlow resulted in the inva- sion, for the first time during this half, of Yorkshire territory. The brothers Berry initiated a capital bit of passing to Lees, who transferred to Sudlow on the right ;-.7ing, and the latter threw back to Lees,who scored be- hind the posts and kicked a beautiful goal. A brilliant passing run by Wood, Rig, Brown, and Firth overcame all oppositionsbut Firth lost the ball over the line. Final result: Lancashire, one goal one try; Yorkshire one try. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>BIZTELTONWOOD V. ST.ELPIIIN'S.—PIaVnd On Saturday at Warrington. Burtonwood won the toss and St.Elphin's kicked off against the wind, and the first ten minutes were occupied hi midfield pl rv,neither goalkeeper being troubled. Then a rush by Dugdale saw St. Elphin's forwards fincking round the goal, only to be repulsed by Moran. However, the Warrington lads were playing with rare dash, and the half backs feeding their for- wards well the Saints gradually gained the upper hand. , At last a tangible result accrued, as J. Duckworth took a speedy dribble along the touch and centred beautifully into goal mouth, Taylor meeting it with his head and putting it well into the net amidst cheers. The Saints kept up the pressure, and a movement in which the whole forward rank participated saw Hatton putting in a swift screw shot which beat Griffith. With a lead of two goals St. Elphin's had the best of the game up to half time, but were unable to again penetrate the de- fence. The second half was not so interesting, as the rain and slush had evidently told on the players. But- tonwood had a little more of the game this half, but were never good enough for the Saints' backs, and the Warrington forwards gave Bridge and Moran great anxiety on several occasions. However, neither side succeeded in again notching a point, and tbe final score was—St. Elphin's, two goals ; Buttonwood, nil.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN ASSOCIATION V. ORFORD BARRACKS .—Run corn jeurneved to Warrincton on Saturday to play off their return league engagement with the Barracks team. The blues were minus S. Walker, H. Pr, Bloor and Hough but found the soldiers were also hard up, the homesters having considerable difficulty in raising a team. Good play and combination were entirely absent and the visi- tors just managed to snat6 a victory by four goals to two. C. Burrows (2), W. Walker and Bailie were responsible for the Runcorn score.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>WHAT WE ARE COMING TO.</head>
        <p>WHAT WE ARE COMING TO.</p>
        <p>SCENE: A SCHOOLROOM IN THE YEAR 1900. Teacher (to new boy) : "Hans, have you got your certificate of vaccination against small-pox ? " " Yes, sir." " Have you been inoculated for croup?" " Yes, sir." " Have you had an injection of cholera bacillus ?"</p>
        <p>" Yes, sir." " Have you a written guarantee that you are proof against whooping cough, measles, and scarlet fever ?" " es, sir." "Are you provided with your own drinking cup ? " " Yes, sir." " Will you mike a solemn promise never to exchange sponges with the other boys and never to use any pencil but your own ?" " Yes, sir." "Do you agree to have your books fumigated with sulphur and your clothes sprinkled with chloride of lime once a week ?"</p>
        <p>"Yes, sir." " Hans, I see that you fulfil all the requirements of modern hygienics. Now you can climb that wire, place vmurself on an isolated aluminium seat and commence doing your sums."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>PRINCESS ALICE ORPHANAGE,</head>
        <p>PRINCESS ALICE ORPHANAGE,</p>
        <p>BIRMINGHAM.</p>
        <p>VISIT OF CHILDREN TO WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The children who form the choir of the Princess Alice Orphanage, of Birmingham, paid a visit to Warrington I on Thursday. The Princess Alice Orphanage is a branch of the Children's Home and Orphanage which was founded in 1869 by the Rev. Dr. Stephenson, and is do- ing geed work in finding homes for orphan children who would otherwise be in danger of being driven to the streets. A preliminary meeting was held in Parr Hall on Thursday afternoon, which was especially for children, and an interesting programme was given to a very crowded audience. In the evening, another meeting was - held in the Parr Hall presided over by the Mayor (Coun- cillor Fairclough), and there were also present, Revs. Owen Watkins,G. D. Mason, W. G. Triggs, and J. Gun- nell, Councillors Shaw Green, and Ellison,Dr. T. Starkey Smith, J.P., and Messrs. J. W. Hayward J.P., R. G. Henshall, R. Garnett J.P., C. E. Parker, J. Dodds, &amp;c. The proceedings commenced with an anthem, "They shall mount up with wings as eagles," which was sung by the children in capital style. The Rev. W. G. TRIGGS, then offered prayer, the children singing the Lord's Prayer. The Rev. OWEN WATKINS, in introducing the Mayor remarked that Warrington was privileged in having as chief magistrate, a Mayor who took a deep interest in every department of life in this town. (Ap- plause.) He was the friend of all, and the enemy of none.—The MAYOR said that they. were there that niglac to assist the children's home founded by Dr. Stephensiou somewhere about the year.lB69,and which had done ex- cellent work ever since. To his mind, they were well worthy of support and patronage.- It gave him great pleasure to preside at that meeting, and he hoped they would all enjoy a very pleasant evenine.—A very enjoy- able programme was then submitted by the children. The choruses were well sustained, and showed evidence of careful training. The soloist showed great promise, and the voices of the singers blended well in the various trios and duets. Selections were also given on the dul- cimer, ar 1 sleigh bells, which earned encores, and in addition a number of action songs were given. Sister Freda acted as pianist. During an interval, Mr. DIIRLEY, the governor of the orphanage gave an account of the work done. He said that Drl Stephenson in his home was doing splendid work for the waifs and strays. There were many cases in the towns and villages, where the head of thelarnily left behind a large number of little children unprovided for, and it was at that critical point in their career, that they were taken by Dr. Stephenson, and rescued from the streets. He said that the work done by this means was very per- manent, and only a very small percentage fell away ic after life. The Princess Alice Orphanage was not so much for the waifs and strays, as for the children of Christian parents who were left orphans at a very early age, when situations were provided for them. During their life in the Home, they had various duties to do, and the whole of the work was done by them. Their life was made as nearly as possible like a home, and he was glad that in many Unions, they were adopting this method, iestead of housing large numbers of children together. As an instance of the good which had been done by various Homes in connection with the Wesleyan Methodists, be mentioned that over 10,000 children had passed through.them, many of them tieing in good posi- tions in England, Canada, and elsewhere. He was sorry that the expenses of the Home during last year were more than the income, and he hoped that the friends of the Home would endeavour to wipe off that deficiency. He hoped that they would have a representative or re- presentatives in Warringtcn,who would collect subscrip- tions annually. Papers would be given to people who would care to subscribe annually.—At the close of the entertainment Mr. ROBERT GARNETT proposed a vote of thanks to the MAYOR for presiding. He said that he had known every Mayor in Warrington since the first was elected, and he would undertake to say that a better Mayor had never,filled the civic chair than Mr. Fair- dough. (Applause.)—Councillor Suave GREEN seconded the proposition and echoed the sentimects of Mr. Gar- nett. He said he was glad that the Mayor had now the opportunity of seeing what Wesleyan Methodism was doing. He was sure that everybody was satisfied with the entertainment that night.—The vote was carried with acelamation, and the MAYOR in responding said, that Mr. Garnett almost made him blush. He thanked them cordially for the way in which they had received him, and he had greatly enjoyed the singing that night, and he thought it reflected great credit to the establish- ment they came from.—Mr. DORLEY then thanked the persons who entertained the children during their stay in Warrington, and also thanked Mr. Triggs for the trouble he had had in making arrangements for the visit.—The meeting closed with the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>CITARGIE OF CILIAIINA.L LIBEL AT</head>
        <p>CITARGIE OF CILIAIINA.L LIBEL AT</p>
        <p>POULTON.</p>
        <p>AN APOLOGY ACCEPTED.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednes- day before Captain Withington and Mr. S. R. Armitage, Thomae Morris, Mancheeter-reed, Paodington, ex- labourer on the Cheshire Lines,was charged with havieg on the 18th Feb. last, in Poulton published a di-lematory libel upon Joseph Adshead, and also one upon Wißiala Jane. Mr. A. Browne appeerel for the prosecution, and in- formed the Bench that this was a case of criminal libel, and defendamt was summoned in two summonses for publishing defatnatory matter concerning the two com- plainants. He was instructed by William Jane,foreman of the masonry department of the Cheshire Lines, 'and Joseph Adshead, 2, Cheshire Lines Cottages, Aintree,and inspector of the permanent way between Aintree and Southport. Defendant had been in the Company's em- ployment, when the Cheshire Lines Committee gave notice for a certain number of men to be discharged, and defendant happened to be one of them, and the foreman, William Jane, had to give him 14 days notice. Af.er the notice defendant wrote two letters one to Mr. Meldrum, manager of the Cheshire Lines, and the other to Mr. Scott, the chief engineer. Mr. Scott was the master of the two com- plainants and the result was that he had had to suspend them until snch time as they had brought proceedings to , prove the untruthfelnees of the letter. Mr. Browne then read a portion fo the letter (which was dated 18th) Feb. ' and which stated the libel that on the 6th December Adshead was drinking with a man named Benton and was so drunk that he could scareely stand. That was utterly untrue and he had 20 or 30 witnesses to prove that Adsbead was not drunk at all. The bead inspector, Mr. Ligo, saw him both at 2.14 and about 5 o'clock, and there was not a trace of drink on him. The libel against Jane was that he allowed some of his workmen to " treat " him and because they did so they got on better than the other men. Defendant alleged that a man could not get on without treating the foreman. Mr. Scott had instructed him (Mr. Browne) that if defendant had any complaint to make he should have made it before he was discharged and not several days after as he did. Be (Mr. Browne) had sent defen- anta letter threatening proceedings and demanding an apology, but defendant had taken no notice of it. Mr. Knowles who appeared for defendant had had a consul- tation with him that morning and he had agreed to certain terms. Mr. Scott said he not oely wanted these men to clear their characters but he wanted it publicly known that no workman must be permitted to send such letters to him without foundation. If defendant would publicly and in open court apologise for having written the letters and express his regret at having done so and admit that there was no truth whatever in any of the statements, hejwoula withdraw the summons on payment of costs. Mr. Knowles said he had shown the defendant that he had slandered the character of the prosecuter,and asked him to apologise. During the heat of passion after his discharge he had libelled his former masters. Be now publicly said he regretted having sent the letters to the gentlemen mentioned and admitted that the statements contained were untrue and through him (Mr. Knowles) he tendered his public apology to Messrs. Adshead and William! Jane, !and he undertook not to repeat the offence. Mr. Browne withdrew the summons on those terms.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>W ESTON.</head>
        <p>W ESTON.</p>
        <p>TRAP ACCIDENTS.—On Monday, when returning from Runcorn to Weston Point in his trap, Mr. Peter Coyle, landlord of the Weaver Hotel, met with an accident. When nearing the hospital on the Weston-road, the horse took fright, and became unmanageable. To add to Mr. Coyle's dilemma, the shafts snapped and the horse be- coming free, raced away, and was not stopped until it reached the Manor Farm in the occupation of Mr. James Ford. Mr. Coyle was precipitated out of the vehicle, and was dragged along the ground a short distance, re- ceiving superficial injuries to his legs, and a severe shaking.—On the same day, a somewhat similar trap accident befel Mr. H. Darlington, of Weston. He was returning from Halton Stati m in charge of a horse and trap, when the horse took fright, and he was thrown out of the vehicle. Beyond a shaking his injuries were</p>
        <p>Deansgate. Manchester. Advice free</p>
      </div>
