      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Eettero to the Ottitor.</head>
        <p>Eettero to the Ottitor.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON FREE LIBRARP—A SUGGESTION. Sir,—The Warrington free lending library is sadly behind the times in nDt possessing an indicator showing the books which are at present in the library ready for issue and those which are out. The Library Committee would save the public and the library staff a great amount of time and trouble if an indicator, something similar to those which are at present in use at the Bir- mingham Free Library, was brought into use. This would enable a borrower to see at a glance whether any particular book was in or out, and would entirely dis- pense with the present system i.e. making application for a book which is out. I trust that this may meet the eye of a member of the Library Committee, and that he will take the matter up.—Yours truly, " PUNCEIASO." February 26th, 1896.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>"THE INFALLIBILITY OF PEERS."</head>
        <p>"THE INFALLIBILITY OF PEERS."</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. Peers assures us that "ignorance and credulity " are " perennial." He supplies in his own person ample evidence of this, for though he mocks at such absurd doctrines as those of the infallibility of the Pope or the inerrancy of the Scriptures he holds as a firmly based truth the infallibility of Peers. The polemi- cal performances of this Penketh Pope chiefly consist in ridiculing those who differ from him ; but he occasion- ally makes a statement as to matters of fact, usually of a more or less inaccurate nature. Thus he repeats this week his fallacy that Christianity is superstition based on ignorance ; and,as you have kindly permitted me to point out previously several errors into which Mr. Peers has fallen,so I trust I may be allowed to contradict this ' gross misrepresentation,and to say that Chriztianity is a religion based on adequate evidence and that Christians have nothing to fear from the most searching examina- tion of that evidence. We should indeed be dishonour- ing Him Whose followers we claim to be and Who was Truth Incarnate if we shrank from any fair investiga- tion of the foundations of our faith, and I personally re- gret that Mr. Peers shrinks from such an investigation which would certainly occupy his time more profitably than the composition of long and laboured letters which may give pain to Christians but cannot injure Chris- tianity.—Yours Scc.,</p>
        <p>LAYMAN.</p>
        <p>February 26, 1896.</p>
        <p>CLOSING WORDS UPON MR. PEERS' MISSIONARY FALLACIES. "Should thy boastings make men hold their peace; and when thou mockest shall no man make thee ashamed." Job. (Revised Version). Sir,—Mr. Peers should not be in a hurry to court castigation. It will come soon enough for his liking. There is a heavy score against him which will have to be settled. Among other things I shall have to call either his sanity or his truthfulness in question. Waving for the present his complete misrepresentation of the article he refers to in the "London Missionary Chronicle" ci this month, let me ask him where he got it from that " Zaminer's creed has stolen from a heathen nation the best bit of morality in its divine teaching ?" I challenge him to the proof of this unmitigated falsehood. To resume just now my remarks upon the true cause of Chinese hostility to Christian missions, I have said that it is not an affair of religion to any extenrsif at all. Of this there is satisfactory evidence taken down from the lips of Mandarins in authority. "The cat is,indeed, out of the bag," Mr. Peers, but it jumps in the very opposite direction to that which you represent by the garbled use you have made of the article referred to above. The national character of Chinamen has been for ages simply a proud, overbearing hatred of foreigners who- ever they may be. As to any care about religion,witness their indifference to Buddhism which is wholly an exotic, but which was readily embraced, and prevails to an enormous extent amongst them. As to Confucianism,it is little more than a philosephy. and can scarcely be called a religion in any sense. It has to do mostly with the duties of man to man; but with regard to any duties owing to God, it knows and enforces little, and cares less. It is well known that throughout the whole upper classes of Chinamen a dark indifferent Atheism prevails. Taoism, the other wretched apology for religion, is no- thing less than a system of demonology, and its victims the last in the worl.l who would be likely to refuse in exchange the blessedness of the Christion faith. To what then is more certainly to be attributed their opposition to missionaries? Let the mandarin who many years ago addressed Hudson Taylor, a missionary of large ac- quaintance with the people, answer the ques- tion. Urging upon the mandarin that the desires of Protestant Christians were entirely of a benevolent character, and consequently the suspicion which his countrymen manifested should not exist, he replied, " It is all very well for you, Mr. Taylor, to talk to the foolish people in this sort of way, but you know very well, and I know very well ,what the policy of Eng- land is with regard to China. It is just this : You came here 40 or 50 years ago; you found China united and prosperous and wealthy, and we were too strong for you to absorb as you were absorbing India, and you deter- mined by opium to impoverish our country ; and you determined by missionaries to deceive the hearts of the people, and win yourselves a party in the country, and then to seize the whole land for yourselves. We knew perfectly well what your designs are, and it is no use your attempting to blind us mandarins by such repre- sentations as you make." Same years after this,another authority plied Mr. Taylor with the same charge " We know perfectly well," said this mandarin, " What the policy of England is: You are first optimising our coun- try, and endeavouring to get a sufficient number of adherents to Christianity, and then you intend to seize the whole land." Now this feeling is so widely spread among the educated and upper classes in China, that no other solution is needed of the hostility often shown to Christian missions. But the truth of my contention is patent enough—that the degrading superstitions, the prevalent infanticides, the cruelties of their law admin- istration, and utter indifference to human life, with all the misery arising out of such wretched conditions among the people—not only select upon the worthlessness of Confucian maxims to meet their needs but demand and justify the introduction of that religion of Christ, which mere windbags among us set themselves up so much to depreciate and to revile. " Yes,Mr. Peers,you may" reel off" your buffoonery by the furlong—the gospel of" Jesus only" is giving a good account of itself in the land of Confucius notwith- standing. The powerless maxims of that philosopher are being supplemented by the might of God's Word which has been circulated by th•Usands among the people, and is welcomed and read even in the palaces at Pekin. Jesus Christ has planted His foot upon the head of the old serpent among the "Yellows," and as He crushed the dragon of Pagan Rome so surely will He conqun in China. Nor is His advent among the" Browns" of India less welcomed hv that people, or less certain of final vic- tory. In 1874 the Rajah of Travancore publicly said, " I accept Christian ethics in their entirety. I have the highest admiration for them " ; and a learned Brahmin has declared, "of one thing I am convinced ; do what we will, oppose as we may, it is the Christian's Bible that will sooner or later vrork.the regeneration of this land." Such testimony could be multiplied almost to any extent. Mr.Peers' missionary fallacies may so far be dismissed. Their transparent folly was evident enough to most folk from the first, and the attention bestowed upon them hardly justified. The points established in opposition to his statements may be tabulated. 1. It is not true that Christianity is forced down the throats of either the " Yellows "or the 66 Browns." by bayonets or gunboats. 2. Chinese opposition to Christian missions does not proceed from any question of religion at all. 3. Five maxims merely are not sufficient to meet the world's needs,or to work the regeneration of any people. 4. The present condition of civilization among the leading nations upon earth is due to the ennobling in- fluence and divine character of the teaching of Christ, which is, therefore, necessary for China and India, and for every other portion of the heathen world. I should be glad, Mr. Editor, if you could transfer to your columns—the whole article from the London Mis- sionary Society's " Chronicle," which I have left with you that your readers may judge for themselves of Mr. Peer's trustworthiness in dealing with evidence upon subjects under discussion.—Yours truly, ZAMINER.</p>
        <p>27th Feb., 1896.</p>
        <p>The article in the Lindon Missionary Society's, " Chronicle" on "Chinese Antamonism to Missionaries," which " Zaminer " a.lcs us to print will be found on the same page as these letters.—Ed. Ex.</p>
        <p>PAGAN WHEAT IN CHRISTIAN CHAFF. " Now therefore. behold, the Lord hath put a lying spirit in the month of all these thy prophets."—l Kings, xxii 23. " To assert that Christianity communicated to man truths previously unknown arg7es on the part of the assertor either gross ignorance or else wilful fraud."—Buckle. Sir,—This correspondence has proved, among other hings, how very little the Christian apologist really knows of the book he imagines he is defending; and also to what extremities of persistt2nt inveracity he can attain when bolstering up his assertions. Of this bad method " Zaminer "is a good example. Persis'zent and pertin- acious denial of obvious facts is not the best way to get at the truth. And it is funny to see the man who has just crept ignominiously out of .a " corner" himself vaingloriously boasting of having " cornered " his opponent on a subject not yet discussed! Our Zaminer is too hasty ; he halloas before he is out of the wood. I have the historic rod in pickle for hill yet. He appears to be fond of obsolete instruments of punish- ment—as the pill.iry—and of using archaic weapons. I opine that the promiscuous throwing about of " stink pots " charged with the " odium theologicum " has now no chance as against the modern "weapons of precision." It is even possible now to bur a pound of candles or a cwt. of cal without previously amcertaining the vendor's oundness on the doctrine of "prevenient grace" or fina' perseverance." But, lest I be accused of evadion "—save the mark !—I must again call up</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 7, 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 7, 1896.</p>
        <p>" Zaminer's " inaptly selected witnesses—Plato and Seneca. For present purposes, however, I shall reverse this order and make the philosophers the judges and get " Zaminer " cornered "in she witness box. Let us see how they would adjudicate upon " Zainiuer's" entirely gratuitous assumption of their acceptance of that creed as Divine which was" foolishness to the Greeks." However, the philosophic pagans, after asking why they had been brought down from Elysium on such a bootless errand proceed to cross-question Zaminer as to the foundation of his assumption and his creed. " It is true," says Plato "that we wait for one,be it a god or a god-inspired man, to teach us our religious duties, but what evidence have you to show that the Jew was either the one or the other ?" "May it please your lordships we have documentary evidence," answered Zaminer. "Produce it." " Documents" handed up to the bench for examination. " Wm, Ah ! Begins at the beginning of things I see." Call the specialists. To the physical astronomer. "I find it stated that the sun was created after the earth, is that so ?" "No ! the physical evi- dences all point to the opposite conclusion." To the botanist. "We have here plants before the creation of the sun, what say you ? " Quite impossible." The geologist, " It is here stated that oar earth was created in six days some six or seven thousand years ago—jade,- ing from its geneology—this so ?" "Quite absurd; the six days would be greatly under-estimated at six millions, and so of the seven thousand." "But, my Lords," interposes Zaminer, "these were six periods and not six solar days!" "We must let the 'do- cuments' speak for themselves; we cannot take your interpolations as evidence." The testimony of the ethnologist, the philologist—in fact, all the 'ologists was of the same darnnatory character. "Not very good credentials as a guide to truth," quoth Seneca, " but let us look further ; where is your evidence that this Jew was the Divine Teacher who would authoritatively reveal to us the certainty cif religious truth " ? " All in those documents, my Lords." " Are they the same documents we have been examining ?" "They are." Flere Plato, who had been looking over the New Testa- ment, interposed and said, "I find here frequent men- tion of redemption, and your Teacher spoken of as the Redeemer, can you explain this ?" "For as in Mem all die, so also in Christ shall all be made alive," quoted Zaminer, "this was the atonement for the sin of Adam." "But," exclaimed Seneca, "we have had evidence that this Adam and serpent episode was unhistorical,' —a pre-scriptural Pagan fable " ! "And I find here,' put in Plato, " that three-fourths of the creed you wish U 3 to adjudicate upon are not original, but borrowed from other and earthly sources; while, as to the other fraction, that is composed of utterly impractical if not immoral precepts. For instance, have you sold all you have and given it to the poor? Have you turned the other cheek when smitten? Do you practise the precept 'Resist not evil'? Did you ever know any one else,out of a lunatic asylum, do this? No ! to all these questions. Then how do you expect us to accept what you pract.i- cally reject? The old Greek ruling confirmed ; it is indeed 'foolishness,' stand down." Which poor Zaminer did with a look of caudal depression. No, no, Mr. Zaminer the "broad mind" of Plato would be much too broad for your narrow creed., and would require acceptance to be based upon evidence, and you have none to offer, except of a damnatory sort. Nor would the rhapsodies of a fantastic creed, or of its hysterical professors, outweigh this want of evidential confirmation. The age of miracular attesta- tion of Christianity having gone, with the credulous ignorance that engendered it, the miracle of the accept- ance of Zeminer's creed by any cultured and well- informed mind, not previously steeped in the stultifying element of the popular superstition, need' not be looked for on this side millenium. Our Zaminer objects to the mild fun I have been poking at him. He cannot under- stand it. This Aristophanic treatment perplexes the ponderous dulness of our " Dominie Sampson." But his creed is, like its professors, utterly impervious to reason and reasoning—it will never be killed by argument, but will die eventually of cultured ridicule. Born of ignorant credulity ; cradled, reared, and nourishei on sentiment, it will meet its death by the laughter of the enlightened. Nevertheless," these objections are valid only on the (quite baseless) assumption, the Divine origin of the creed. The many and obvious signs of its earthly origin make this assumption ridiculous. But, if we regard Jesus as a human eclectic,making a selec ion of the hest from all passible sources,incultating by precept and example the beauties of plain living and high thinking, we have everything to admire and little or nothing to regret in his teaching. This prin- ciple is the sweetener and preserver of life, individual and national, and is well illustrated by the saying of Epictetus,:" There is no need of adorniag a man's house with rich hangings or paintings, since the most graceful furniture is temperance and modesty, which are lasting ornaments and will never be the worse for wearing." It is the fate of Empires, as of all other things, to rise, culminate and fall, but I am convinced that this fate is accelerated if not solely caused, by the neglect of this golden rulc.—Truly yours,</p>
        <p>JOHN PEERS. BETTING AND GAMBLING.-AN APPEAL TO</p>
        <p>THE NATION.</p>
        <p>Sir,—We believe that the public morals are being seri- ously affected in every class of society, while the national sports, especially horse-racing, are degraded by the development of betting and bookmaking, which are steaiily increaing. The increase is to a great extent to be traced to the trade of the professional betting men, which has been allowed until recently to grow quite un- checked. That the danger of continued indifference to the evil has been at length recognized by the magistrates is shown by the multiplication of convictions,and by the severity of the remarks from the Bench which frequently accompany the decisions.' The pi inciples of English law in dealing with social evils, while respecting individual liberty, forbid tempta- tion being placed in the way of others for profit ;and this distinction seriously needs emphasizing in the cage of betting, by a systematic enforcement of existing laws, I and of' such amendments to them as public opinion may sanction. It has become necessary in this way to re- strain the demoralizing work of the bookmakers, whether carried on in the public streets, or in places where they are allowed for an entrance fee to ply their traffic. And to the enforcement of the law must be added strenuous efforts, in every direction, to influence public opinion by that moral suasion which alone can be used in dealing with individuals and their action. We haw satisfied ourselves that the National Anti- Gambling League would in no way trespass upon the liberty of the snbject, nor meddle to the slightest extent with the sports of the country. The evil it opposes is assuming the proportions of algrave social and national peril. The Society has already done much by its unsel- fish educaticnal labours to arouse and improve public opinion. Its funds are administered with eccnomy ; the principal part of its work is done without remuneration; all shades of religious and political opinion are repre- sented among its members, and we censider that it is worthy of generous countenance and pecuniary SUM:Wt. of Signed by ;—The Bishop of London, the Bishop Durham, Earl Nelson, Viscount Halifax, Rev. J. Morlais Jones (Chairman of the Congregational Union), Rev. Richard Leitch (Moderator of the Presbyterian Church of England). Rev. J. G. Greenough (chairman of the Baptist Union), Rev. D. J. Waller, D.D. (Wesleyan). Sir John H. Kennaway, Bart, M.P., Hon. Thomas H. W. Pelham, Mr. Quintin Hogg, Judge Thomas Hughes, QC.</p>
        <p>sweet, and they always go together. For a man to be told to repent, and to be told that he has something he needs to repent of,is not soothing to be sure ; but to be told to repent for the reason that if he does so, for him the Kingdom of Heaven is at hand, and what occasion for irritatica is left ? The missionary is a bearer of good tidings, and unhesitatingly we affirm that the pre- dominant staple of missionary preaching in China is just that." Passing from the subject-matter of the missionary's preaching to the policy adopted by the missionary in dealing with the people, Dr. Ashmore writes : " We affirns that missionary policy is not to irritate, but to make friends; and the missionary going into the towns and villages does make friends. He is politic and civil ; he is friendly and affable ; he is courteous and dignified ;he nays for what he gets, and soon finds he can get credit for anything the shops offer if he has oc- casion to ask it. In his preaching and argumentation he seeks to be fair-minded and to avoid arousing ani- mosity. Animosity is the very thing he is most anxious to avoid. He wants to conciliate, to gain a candid and hospitable hearing for what h 4 has to offer. He is not such a hlunclerhead as to spoil his own case by coarseness and vituperation. And yet, somehow, there is the anti- missionary feeling." Where does it come from ? How does it arise ? "It has a genealogy of its own, and the register is well kept. It is not a case of spontaneous generation, but of systematic propagation, and that by the same class of men—the Literati and the Yemen men. If these disturbers of the peace would keep their hands off, the missionaries would get along well enough with the vil- lagers. " Bs it observed next that it is not zeal for their own religion that animates the Yemen underlings—i.e., the lictors and policemen—who do the behests of a Chinese mandarin; nor is it wounded sensibility for the honour of Confucius that fires up these Literati to insatiate hate. The missionary may not have said a word about Con- fucius. The charge is not made against him that he has. Note that point. And note another one along with it. Yemen officers and literary aspirants and expectant' officials are not given to religious senti- mentalism ; it would surprise themselves very much to have such a character imputed to them. They are not zealots,they are not even Pharisees. They are Sadducees. They do not trouble themselves about either angel,spitit, or resurrection from the dead. For such doctrines they have a supercilious disdain. If the common people are se stupid as to swallow such things, let them do it. " So far as these speciiic. notions are concerned the Confucian scholar does not care a rap. If occasion re- quires he can hobnob with a Parsee or a Shinto all day Long. At the open ports he does not trouble his brain to ask what men belisve. Nor would it be different in the interior if it were not that it is a foreigner that is doing it. It is the foreigner himself that he wants to keep ,out. If a native were to pass along the street telling a story about a resurrection from the dead of somebody who lived eighteen hundred years ago, the Confucian scholar would simply curl his lip and hurry on : but when a foreigner does the some thing his wrath is aroused. These foreigners ! these hated foreigners ! they have come here also. Missionaries they call them- selves, but what is a missionary ? Allow them to stay and others will come, and by and by we shall have consuls, and consuls will have gunboats, and trcuble with the foteigners will begin. It is along that line that a tru3 explanation is to be sought.—London Missionary Society's " Chronicle."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>CHINESE ANTAGONISM TO MIS-</head>
        <p>CHINESE ANTAGONISM TO MIS-</p>
        <p>SIONARIES.</p>
        <p>In an article by the Rev. W. Ashmore, D.D., of Swatow, published in the Chinese " Recorder" for November, this veteran missionary undertakes to answer the question : "Is this antagonism against the missionary because he .is a missionary, or because he is a foreigner ? " and he gives some strong cmtnmon-sense reasons for believing that Chinese anti-missionary out- breaks are in no sense religious in their origin. He is writing with Protestant missionaries in his eye,and some of his remarks on missionary policy are more applicable to Protestants than to the Roman Catholics, who,by the secrecy of their methods,by the occasional high-handed- ness of their dealings with the officials,and by their plan of continually mixing themselves up with the lawsuits of their converts with heathen neighbours,have in some Instances awakened a feelins; of very strong resentment against themselves in the Chinese mind. It is rare to find Protestant missionaries, however, who either court secrecy in their work, or who attempt. to interfere in legal proceedings which are not connected with religious persecution. Indeed, an English missionary, even if he were minded to carry things with a high hand in dealing with mandarins, would find his consul would give him no assistance, but would at once check him. Dr. Ashmore begins his article by quoting an objection often heard from the lips of those who criticise missionaries : "But in preaching their Gospel of Salvation the mission- aries may run across the popular view ef idol worship, and ancestral worship, and of Confucianism in so far as it is a religion." " Yes," he replies, " there is that possibility. But here let the missionary be heard in-his own defence. It is assumed commonly that missionaries go smashing round like a bull in a china-shop,thrusting and punching in all directions, as if bent on giving on giving the greatest amount of annoyance possible. That is a calumny. It is true, that now and then, here and there, may be found some ill-balanced, ill-tempered talker, who violates the rules of common missionary courtesy, but there are few in number and feeble in influence comnared with the body of solid, sober, and sensible missionaries. Now note what we affirm in connection with them. Their moiety of influence, though defective so far as it goes, has not sufficed to stir up the anti-missionary feeling that has been manifest in these recent years. Why saddle the missionary body with such an awful charge as would make them out to be ill-bred and inconsiderate distur- bers of the public peace? "It is said that it is the missionary's unpalatable doctrines which are at fault. Granting freely that the missionary has some truths to utter not in themselves acceptable, it must be admitted that even these are at- tended with other truths fitted to turn bitter waters into</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening the monthly meeting of this Board was held in the Board-room at the Town Hall, Runcorn. Mr. J. Pritchard occupied the chair,and there were also present :—Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Dr. McDougall, Messrs. S. Taylor, J.P., C.C., F. J. Norman, J.P., E. Imison, J.P., and,G. Moss (clerk).—The minutes of tho School Attendance Committee showed that 67 parents were summoned, 60 appeared, and it was decided that proceedings be taken against 8. The visitors' report for the month eudidg February 21st, showed that the num- ber of children on the rolls was 3,292, as com?ared with 3,433 in the same period last year, a decreese of 141. The average attendance was 2,775, a percentage of 81.8, a slight decrease on February, 1895, The committee recommended that the tender of Mr. John Yates, to supply uniforms for the visitors at a cost of £8 be accepted.—The Rev. Dr. Gastaldi moved, and Mr. Imison seconded the adoption of the minutes, and they were approved of.—Two communications were read from the Education Department. The department noted the changing ot the name of Greenway-road schools to Victoria-road schools, and also that on the recommenda- tion of Her Majesty's Inspectors, there would be no examination of the children attending Mill Brow school in July next.—The minutes of the Schcal Management Committee showed that the arranging of a scale of salaries for head teachers and assistants at the Board schools had been under con- sideration,but were not yet in a completed state.—Dr. McDougall said he would like to ask the object in view in postponing the decision of the committee on the mat- ter.—The Chairman said nothing definite had been ar- rived at, and the sale of salaries was still in an incom- plete state.—Dr. McDougall : It is still in the incubatory state. (Laughter.)—The Board thea went into com- mittee.</p>
        <p>THE SUICIDE SUICIDE OF A TRADESMAN AT</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND 1 ATHLETICS. I</p>
        <p>RI7GBY FOOTBALL. S. Helens and Warrington have long been rivals, as representatives of neighbouring towns generally are, but they have never played a tougher matoh than they did on Saturday. In the six matches played between them during the present and previous two seasons, Bt. Helens have by far the best record, for the result has been four wins for St Helens and one for Warrington, and the re- maining game has been drawn. Warrington,in fact,for a great number of years, have been able to do but little against St. Helens clab.</p>
        <p>The game on Saturday was a very uninteresting one, but this was to a great extent due to the miserable weather, for the ground was sodden with the rain. There was a pretty good gate even with the bad day, but had these atmospheric conditions been different, there would have been a " bumper " crowd. The St. Helens gener- ally come in their hundreds to the Warrington match, Lut on the present occasion only about 250 made their appearance.</p>
        <p>Warrington were exceedingly fortunate in drawing, for during the last two minutes of the match, a mark was made almost tinler the Warrington goal, but Bate managed to spoil Cross's kick, by charging it. St. Helens had also the major part of the game during the last half, while the te ims were about level in the first. There was a vast difference, however, in the merit of the tries which were scored. The one by Lawless was one of the best seen this season, for beyond the kick of Bate which began the movement, it was a splendid bit of individual work. The St. Helens try was on the con- trary scored all in a heap. The ball ought to have been stopped several times, but the sudden rush caught War- rington napping and thus St. Helens were enabled to equalise.</p>
        <p>It rarely falls to the lot of any team to have such an easy chance of winning a match as fell to St. Helens during the last two minutes. A mark made almost under the goal posts is almost a certain goal, and how Cross failed to send the ball over the bar is a mystery. It will probably be a long time betore such another chance offers itself. With the exception of that one kick, St. Helens seemed to be in good kicking form, and it was surely a mistake of the St. Helens captain not to depute Jacques to place the ball, after be had done so well dur- ing the atternoon.</p>
        <p>To come to the individual players, Heesom played a better game than be did the previous week,while it car.- not be said that Foulko played a grand Lame by any means. Barber was in form and played in a sterling manner all through the match, as did Cross. Carey had comparatively little to do,but he made his presence felt whenever he got the chance. Lawless gave further evi- dence of his scoring powers,and this was the third suc- cessive match in which he has scored. He has not only , the best record up to date for tho " A "team, but he also holds the same position for the first team, having scored four tries,and dropped a goal.</p>
        <p>Bate was superior to either the St. Helens halves and continues in good form, and Foden was playing a good game until he was hurt. It was among the forwards where the St. Helens were the superior. The visiting torwards excelled in dribbling and frequently non- plussed Warrington thereby. This was a feature in which Watrington showed up prominently in for some time but it appears to have fallen off of late. The Warrington lot were much on a level,though Nevins deserves especial I remark owing to the fact that he played ferward, half back,and threequarter,during the game. This is not the first tune he has done so, and he thus shows his useful- ness in the team.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn team is tie be congratulated upon thrash- ing the leading club in the league in such a decisive fashion as that of Saturday's. Eight points to nil is no mean performance, and the score represented the game. Runcorn were the superior team, but in justice to Man- ningham it must be stated that the county match at Huddersfield had robbed them of two of their smartest players in Lorrimer and Brown. Runcorn, however, cannot be blamed for this, and their play on SAurclay, considering the state of the ground and the inclemen weather, was highly creditable to them. The three suc- cessive home matches have resulted as was anticipated a month ago, and Runcorn have now to their credit 42 points in the combination table. Taking into account the number of games played, Runcorn are now in as good position as Manningham, and one point better than Halifax. Trif3 surely, should be extremely satisfactory to the suppor- ters of the now renowned Cheshire club, and the Run- corn team may yet surprise the football world by cal- ryina off the much coveted medals. To-day they are to visit" Wakefield to play the Trinitarians—a team which of late seems to have got into its old stride again.</p>
        <p>The Northern Union is as follows : Score. Score. Pld. Won. Lost. Dm. For. Alest. Pte. 115 anningham 31 ... 23 ... 8 ... 0 ...224 ...122 ... 46 Halifax Runcorn 29 ... 17 ... 4 ... 8 ...221 ... 83 ... 42 Oldham 3O ... 20 ... 8 ... 2 ...267 ...121 ... 42 Beghouse Ran 3l ... 14 ... 10 ... 7 ...140 ... 87 ... 35 Leigh Bradford Hull Hunslet Wigan 3O ... 12 ... 11 ... 7 ...148 _lO7 ... 31 , Leeds Liversedge 29 ...13 ...13 ... 6 ...206 ...184 ...29 Tyldeslev Widnes *St Helena 2B ... 10 ... 11. ... 7 ...104 ...125 ... 25 Warrington Wakefietd V 3l ... 10 ... 17 ... 4 ... 87 .. 220 ... 24 Stockport : 3O ... 7 ... 15 ... 8 ...108 ...206 ... 22 Batley 29 ... 6 ... 16 ... 7 ... 72 ...207 ... 19 Beght'n Ran 29 ... 5 ... 17 ... 7 ... 97 —153 ... 17 Huddersfield— 29 ... 6 ... 20 ... 33 ... 94 ...166 ... 15 Rochdale H 2B ... 3 ... 17 ... 8 ... 56 ...203 ... 14</p>
        <p>The Lancashire section is as follows : Score Score Fts. Yid. Won Lost Drn For. Agrst. Runcorn Leigh Oldham Wigan Tyldesley *St. Helens Warrington Widnes Stockport Rochdale H l3 ... 2 ... 6 ... 6 ... 32 ... 93 ... 9 E g'ht'n Ran l6 ... 8 ... 10 ... 6 ... 21 ... 81 ... 6</p>
        <p>The Yorkshire section is as follows : Score Score Pts. Pld. Won. Lost. Drn For. Ag'st. Manningham 16 ... 13 ... 3 ... 0 —ll4 ... 48 ... 26 Leeds Liversedge l6 7 ... 7 ... 2 ... 98 ... 75 ... 16 Halifax Hunslet l3 ... 7 ... 6 ... 1 ... 73 ... 50 ... 15 Hull l4 ... 7 ... 6 ... 1 ... 83 ... 42 ... 15 B'ghouse Ran l5 ... 6 ... 6 ... 3 ... 61 ... 39 ... 15 Bradford l7 ... 5 ... 7 ... 5 ... 53 ... 73 ... 15 Wakefi'd Tri'tv 16 ... 5 ... 10 ... 1 ... 44 ...IN ... 11 Batley l7 ... 4 ... 10 ... 3 ... 37 ...10 ... 11 Huddersfield l4 ... 4 ... 9 ... 1 ... 35 ... 64 ... 9 --4110.----. FRODSB.A.M.</p>
        <p>PAROCIIIAL COMAIITTEI3.—The monthly meeting of the he Free Church cottage, but on the appli- Frodsham Parish Council, acting as a Parochial Com- mittee, was held in the Court-room, Frodsham, on Fri- day evening. when Mr. H. B. Heywood presided. A tender was produced, amounting to £2 10s., for laying itenti atayat reuc trauepmrea: l.ssAant improvements. Fro ocantiwonatoerf lALE clam this was postponed for the present, eh: owned wdb :woe been by Mrs. Holland and a cottage owned by Mr. T. Davies d applicationshani WBawrti Water rgs behalf of Mr. John Cotterill, of Frodsham Bridge, for the occupation of a newly-erected slaughter-house, where- upon it was resolved, on the recommendation of Mr. Ipoaepindpdo°isnlitgteo:T(c) Williams, seconded by Mr. Shore, that such application be granted for one year. An application was also made on behalf of Mr. Owen,Frodsham for a licence te open a milkshop in Church-street, Frodsham, when it was de- cided that this be left over until the next meeting, prier to which the matter would be brought up at the Dis- trict Council. A letter was read from Mr. Diggle with reference to a certain watercourse in the Marsh, when after some consideration it was resolved that a deputa- tion be appointed, consisting of Messrs. Booth, Youd, Illidge, and Earlaan, to confer with Mr. Charles E. Linaker, the surveyor of the Frodsham and Helsby Drainage Board, with respect thereto. Mr. J.M.Williams read a long report,on matters connected with the ?niter supply, which was considered satisfactory.—The minutes of this committee were approved of at the meeting of the Runcorn Rural Council on Tuesday afternoon. COUNTY COUNCIL LECTURRS.—An interestin , lecture was delivereclin the National Girls' School, Frodsham on Wednesday evening, by Dr. Jones,' lecturer of the Lancashire and Cheshire Bee-keeper,' Association on bee keeping. Mr. J. M. Williams presidel over a small atten• dance, ACTION AGAINST A TRADESDIAN.—OR Tuesday at Run- corn County Court, before his Honour Judge Wynne Floulkea, a claim was made by John Mcßurney, of Five Crosses, against John Henry Davies, for £1 is., wages in lieu of notice. It appeared that defendant engaged plaintiff at 28s. per week as baker, when times were good. Trade falling off his wages were reduced to 255., and later on plaintiff contended that he agreed to give him £1 is. a week, to do what was required, and permit him to go when he obtained another situation. A fort- night after this arrangement Mcßurney received an inti- mation that his services were no longer required, and plaintiff urged that he was entitled to a week's notice.— His Honour being of opinion that there was no agree- ment between the parties gave judgment fot the defen- dant.</p>
        <p>Adolph Beck, " the sham Earl," was convicted at the Old Bailey, on Thursday, and sentenced to seven years' penal servitude. He had perpetrated many frauds of the most audacious and heartless description on credul- ous women.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>THE INQUEST.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. E. Worsley held an inquiry on Saturday into the circumstances attending the death of Thomas Ast- bury. aged 56 years. of 2, Union-street, Runcorn, grocer and farmer, who was found hanging in an outbuilding, on Friday, and reported in our last issue.—Walter Ast- bury, son of the deceased, gave evidence of identifica- tion, and said his father bad been weak and depressed for four months. They bad lost a valuable mare and two foals. They had also lost calves, and generally speaking the farm was not doing well. He had been ail- ing for 12 months, but was worse consequent on the losses sustained. He had consulted Dr. Robinson, who hal attended him for about two months. He had never expressed the opinion that things were so bad that he could not live. He complained of bodily weakness and want of sleep.—Mary Ellen Bebbington, domestic ser- vant with the late Mr. Astbury for about four years,said her master s- ed melancholy, but had never to her knowledge threatened to destroy himself. On Friday morning, about ten o'clock she went into the sbippon for some eggs, when she saw deceased hanging from a beans. There was a step-ladder close by. She raised an alarm.—William Povey, Halton-road, spoke to hearing screams about ten o'clock on Friday morning, near the shippon. He went with Walter Astbury, and saw deceased hanging by a rope from the beam. He cut him down. He did not seem quite dead. They sent for medical assistance. He apparently breathed for some ten minutes. Dr. Robinson was speedily in attendance, and he pronounced life to be extinct.—Dr. Robinson said he had known Mr. Astbury for many years. De- ceased came to him about four months ago sufferiag from "trouble." He was much depressed. He had been an industrious, steady, respectable man, working early and late. Latterly he had suffered several losses, and expressed regret at having neglected to dispose of the farm at the proper time. He was physically as well as mentally depressed, the one resulting from the other. The members of the lamily were much attached to each other—it was a pattern family—and deceased had said to witness that his wile and children 'could get on bet- ter without him. His thoughts were apparently for them.—The Coroner,in summing ur, said there could be no doubt that depression and anxiety more for his family than himself so preyed upon his mind that he destroyed himself when in a temporary state of insanity.—The jury returned a verdict that the deceased committed suicide whilst temporarily insane.—The Coroner said he was sure he was expressing the feelings of the jury when he stated how sorry they were, and how they syrapathised with his wife and family.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Board was held on Tuesday after- noon, and was presided over by Mr. Knowles. There were also present in addition to the members of the Rural Council, Rev. J. S. Simpson, Messrs. S. Taylor, J.P., C.C.. A. R. Norman, T. Lea, S. Yarwood, with the clerk (Mr. J. Ashton),and deputy clerk (Mr. G. Ashtin), and Mr. Cunnah (Workhouse master). Mr. Dansey (Local Government Board Inspector), was also present. THE APPOINTMENT OF OVERSEERS. The Clerk presented an order from the Local Govern- ment Board transferring to the Urban District Council of Roncern,the appointment of overseers for each parish comprised in the Runcorn district. TRAMPS AND THEIR TREATMENT. An order from the Local Government Board was read by the Clerk, in which the Board urged the carrying out of their previous orders in respect to vagrants. The principal instruction was to keep a vagrant two days in the House' and find labour for him during that time.— Mr. Wright stated that they had not accommodation sufficient for this. To carry cut this order would entail considerable expense for extra tramp wards.—Mr. Parr thought they might look at it in another way ; they would not have as many visitors.—Mr. Baxter suggested that it would be cheaper to have the water of the House pumped by hand, and thus find employment for vagrants. They now pumped by steam.—lt was de- cided to refer the matter to the Parliamentary commit- tee.—The Guardians of Coventry Union had resolved to send a memorial to the Local Government Board re- questing that Board to urge upon H.M. Gouernment the necessity of legislation for the protection and education of the children ot tramps. They asked the Runcorn Union to consider the same.—The Chairman thought this should be done if possible.—Mr. Woodward thought the memorial did not go far enough. He proposed ,how- ever, that a similar memorial be forwarded.—This was agreed to. THE PROPOSED GRAVING DOCK AT WESTON POINT. _ _</p>
        <p>_ With reference to this matter the following letter was I read :—" Northwich, Feb. 28, 1896. Dear Sir,—Your letter of the 12th inst., on the subject of the proposed conversion of one of the docks at Weston Point into a graving dock was laid before the trustees of ihe Weaver yesterday, and it was resolved That the project could not be entertained by the trustees as it was not practic- able in the interests of the Navigation.' Yours truly, Trafford and Cook."—Mr. Aaron: It is a bad job.—The Chairman: No doubt.</p>
        <p>LUNATICS. The Sheffield Union forwarded a resolution they bad passed in faveur of all cases of mental infirmity being under management of County Authorities as the larger number to be dealt with would facilitate recovery by special classification and treatment. As some time must elapse before this could be carried out the Sheffield Board strongly recommended that in the meantime the 4s.grant now allowed for lunatics in asylurnsghould be extended to those maintained in Workhouse wards. The Runcorn Beard was asked to forward a similar resolution to the Parliamentary Committee. TIM DINING ROOM.</p>
        <p>Mr. Norman in moving the adoption of the report of the Dinina Room Committee,stated that Mr. Davies had promised to prepare plans and estimates for the enlarge- ment of the dining room free of charge,and the commit- tee recommended that this should be done, and further recommended that in the meantime intending diners give four days notice and that seats be reserved for them.—Mr. Aaron and others objected to the proposal of giving the notice mentioned,urging that frequently busi- ness men did not know whether they could attend the meeting or not the evening before.—The report was adopted.</p>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF A NURSE. On the motion of Mr. Brandreth, it was unanimously decided that Harriet Hanley, of Dales Cottage, White- field, near Manchester, be appointed nurse to the House, at a salary of £25 per annum. THE NEW RATE. Mr. Heywood submitted the estimate of the expendi- ture of the Union for the ensuing half-year ending Michaelmas, 1896. It showed the common charges to be £4.800, county rate £3,681, balance and contingency fund £2,000, total £10.481. There would probably be a balance of £2,886 at Lady Day, and deducting this sum, left the amount required isy contribution orders for the overseers at £7,595. The rateable value of the Union ad to valuation lists in force, is £240,095, being a decrease of £2,286 as compared with the corre,ponding period of last year. A rate of 7icl. in the £ would produce the sum of £7,500, and it was recommended that a call for the sum of £7,595 be made upon the overseers in accord- ance with the detailed estimate, being the same as the corresponding period -)f last year. The details of com- mon r•harges included in maintenance £6OO, out-relief £1,900, lunatics £550, salaries £650. rations £BO, vac- cination £lOO, and registration £75. He moved the adoption of the estimates.—lVlr.Ww•dward seconded,and contended that a number of townships in the Union were working their rates out at a lower valuation than was in force in other townships. —rhe Clerk stated that he had obtained the information required on the matter, but no committee had been called together to deal with it.— The estimate was approved, and it was decided to call a meeting of the committee to deal with the valuations.</p>
        <p>UNPAID CALLS. Mr. Aaron wished to know what townships had not paid their calls.—Mr. Heywood stated that they were as follows :—Acton Grange, Alvanley, Antrobus, Aston, Bartingtan,Budworth, Dutton, Halton ,Hatton ,Kingsley, Kingswood, Newton-by-Frodsham, Preston-o'th-Hill,and Whitby Superior. The calls were due on February 16th, This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Board was held on Wed- nesday at the Workhouse. Mr. William Houghton pre- sided, and there were also present, Mrs. Clay,Rev. T. R. Matthews; Messrs. S. Davies A. J. Jolley, G. Thompson, W. Cross, T. B. Malkin, A:Lang, W. Hickman, E. L. Bent, J. Ball, T. Halton, J. Warburton, J. Clarke, A. Jolley, J. Billinge, Dr. Patterson, J. Johnson ; S. Sutton (deputy clerk), C. H. Ward (master), H. M. Mines (sup- erintendent). THE DEATH OF THE REV. G. F. ROBERTS. Previous to the business of the Board being commenced the CHAIRMAN referring to the death of the Rev. G. F. Roberts, M.A.., of Hollinfare, said he was sorry there were not present more of his former colleagues on the Board, as they would have appreciated more than he could except the new members to do, the character of the deceased clergyman. He did not exactly know how long Mr. Roberts had been a member of the Boari (the Assistant Clerk : "16 or 17 Years ")—but during the time he (the Chairman) had been connected with the Board he had always found him to be a quiet and unabstrusive member, working steadily in every department of poor law wor,k until he was elected Chairman of the Board, and during the time he occu- pied that post he could never forget the quiet influence and the dignified manner which he displayed. Be was, of course, a man of education, a noble-minded man,and his services to the Board were almost invaluable. The Clerk, who was away in London on business connected with the Board, had been thoughtful enough to prepare a regolution, which he would shortly read to the Board. Mr.Sutton,in hie letter to him (the Chairnaan), expressed his own deep personal regret at the loss which the Board had sustained in Mr.Roberts' death. He had the melancholy satisfaction of moving the following resolu- tion :—" That the Guardians and Rural District Coun- cillors of the Warrington Union desire to express deep regret at the decease of the Rev. G. F. Roberts, M.A.., and to place upon record their high appreciation of the valuable public services he for many consecutive years rendered in the administration within this union of the poor and sanitary laws, included with which services were the duties of Chairman of the Board, Chairman of the Assessment Comnsittee, and Chairman of the Rural Sanitary Authority and District Council. They desire further to place upon record that in the discharge of the duties devolving upon him Mr Roberts invariably mani- fested tact and kindnesss of disposition. They desire also to express deep sympathy with Mrs. Roberts in her sad bereavement." Mr. DAVIES seconded the resolution, and also spoke in highly complimentary terms of the late Mr. Roberts, whose administrative abilities, he said, could hardly be sureassed. Mr. THOMPSON assured the Chairman that the younger members of the Board and the District Council appreci- ated Mr. Roberts's services to the district quite as much as any of the older members of the Board. During the short time he (Mr. Thompson) had been connected with the Board he had seen that Mr. Roberts had won the res- pect and the esteem) ef all who were associated with him. The resolution was then put to the meeting and car- ried unanimously. THE RECOGNISED RATE OF WAGES. The House Committee reported that the Clerk sub- mitted to the committee the form of advertising tenders for the supply of goods and provisions for the half year ending at Michaelmas next. The form of advertisement was approved, and it was resolved that the advertise- ment appear in the usual local papers' and that the ten- ders be opened ani examined at the Board meeting be treated as a special day for such examination of accept- ance or otherwise. It was moved that the advertise- ment contain the following words: "Contractors for the supply of goods manufactured in the district must be the employers who pay their workpeople the recognised rate of wages." An amendment was carried that no such addition be made —Mr. CLARKE moved the adoption of the minutes,and Mr. A. J. JOLLEY seconded.—Mr. Lona: Does that entirely settle the question of the fair wage clause in the contracts for the ensuing half-year.—The Chairman : Quite so. A discussion arose in committee and if the decision is carried by this Board it will be confirmed.—Mr. LONG said that he would like the clause to be inserted into contracts, and the only feasible plan seemed to be to have it put into the advertisement . The Chairman The matter was referred to the House Committee to be dealt with. Do you move any amend- ment.—Mr. LONG: I do not.—The minutes were carried. THE MINUTES OF THE LADIES COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Mr. CLARKE asked that the minutes of the Ladies Committee he read. hut Mr. Davies said that although he had asked Mr. Mines to bring the book, he had not done so.—Mr. HICKMAN : Did you request Mr. Mines to bring the books.—Mr. DAVIES : I did.—Mr. MINES : I think you forget sir. You have not asked me.—Mr. flicKmax : I cannot understand such work as that. —lt was agreed on the motion of Mr. TEtomPsow,seconded by Mr. CLARICE, that in future, the book containing the minutes of ao Ladies Committee be laid on the tatle at I each meeting of the Board. THE NEXT MEETING. The DEPUTY CLERK announced that a special meeting would be held on March 18 for the purpose of consider- ing the tenders for the subsequent half-rar.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-07_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>HA.L-TON.</head>
        <p>HA.L-TON.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S HOME AND PRINCESS ALICE ORPHANAGE. - With the object of raising funds far this most worthy in- stitution, two excellent concerts were given in the Wesleyan Chapel, Halton, on Friday evening by a choir of boys and girls from the orphanage mentioned. The afternoon concert was not so well attended as was that in the evening. The chairman in the afternoon was the Rev. C. De Putron Gliddon, the resident Wesleyan minis- ter, and at night, Mr. R. H. Posnett occupied that posi- tion. The programme submitted at each concert was of an exceptionally interesting character, the children acquitting themselves with credit, alike to themselves and their instructors. Selections of sacred, classical,and popular music were rendered, and there were also per- formances on the carillons, sleigh bells, and dulcimer. During an interval, the governor of the institute (Mr. Thomas Durley) gave an interesting account of the work b?ing carried on in the home and orphanage, and showed that useful and beneficent Christian service was being done. Sister Freda was the pianist.</p>
